CET6.passages("freq.cet4",{"1":{"w":["human","research","social","researcher","might","less","increase","experience","part","product","author","level","likely","goal","state","university","public","focus","benefit","brain"],"title":"Why Friendship Is Good for the Brain","t":"For decades, scientists have studied what keeps the **human** **brain** healthy. Much of this **research** has focused on diet and exercise, but a growing body of evidence suggests that **social** connections **might** be just as important.\n\nA team at a leading **university** recently followed two thousand adults over twenty years. The lead **researcher**, a psychologist and the **author** of several popular books, found that people with close friendships were **less** **likely** to suffer memory loss in old age. Those who felt lonely, by contrast, performed at a lower **level** on memory tests.\n\nWhy should friendship matter? One explanation is that conversation and shared **experience** challenge the brain, forcing it to stay active. Another is that friends reduce stress, which can damage brain cells. Whatever the reason, the **benefit** appears to be real.\n\nThe findings have implications for **public** policy. In many countries, the number of people living alone continues to **increase**. Some governments now **state** openly that loneliness is a health problem, and they have set a **goal** of reducing it. Community centres, volunteer programmes and even a new **product** — apps that connect older people with neighbours — are **part** of the response.\n\nThe researchers hope their work will shift the **focus** of health advice. \"We tell people to eat well and exercise,\" the author says. \"We should also tell them to call a friend.\""},"2":{"w":["self","create","employee","result","technology","behavior","government","fact","media","cause","tend","lead","ability","impact","include","provide","education","effect","sense","actually"],"title":"Technology at Work","t":"Modern **technology** has changed the way people work, but its **impact** on **employees|employee** is not always positive. A recent study by a **government** research centre found that office workers check their emails more than seventy times a day. As a **result**, many feel constantly distracted and tired.\n\nThe study's authors argue that technology **tends|tend** to **create** new demands. Smartphones and laptops **provide** flexibility, but they also blur the line between work and home. In **fact**, many employees reported answering messages late at night, which can **lead** to poor sleep and stress.\n\nSocial **media** is another **cause** of distraction. Workers who spend time online during the day often feel less satisfied with their jobs. The **effect** is especially strong among young people, whose **sense** of **self** is closely linked to their online image.\n\nYet technology is not the enemy. Used wisely, it can improve our **ability** to learn and communicate. The researchers recommend that companies **include** digital skills in workplace **education** and encourage healthy **behavior**, such as turning off notifications after work. \"People think they are in control of their devices,\" one expert said. \"But **actually**, the devices often control them.\""},"3":{"w":["area","economic","community","however","improve","value","process","recent","skill","memory","base","scientist","share","team","happen","rather","art","society","personal","learning"],"title":"Learning in the Community","t":"**However** advanced our schools become, much important **learning** still **happens|happen** outside the classroom. A **recent** study by social **scientists|scientist** found that young people who take part in **community** activities develop **skills|skill** that school alone cannot teach.\n\nThe study was **based|base** on interviews with three thousand teenagers across the country. Those who joined local sports **teams|team**, volunteered or took part in **art** projects reported greater confidence and better relationships. Many said they had learned to **share** responsibilities and solve problems together.\n\nResearchers believe that this **process** works because community activities connect learning with real life. When students help clean a river or organise a festival, they see the **value** of their work immediately. The experience also stays in their **memory** far longer than a textbook lesson.\n\nThere is an **economic** argument too. Employers increasingly look for teamwork and communication, not just exam results. Communities that invest in youth programmes may therefore **improve** local job prospects. Some **areas|area** have already begun to fund such programmes through local businesses.\n\nOf course, not all students have equal access to these opportunities. Children from poorer families often have **rather** fewer choices. Experts argue that **society** must make community learning available to everyone, because its **personal** and social benefits are too important to ignore."},"4":{"w":["culture","business","consider","decision","allow","attention","space","lose","resource","system","power","finding","avoid","local","relationship","means","individual","interest","belief","risk"],"title":"The Risks of Living Online","t":"In just two decades, the internet has transformed our **culture**, our **business** and our **relationships|relationship**. Most of us now spend several hours a day online, and many young people **consider** their phones essential. But researchers warn that this new way of living carries hidden **risks|risk**.\n\nOne recent **finding** is that constant online activity weakens our **attention**. When we switch quickly between apps, the brain has little time to think deeply. Another concern is privacy: every click provides data that companies use to influence our **decisions|decision**. Few people fully understand how much **power** this gives large technology firms.\n\nThe problem is not technology itself but the **system** that surrounds it. Many platforms are designed to keep users online for as long as possible. Their business model depends on our time and **interest**. In this way, our attention has become a valuable **resource**, much like oil or water.\n\nWhat can an **individual** do? Experts suggest simple steps: **avoid** using phones before bed, turn off notifications and create phone-free **space** at home. **Local** schools and libraries can also teach digital skills. These are not complicated **means**, but they help us keep control.\n\nMany people hold the **belief** that they can multitask without any cost. Research suggests otherwise. If we **allow** technology to shape every moment, we may **lose** something important: the ability to be fully present."},"5":{"w":["tax","global","test","factor","program","environment","control","growth","rise","view","data","effort","history","point","term","common","climate","consumer","speaker","positive"],"title":"Climate and the Consumer","t":"Climate change is one of the most serious problems of our time. Scientists warn that global temperatures continue to **rise**, and the **data** show clearly that human activity is a major **factor**. Yet many people still feel that the problem is too large for them to **control**.\n\nEconomists take a different **view**. They argue that **consumer** choices can drive real change. When enough people buy energy-efficient products, companies have an incentive to invest in cleaner technology. Some governments support this **effort** by introducing a carbon **tax**, which makes polluting products more expensive.\n\nHistory offers useful lessons. In the past, public pressure helped ban harmful chemicals and reduce air pollution in many cities. The **history** of these campaigns suggests that change is possible when citizens, businesses and governments work together.\n\nNot everyone agrees on the best approach. One speaker at a recent conference argued that **growth** and environmental protection are compatible. Another **speaker** disagreed, saying that rich countries must consume less. The key **point**, both agreed, is that the **global** **climate** affects everyone.\n\nMany schools now include environmental education in their **program**, and some **test** students on their knowledge of sustainable living. In the long **term**, experts believe, these lessons will help create a more **positive** relationship between people and the natural **environment**. It is now **common** to see children reminding their parents to recycle."},"6":{"w":["industry","rate","choice","screen","design","offer","opportunity","living","academic","natural","population","quality","claim","challenge","issue","require","project","expert","support","crop"],"title":"Feeding a Growing World","t":"By 2050, the world's **population** is expected to reach almost ten billion. Feeding so many people will be one of the greatest **challenges|challenge** of the century. Farmers must grow more food on less land, while protecting the **natural** environment.\n\nMany **experts|expert** **claim** that technology **offers|offer** the best **opportunity** to solve this **issue**. Scientists have developed new **crop** varieties that grow faster and resist disease. Some farms now use drones and computer **screens|screen** to monitor soil and water, and an **academic** study found that such methods can increase harvests at a remarkable **rate**.\n\nBut technology alone is not enough. Large-scale farming often **requires|require** heavy use of chemicals, which can damage soil **quality** and water supplies. The food **industry** also wastes huge amounts of food every year. Critics argue that we need to **design** smarter systems, not just bigger ones.\n\nConsumers have a role too. Every food **choice** we make sends a message to producers. Choosing local, seasonal produce can **support** small farmers and reduce transport emissions. In some cities, community **projects|project** teach residents to grow vegetables on rooftops.\n\nThe future of food is uncertain, but one thing is clear: making a **living** from farming must become fairer and more sustainable. As one expert put it, \"Feeding the world is not only a technical problem. It is a moral one.\""},"7":{"w":["real","promote","towards","achieve","average","difference","income","habit","energy","reach","understanding","condition","activity","affect","century","continue","negative","success","kid","advice"],"title":"The Power of Small Habits","t":"Why do some people **achieve** their goals while others give up? According to psychologists, the **difference** often lies in small daily **habits|habit**. A **real** change in life, they argue, rarely comes from one big decision; it comes from many small actions repeated over time.\n\nConsider exercise. Research shows that the **average** person who sets a vague goal, such as \"get fit,\" is unlikely to **reach** it. But people who commit to a specific **activity** — a twenty-minute walk after dinner, for example — are much more likely to **continue**. Over months, their **energy** levels rise and their health improves.\n\nHabits also **affect** our finances. People who save a small part of their **income** every month build wealth more steadily than those who wait for a large bonus. Financial advisers often give the same **advice**: start small and be consistent.\n\nOf course, bad habits can have a **negative** effect too. Checking social media late at night, for instance, reduces sleep and makes it harder to focus the next day. A better **understanding** of how habits form can help people **promote** healthy routines and break harmful ones.\n\nEven a **kid** can learn this lesson. Parents who encourage children to read for ten minutes each day help them develop a positive attitude **towards** learning. As scientists of the past **century** discovered, **success** depends less on talent than on the right **conditions|condition** for growth."},"8":{"w":["concern","course","later","appear","reduce","feeling","situation","career","role","follow","stress","force","task","prevent","seek","adult","emotion","measure","model","publish"],"title":"Stress at Work","t":"In many countries, workplace **stress** has become a major health **concern**. A recent survey found that more than half of **adult** workers feel anxious about their jobs at least once a week. The **situation** is especially serious among young employees at the start of their **careers|career**.\n\nResearchers have tried to **measure** the causes. Long hours, unclear **tasks|task** and pressure from managers are common factors. Many workers also say they feel **forced|force** to stay online after work, answering emails late at night. Over time, this can **reduce** both productivity and well-being.\n\nStress is not only a physical problem but also an emotional one. Workers who feel unappreciated often describe a **feeling** of helplessness, and some begin to avoid colleagues. Psychologists say that ignoring these signs can **prevent** people from seeking help until it is too late. Recognising each **emotion** is the first step towards managing it.\n\nSome companies have begun to take action. They offer training **courses|course** on stress management and encourage employees to **seek** support. One technology firm has developed a new **model** of flexible working that lets staff choose their own hours. Its managers **follow** a simple rule: no emails after seven o'clock.\n\nThe results **appear** promising. A study **published|publish** **later** that year found that employees at such companies took fewer sick days. Managers play a key **role** in this change, experts say, because workers often copy the behaviour of their leaders."},"9":{"w":["access","event","species","argue","decade","encourage","economy","communication","nature","conflict","traffic","alone","influence","performance","degree","digital","increasingly","produce","add","gain"],"title":"Life in the Digital Age","t":"Over the past **decade**, **digital** technology has transformed nearly every part of daily life. People are **increasingly** connected through phones and social networks, and **access** to information has never been easier. Yet experts **argue** that these changes bring both opportunities and risks.\n\nOn the positive side, digital tools have made **communication** faster and cheaper. Families separated by distance can talk face to face, and small businesses can reach customers around the world. Some economists believe that the digital **economy** will continue to **gain** importance and **produce** millions of new jobs.\n\nAt the same time, technology can have a strong **influence** on our behaviour. Many people feel anxious when they are **alone** without their phones. Studies show that constant online activity can harm sleep and reduce **performance** at work or school. In some cases, online disagreements even lead to real **conflict** between friends.\n\nTechnology also affects the natural world. Scientists use digital sensors to track endangered **species** and monitor air **traffic** over protected areas. Such data **encourage** better decisions about how to protect **nature**.\n\nThe key, experts say, is balance. Technology is a tool, and the **degree** to which it helps or harms depends on how we use it. A major technology **event** in Beijing last year focused on this question. One speaker **added|add** that the best innovations are those that make us more human, not less."},"10":{"w":["against","exist","material","percent","act","especially","within","compare","explain","due","effective","evidence","further","financial","professor","region","training","mental","physical","policy"],"title":"Mind and Body","t":"For centuries, doctors treated the mind and the body as separate. **Physical** health was the concern of medicine, while **mental** health was often ignored. Today, a growing body of **evidence** suggests that the two are closely linked.\n\nA **professor** of psychology at a leading university recently led a study comparing people who exercise regularly with those who rarely do. The results showed that regular exercisers were thirty **percent** less likely to suffer from depression. \"Exercise is one of the most **effective** treatments for mild anxiety,\" she explained.\n\nResearchers are now trying to **explain** why. One theory is that exercise reduces stress hormones and improves sleep. Another is that physical activity helps people feel more in control of their lives. Whatever the reason, the benefits seem to **exist** across different ages and cultures.\n\nThese findings have important implications for public **policy**. In some **regions|region**, doctors now prescribe exercise **training** programmes instead of medicine. Governments are also considering **financial** support for community sports centres, **especially** in poorer areas. Such programmes must be designed carefully, **due** to differences in people's health and fitness.\n\n**Further** research is needed, of course. Scientists want to **compare** different types of exercise and study the **material** conditions that make healthy living possible. But the message is already clear: we must **act** now, **within** our own daily routines. The fight **against** mental illness may begin with a simple walk."},"11":{"w":["scientific","journal","passion","patient","identify","certain","survey","gas","background","beyond","importance","organization","pattern","respect","artist","involve","account","image","topic","contribute"],"title":"The Science of Passion","t":"Why do some people pursue a hobby with lifelong **passion**, while others lose interest quickly? A recent **survey** published in a **scientific** **journal** tried to answer this question. Researchers interviewed thousands of people, from office workers to a famous **artist**, about the activities they love most.\n\nThe study **identified|identify** a clear **pattern**. People who stayed passionate about an activity usually felt that it **involved|involve** growth and challenge. They also believed that their efforts **contributed|contribute** to something larger than themselves. A painter, for example, might see her work as part of a cultural tradition, not just a personal **image** of beauty.\n\n**Background** also mattered. Many participants said that a parent or teacher had shown **respect** for their interests when they were young. This support gave them confidence to continue **beyond** childhood. The researchers stress the **importance** of encouragement, especially in the early stages of learning.\n\nInterestingly, passion is not always positive. In **certain** cases, people became so focused on one activity that they ignored health or relationships. One **patient** described working on his hobby until he was exhausted. Experts say a healthy balance is essential.\n\nThe findings have practical uses. An **organization** that supports young people now uses the research to design its programmes. When choosing a **topic** for a new class, it takes **account** of students' interests. As one researcher put it, \"Passion is like **gas** in a car: it keeps you moving, but you must still steer.\""},"12":{"w":["despite","achievement","choose","professional","tiny","tool","fountain","action","consequence","demand","fear","reflect","shift","top","anxiety","nation","position","engage","least","realize"],"title":"Living with Anxiety","t":"**Anxiety** has become one of the most common health problems among young people. Surveys across the **nation** suggest that students feel more pressure than ever before. They **fear** failure, worry about their future **position** in society and feel constant **demand** to succeed.\n\nPsychologists say this trend **reflects|reflect** a broader **shift** in modern life. Social media encourages people to compare themselves with others who seem to be at the **top** of their field. Even a **tiny** mistake can feel like a disaster. The **consequence** is a culture of stress, where many students rarely feel satisfied with their **achievement**.\n\n**Despite** these challenges, there are effective ways to cope. **Professional** counsellors recommend practical **tools|tool**, such as breathing exercises, regular sleep and time away from screens. Taking **action** early is important: those who seek help at the first signs of anxiety recover more quickly.\n\nSchools also have a role. Some universities now **engage** students in group discussions about mental health, helping them **realize** that they are not alone. Others have created quiet spaces on campus, such as gardens with a **fountain**, where students can rest.\n\nExperts advise that we should **choose** our goals carefully. Instead of trying to be perfect, we should focus on progress. \"At **least** once a day,\" one counsellor suggests, \"do something just because you enjoy it.\""},"13":{"w":["connect","various","color","creative","explore","variety","although","prove","reading","connection","deal","determine","knowledge","lack","manage","internet","wage","introduce","solution","shape"],"title":"The Creative Mind","t":"What makes a person **creative**? For a long time, people believed creativity was a gift that only artists possessed. Today, research suggests that it is a skill anyone can develop. **Although** some people are naturally more imaginative, most of us can learn to think in new ways.\n\nStudies **prove** that creativity depends on **knowledge** and experience. The more we **explore** **various** subjects, the more ideas we can **connect**. A designer who reads about biology, for example, might find a new **solution** to an engineering problem. Scientists call this the **connection** of distant ideas.\n\nDaily habits also **shape** creativity. **Reading** widely, travelling and meeting different people expose us to a **variety** of perspectives. Even the **color** of a room can affect our thinking: one study found that blue walls encourage imagination.\n\nHowever, many people **lack** the time or freedom to be creative. Workers who earn a low **wage** may need several jobs to survive, leaving little energy for new ideas. Companies that want creative employees must learn to **manage** workloads fairly.\n\nThe **internet** has made it easier to share ideas, and many schools now **introduce** creative projects into their lessons. Teachers say that such projects help students **deal** with complex problems. In the end, our curiosity, not our talent, may **determine** how creative we become."},"14":{"w":["strategy","whole","crisis","personality","collect","perform","potential","intelligence","maintain","progress","reality","daily","death","cell","happiness","medical","private","recently","resident","argument"],"title":"The Science of Happiness","t":"What is the secret of **happiness**? Scientists have studied this question for decades, and their findings often challenge common beliefs. Money, for example, matters less than most people imagine. Once basic needs are met, **daily** relationships and a sense of purpose have a far greater effect.\n\nOne long-term study followed hundreds of people from youth until **death**. Researchers found that those with strong friendships lived longer and healthier lives. Their **medical** records showed fewer heart problems, and even their brain **cells|cell** appeared to age more slowly.\n\n**Personality** plays a role too. People who are optimistic tend to recover faster from a **crisis**, such as losing a job. But happiness is not fixed. Psychologists have developed practical **strategies|strategy** to **maintain** a positive mood: writing a gratitude journal, helping others and spending time in nature.\n\nSome experts warn against confusing happiness with constant pleasure. \"Real happiness includes sadness and challenge,\" one researcher argues. Her **argument** is based on interviews with people who had faced difficult **reality** but still described their lives as meaningful.\n\nGovernments have **recently** begun to **collect** data on happiness as well as income. In some cities, every **resident** is asked to rate their well-being once a year. Officials hope to measure **progress** not only in economic terms. A **private** company in Denmark even uses artificial **intelligence** to analyse what makes workers **perform** best. The **whole** field, experts say, has great **potential** to improve lives."},"15":{"w":["decline","earn","successful","type","apply","enable","phone","beauty","island","rural","absence","robot","imagine","recognize","accept","audience","interaction","rely","current","responsibility"],"title":"Robots and Rural Life","t":"In many **rural** areas, young people are leaving for the cities, and farmers are growing older. The **absence** of workers has caused farm production to **decline** in some regions. Now, a surprising helper has appeared: the **robot**.\n\nOn a small **island** in southern Japan, farmers have begun to **rely** on machines to plant rice and pick fruit. These robots **enable** elderly farmers to continue working and to **earn** a living. One farmer said he could hardly **imagine** life without them.\n\nThe technology is not perfect. Some robots cannot **recognize** ripe fruit, and others break down in bad weather. The **current** models are also expensive. Yet engineers believe that costs will fall as technology improves. A **successful** project in one village has already attracted interest from farmers across the region.\n\nNot everyone is ready to **accept** this change. Some worry that robots will reduce human **interaction** and destroy traditional farming culture. Others argue that it is society's **responsibility** to support farmers in new ways, rather than leaving them alone.\n\nThe village has even become a tourist attraction. Visitors film the robots on their **phones|phone**, and a local television programme about them attracted a large **audience**. Many people say they want to **apply** similar ideas in their own communities. Whatever **type** of future we choose, the **beauty** of rural life is worth protecting."},"16":{"w":["amount","desire","disease","code","teaching","elderly","stranger","depend","source","major","colleague","gap","safe","step","supply","unite","writing","law","taste","attract"],"title":"Caring for an Ageing Society","t":"As populations grow older, caring for **elderly** people has become a **major** challenge in many countries. A large **amount** of public money is spent on hospitals and care homes, yet there is still a **gap** between what older people need and what is available.\n\nOne problem is the **supply** of carers. Nursing is hard work, and many young people choose other careers. In some places, families **depend** on paid helpers who are almost **strangers|stranger** to them. Experts warn that loneliness can be as dangerous to health as **disease**.\n\nSome communities have found creative solutions. In Japan, schools and care homes share buildings, so children and older people meet every day. In the Netherlands, university students live in care homes for free, in exchange for spending time with residents. Such projects **unite** different generations and **attract** wide public interest.\n\nTechnology is another **source** of help. Simple devices can remind people to take medicine, and a door **code** can keep residents **safe** without locking them in. But researchers stress that machines cannot replace human warmth.\n\nGovernments are taking **steps|step** too. A new **law** in several countries gives workers paid leave to care for parents. Universities have improved the **teaching** of care skills, and many nurses now study creative **writing** and music to make their work more personal. One **colleague** of mine, a nurse, says older people share the same **desire** as everyone else: to be respected. \"They don't want pity,\" she says. \"They want to **taste** life fully until the end.\""},"17":{"w":["associate","ensure","struggle","link","majority","national","popular","pursue","throughout","rule","thought","significant","consumption","probably","prepare","protect","traditional","management","forest","automation"],"title":"Protecting the Forest","t":"**Throughout** history, **forests|forest** have provided food, shelter and medicine. Yet in the past century, the **majority** of the world's ancient forests have been cut down. Scientists warn that this loss has **significant** effects on climate and wildlife.\n\nThe main cause is human **consumption**. Rising demand for wood, paper and farmland has pushed companies to clear huge areas. In many countries, **national** governments have struggled to enforce the **rule** of law in remote regions. Some forest communities **struggle** to protect their land against powerful businesses.\n\n**Traditional** knowledge can help. Indigenous people often **associate** forests with their history and identity, and they have managed them carefully for generations. Researchers have found a strong **link** between local ownership and healthy forests. When communities control the land, they usually **protect** it.\n\nTechnology is also changing forest **management**. **Automation** allows satellites and drones to watch forests around the clock, sending alerts when illegal logging begins. Such tools help to **ensure** that rules are followed.\n\nIndividuals can also make a difference. Choosing recycled paper is a **popular** way to reduce demand. Environmental groups encourage citizens to **pursue** greener lifestyles and **prepare** for a future with fewer resources. As one activist put it, \"We **probably** won't save every tree, but every **thought** about what we buy matters.\""},"18":{"w":["regard","win","approach","chance","general","stick","complex","conduct","damage","feature","mostly","emotional","purchase","relevant","graduate","leader","greenhouse","interview","trade","complete"],"title":"What Makes a Good Leader?","t":"What qualities make a successful **leader**? Business schools have studied this question for decades. Many researchers **conduct** interviews with company managers, while others **regard** history as the best teacher. The results suggest that leadership is more **complex** than people once believed.\n\nIn **general**, good leaders share several **features|feature**. They communicate clearly, listen carefully and stay calm under pressure. They also show **emotional** intelligence: the ability to understand their own feelings and those of others. Employees say that leaders who care about them are **mostly** more effective than those who focus only on profit.\n\nA modern **approach** to leadership emphasises teamwork. Instead of giving orders, leaders encourage staff to share ideas and **complete** projects together. When problems occur, they take responsibility rather than blaming others. Poor leadership can **damage** a company's reputation and drive talented workers away.\n\nYoung graduates often ask how they can become leaders. Experts advise them to **stick** to their values and look for every **chance** to learn. A recent **graduate** who now runs a small company told an **interview** panel that his first job, in a **greenhouse** growing vegetables, taught him patience. \"You can't **win** if you don't understand the people around you,\" he said.\n\nLeadership skills are **relevant** in every field, from global **trade** to small family businesses. Even the decision to **purchase** a new machine involves judgement, communication and trust."},"19":{"w":["international","pressure","specific","basic","serious","message","experiment","principle","temperature","brand","cathedral","boost","describe","generally","invest","particularly","perhaps","content","main","solve"],"title":"Testing a Theory","t":"Scientific progress depends on a simple **principle**: every theory must be tested. When scientists **describe** the natural world, they must be able to prove their ideas through careful **experiment**. This **basic** rule has guided research for centuries.\n\nConsider a recent study on climate. Researchers wanted to know how rising **temperature** affects old stone buildings. They studied a famous **cathedral** in northern Europe, measuring the **pressure** of heat and moisture on its walls. Their findings were **serious**: warmer weather was damaging the stone faster than expected.\n\nThe **main** lesson of the study was that ancient buildings need **specific** protection. Experts **generally** agree that monitoring and early repairs are the best way to **solve** the problem. Some governments now **invest** in new technology to protect historic sites.\n\nThe study also attracted **international** attention. Its **message** was shared widely on social media, and some companies used the topic to **boost** their **brand** image. Critics warned that such marketing can oversimplify scientific **content**.\n\n**Perhaps** the most important point is that science is a process, not a set of final answers. New evidence can change our understanding, **particularly** when technology allows more accurate measurement. The scientists who studied the cathedral plan to continue their work for another ten years."},"20":{"w":["easily","appeal","concept","identity","chemical","female","reward","institution","coin","address","central","available","discover","unique","exactly","response","harm","observe","overall","communicate"],"title":"The Meaning of Money","t":"Money is a **central** part of modern life, but it is also a powerful idea. Historians explain that the first **coins|coin** appeared around 2,600 years ago, and merchants tested their **chemical** purity by weight. Since then, the **concept** of money has changed **exactly** as quickly as society itself.\n\nToday, money shapes our sense of **identity**. Some people judge others by their income, and advertisements constantly **appeal** to our desire for wealth. Researchers **observe** that people who focus only on money often feel less satisfied with their lives.\n\nPsychologists have tried to **discover** why. One theory is that money works as a **reward**, giving short-term pleasure. But when people compare themselves with others, this pleasure quickly fades. The **overall** effect can even **harm** mental health.\n\nFinancial **institutions|institution** also influence how we think about money. Banks make credit **easily** **available**, encouraging people to spend more than they earn. In **response**, some schools now teach financial skills so that students can **communicate** about money and make wise choices.\n\nThere are also differences between groups. Studies show that **female** workers still earn less than men in many industries, and campaigns aim to **address** this unfairness. Each country has a **unique** history with money, but the lesson is universal: wealth is useful, but it cannot buy everything."},"21":{"w":["property","loss","trend","candidate","employer","aim","thus","essential","ignore","novel","particular","treat","balance","completely","function","limited","being","diet","special","standard"],"title":"Getting the First Job","t":"For many young people, finding a first job is one of the most stressful experiences of their lives. Every year, millions of **candidates|candidate** compete for a **limited** number of positions. **Employers|employer**, meanwhile, complain that graduates lack practical skills.\n\nRecruitment experts say that the **trend** is changing. Many companies now **aim** to hire people with strong communication and teamwork, not just high grades. A degree is still **essential**, but it is no longer enough. Candidates who **ignore** this shift may find themselves at a disadvantage.\n\nWhat do employers look for in **particular**? Surveys suggest that flexibility, honesty and the ability to learn are high on the list. Employers also **treat** volunteer work and internships as evidence of maturity. A student who has organised a charity event or written a **novel**, for example, shows creativity and determination.\n\nExperts also warn against overworking. Young employees often try to impress their managers by working long hours, but a healthy work-life **balance** is important. Poor **diet**, lack of sleep and stress can lead to a **loss** of energy and health. \"You are a human **being**, not a machine,\" one career adviser reminds students.\n\nFinally, preparation makes a **special** difference. Candidates should research the company, understand its **function** in the market and practise answering questions. Some even study the company's **property** and investments. Such efforts meet a high **standard** and may **completely** change an interview's result. **Thus**, the job search becomes not only a test but a learning experience."},"22":{"w":["production","farmer","spider","aware","occur","reveal","advantage","aspect","period","prefer","customer","investment","option","remove","scale","trust","dream","lower","style","user"],"title":"Farming in the Digital Age","t":"For thousands of years, **farmers|farmer** relied on experience and weather to decide when to plant and harvest. Today, digital technology is changing agricultural **production** in remarkable ways. Sensors, drones and apps allow farmers to monitor crops on a large **scale**.\n\nThe **advantage** of these tools is clear. Data can **reveal** problems before they **occur**, such as a lack of water or the presence of insects. Farmers can then **remove** harmful pests without spraying the whole field. This approach can **lower** costs and reduce pollution.\n\nNot every aspect of the change is positive, however. Some farmers are not **aware** of the risks of sharing data with large technology companies. Others find the new systems difficult to use. Experts say that companies must design products with the **user** in mind and earn farmers' **trust**.\n\nConsumers also play a role. Many **customers|customer** now **prefer** food produced in an environmentally friendly **style**. Supermarkets offer more organic **options|option**, and some farms invite visitors to see how food is grown. Every **aspect** of the supply chain is becoming more transparent.\n\nThe **period** ahead will require significant **investment** in training and equipment. Young farmers, in particular, **dream** of combining tradition with technology. One young woman in Yunnan uses a drone to protect her tea fields from a harmful **spider** mite. \"My grandparents taught me patience,\" she says. \"Technology gives me speed.\""},"23":{"w":["competition","destination","vacation","recommend","respond","save","track","worse","demonstrate","enhance","owner","senior","burn","motivate","staff","flood","anyone","debate","establish","generation"],"title":"Choosing a Holiday Destination","t":"Choosing a **vacation** **destination** has become more complicated than ever. Travellers can now **track** prices online, read thousands of reviews and compare options in seconds. Yet many people say that too much choice makes decisions **worse**, not better.\n\nTravel experts **recommend** a simple approach. First, decide what you want: relaxation, adventure or culture. Then set a budget. Planning ahead can **save** money, as prices tend to rise closer to the travel date. Many travel companies now **respond** to customer feedback quickly, adjusting offers to stay ahead of the **competition**.\n\nTourism also raises serious questions. In some popular places, the number of visitors has caused environmental damage. Forest fires **burn** more often in dry regions, and a **flood** of tourists can overwhelm small towns. There is an ongoing **debate** about how to **establish** sustainable tourism.\n\nSome local business **owners|owner** have found creative solutions. They limit visitor numbers and **demonstrate** traditional crafts to **enhance** the cultural experience. In one mountain village, **senior** residents teach visitors to cook local dishes, sharing knowledge with a new **generation**. Hotel **staff** are encouraged to recommend quieter places to **anyone** looking for peace.\n\nUltimately, travel can **motivate** us to understand the world better. The best holidays, many people say, are not the most expensive ones, but those that leave us with new perspectives."},"24":{"w":["powerful","record","repeat","wealth","purpose","truth","board","citizen","fashion","remote","electronic","figure","addition","cultivate","independent","root","safety","voice","comment","suffer"],"title":"Voices from Remote Villages","t":"In **remote** villages, many people still lack access to clean water, healthcare and the internet. For decades, their **voices|voice** were rarely heard in national debates. Today, **electronic** devices and social media are beginning to change this.\n\nA **powerful** example comes from a mountain village in Guizhou. A young teacher began to **record** short videos about daily life there and post them online. In **addition** to showing beautiful scenery, the videos revealed the **truth** about difficult conditions: children walking hours to school, elderly people who **suffer** without medical care.\n\nThe videos quickly attracted attention. Thousands of viewers left a **comment** asking how they could help. A charity **board** decided to fund a new school, and local officials promised to improve road **safety**. The teacher became a public **figure**, but she insisted that her **purpose** was simple: to give villagers a voice.\n\nExperts say such stories show the potential of digital media to **cultivate** active **citizens|citizen**. When people can share their experiences, they become more **independent** and confident. However, critics warn that online attention can also be shallow and short-lived; the same problems may **repeat** if real change does not take **root**.\n\nSome worry that rural life is becoming a **fashion** online, attracting viewers without creating real **wealth** for villagers. The teacher agrees that attention alone is not enough. \"Videos open the door,\" she says. \"But people must walk through it.\""},"25":{"w":["oil","profit","speed","theory","billion","monitor","debt","plastic","signal","classical","widely","characteristic","clearly","conclusion","operate","previous","sign","succeed","define","divide"],"title":"The Price of Plastic","t":"Since the 1950s, the world has produced more than eight **billion** tonnes of **plastic**. It is cheap, light and useful, which is why it is used so **widely**. But scientists have now **clearly** shown that plastic pollution is one of the greatest environmental threats of our time.\n\nThe problem has a long history. Plastic is made from **oil**, and its production has grown with the **speed** of economic development. Companies make huge **profits|profit** from plastic packaging, and consumers enjoy convenience. One **characteristic** of plastic, its durability, is also its biggest problem: it can last for hundreds of years.\n\nResearchers **monitor** plastic waste in rivers and oceans, and the data send a worrying **signal**. Tiny pieces of plastic have been found in fish, salt and even drinking water. A common **theory** suggests that these particles may harm human health, though more research is needed to reach a firm **conclusion**.\n\nSome countries have begun to act. **Previous** attempts to reduce plastic use often failed, but newer policies seem to **succeed**. Taxes on plastic bags, for example, have cut use dramatically. Factories that **operate** recycling systems receive government support, and some poorer countries receive help to reduce their **debt** in exchange for environmental action.\n\nExperts **define** the challenge as a shared responsibility. Governments, companies and individuals must **divide** the work fairly. Every **sign** of progress counts: one city orchestra now bans plastic bottles at its **classical** concerts, and many schools teach children to recycle from an early age."},"26":{"w":["urban","dangerous","handle","status","partner","satisfaction","instruction","label","gratitude","attempt","valuable","huge","method","total","advance","finance","oppose","structure","basis","express"],"title":"The Power of Gratitude","t":"In a busy **urban** life, it is easy to focus on what we lack rather than what we have. Yet psychologists say that **gratitude** — the habit of noticing and appreciating good things — can bring **huge** benefits. People who regularly **express** thanks report greater life **satisfaction** and better health.\n\nOne study asked participants to write down three things they were grateful for each evening. The **instruction** was simple, but the results were striking. After two months, the **total** level of stress among participants had fallen, while their sleep and mood had improved. Researchers concluded that gratitude provides a strong **basis** for well-being.\n\nWhy does this simple **method** work? One explanation is that gratitude changes the **structure** of our attention. Instead of noticing every problem, we begin to see **valuable** moments: a kind word from a **partner**, a beautiful sunset or a meal shared with friends. This shift helps people **handle** stress more calmly.\n\nNot everyone accepts these findings. Some critics **oppose** the idea that gratitude can solve serious problems. They warn that it is **dangerous** to tell people in poverty simply to \"be grateful,\" because it may ignore real injustice. Others say we should not **label** every positive emotion as a cure.\n\nStill, many experts believe gratitude is worth practising. Some companies now encourage employees to thank colleagues publicly, and **finance** departments even report fewer conflicts. In schools, teachers **attempt** to **advance** students' emotional skills through gratitude exercises. Regardless of social **status**, anyone can benefit from saying \"thank you\" a little more often."},"27":{"w":["adapt","line","original","score","spread","agriculture","budget","advertise","hire","object","rent","revolution","atmosphere","bacteria","drawing","livestock","direct","embrace","frequently","list"],"title":"A Farming Revolution","t":"Over the past century, **agriculture** has gone through a quiet **revolution**. New machines, chemicals and seeds have transformed the way farmers grow crops and raise **livestock**. In many countries, food production has increased dramatically, and fewer people go hungry.\n\nBut this progress has a cost. Large farms often depend on chemicals that damage soil and water. Some fertilisers release gases into the **atmosphere**, contributing to climate change. Overuse of medicine in animals has also helped dangerous **bacteria** **spread**, threatening human health.\n\nToday, many farmers are trying to **adapt**. They **embrace** new methods that protect the environment while maintaining production. Some **hire** experts to test soil quality; others **rent** small machines that use less fuel. Farmers **frequently** share ideas online, and some forums let users **score** each method out of ten.\n\nThe change also affects how food is sold. Supermarkets **advertise** \"organic\" and \"local\" products, and consumers are willing to pay more for them. Some farms sell **direct** to customers, who **line** up at weekend markets for fresh vegetables. Even so, many small farmers struggle to manage their **budget**.\n\nEducation is key. In one village, children made a **drawing** of their ideal farm, full of trees and animals. Their teacher displayed the pictures in a **list** of \"future farm ideas.\" The **original** goal was to teach environmental awareness, but the project became an **object** of national attention when a newspaper reported on it."},"28":{"w":["pretty","satisfy","vary","advocate","appreciate","article","assume","combine","detail","improvement","initiative","outcome","planet","remain","network","pollution","vision","waste","worldwide","firm"],"title":"Fighting Pollution Together","t":"**Pollution** is one of the greatest threats facing our **planet**. From plastic in the oceans to smoke in city air, its effects are felt **worldwide**. Scientists **assume** that the problem will get worse unless governments, companies and citizens **combine** their efforts.\n\nEncouragingly, there has been real **improvement** in some areas. Many cities have introduced cleaner buses and stricter rules on factories. In London, for example, a new **initiative** charges drivers of old cars a daily fee. The **outcome** has been a significant drop in air pollution levels.\n\nEnvironmental groups **advocate** even stronger action. They argue that we must reduce **waste**, especially single-use plastic, and invest in renewable energy. Some companies have responded positively. One technology **firm** has promised to run all its offices on solar power by 2030.\n\nIndividuals also play a part. Small changes, such as recycling or using public transport, may seem **pretty** minor, but together they make a difference. A recent **article** in a scientific journal showed that consumer choices **vary** widely between countries, and that education strongly influences behaviour.\n\nThe challenges **remain** enormous, but there is reason for hope. Global **networks|network** of scientists and activists now share data and ideas instantly. Their shared **vision** is of a cleaner, healthier world. As one activist put it, \"We must **appreciate** what nature gives us. It cannot **satisfy** our needs forever if we destroy it.\" Every **detail** of our daily lives, from what we eat to how we travel, affects the environment."},"29":{"w":["accident","luxury","pursuit","wedding","strengthen","encounter","generate","political","charge","expectation","highly","limit","adopt","recommendation","search","video","character","entire","extent","fight"],"title":"Weddings in Modern China","t":"In China, the **wedding** has long been one of the most important events in a family's life. In the past, ceremonies were often simple, but today many couples, **highly** influenced by social media, hold luxurious celebrations. Some families even borrow money to meet social **expectations|expectation**.\n\nSociologists say this trend reflects the **entire** country's rapid economic growth. As incomes rise, people **adopt** new lifestyles and **generate** new demands. **Luxury** weddings, complete with professional photographers and **video** crews, have become a symbol of success. But critics argue that the **pursuit** of status can put families under heavy pressure.\n\nThe government has tried to **limit** extravagant spending. **Political** campaigns encourage simpler ceremonies, and some local officials have published a **recommendation** for modest celebrations. To some **extent**, these efforts have worked. In some villages, couples now hold group weddings at low cost.\n\nYoung couples have their own views. Many say they want weddings that reflect their **character**, not just tradition. Some **search** online for creative ideas, while others choose to spend their money on travel instead. \"We don't want to **fight** with our parents,\" one bride explained, \"but we want our own style.\"\n\nThere are also practical concerns. Organisers must handle large crowds and prevent any **accident**. Many hotels **charge** high fees during popular months. Couples sometimes **encounter** difficulties in finding a free date. Experts suggest that families **strengthen** communication early to avoid conflict."},"30":{"w":["mainly","recognition","depression","practical","speech","confidence","editor","danger","extremely","virtue","consume","impossible","opinion","rapid","sort","ancient","attend","eliminate","initial","multiple"],"title":"The Ancient Art of Public Speaking","t":"Public speaking has been valued since **ancient** times. Greek philosophers taught students to persuade audiences through clear arguments and **virtue**. Today, the ability to give a good **speech** is still considered an essential skill.\n\nYet many people find it **extremely** difficult. Surveys show that fear of public speaking is one of the most common anxieties, and for some it can even lead to **depression**. \"When I stand in front of an audience, my mind goes blank,\" one student explains. \"It feels **impossible**.\"\n\nExperts offer **practical** advice. First, prepare carefully and **sort** your ideas into a clear structure. Second, practise in front of friends and ask for their **opinion**. Third, focus on the message rather than on yourself. **Initial** nervousness is normal, they say, and it usually disappears after the first minute.\n\nConfidence grows with experience. Many universities encourage students to **attend** debate clubs, where they can practise **multiple** times a week. Over time, students learn to speak at a natural pace rather than a **rapid**, nervous rush. Their **confidence** grows, and their fear is gradually **eliminated|eliminate**.\n\nSome speakers achieve great **recognition**. A former newspaper **editor** who now gives talks around the world says she was once terrified of speaking. \"The **danger** is not that people will judge you,\" she says. \"It's that you will never share your ideas.\" Her talks focus **mainly** on how we **consume** information in the digital age."},"31":{"w":["regardless","regularly","reliable","appropriate","extreme","normal","perceive","psychological","quit","rarely","universal","used","visitor","yield","examine","hide","ideal","illness","male","moral"],"title":"Why We Avoid the Doctor","t":"Many people **rarely** visit the doctor, even when they feel unwell. Some believe their symptoms are **normal**; others **perceive** medical visits as expensive or frightening. Doctors say that this habit can turn a small **illness** into a serious one.\n\nResearch suggests that the reasons are largely **psychological**. People fear bad news, so they **hide** their worries or wait for problems to disappear. **Male** patients, in particular, are less likely than women to seek help early. Many of them grew up believing that strength means never complaining.\n\nExperts argue that regular check-ups are the **ideal** way to prevent disease. People who see a doctor **regularly** are more likely to catch health problems early, when treatment is simpler and cheaper. A **reliable** doctor can also give **appropriate** advice on diet, exercise and stress.\n\nCultural attitudes play a role too. In some communities, people **used** to rely on traditional remedies rather than modern medicine. Doctors now work with community leaders to **examine** these beliefs respectfully. They stress that health care is a **universal** right, **regardless** of income or background.\n\nSimple changes can **yield** big results. Some hospitals now send text reminders to patients, and others offer evening appointments for busy workers. A **visitor** to one clinic in Shanghai noticed posters encouraging people to **quit** smoking and walk more. Doctors say there is also a **moral** dimension: caring for our health helps protect our families. In **extreme** cases, early action can save lives."},"32":{"w":["failure","greatly","obvious","count","implement","location","abandon","launch","path","refer","replace","wealthy","alternative","indeed","instance","range","regarding","resist","separate","statement"],"title":"Learning from Failure","t":"Most of us fear **failure**. We **abandon** projects at the first sign of trouble, worried that others will judge us. Yet history shows that failure is often a necessary step on the **path** to success.\n\nConsider the story of a young engineer who wanted to **launch** a small rocket company. His first three tests failed, and investors began to **resist** further funding. **Indeed**, many people told him to **replace** his dream with a safer career. But each failure taught him something new, and his fourth attempt succeeded.\n\nPsychologists **refer** to this ability as \"resilience.\" People who are resilient see failure as information rather than a judgement. For **instance**, a student who fails an exam might analyse which topics were weak and **implement** a better study plan. The **range** of benefits is wide: resilient people tend to be healthier and happier.\n\nIt is **obvious** that some failures have serious consequences. A medical mistake or a financial loss can affect many lives. But experts say that society **greatly** underestimates the value of small, everyday failures. Schools in some countries now **separate** grades from feedback, so that students can learn without fear.\n\nNot everyone has the same freedom to fail. **Wealthy** families can support children who try risky careers, while poorer students may have no **alternative**. A recent government **statement** **regarding** youth unemployment highlighted this problem. Experts argue that what really **counts|count** is creating safe spaces to try, fail and try again, wherever a person's **location** or background."},"33":{"w":["statistics","whatever","celebrate","critic","goods","witness","proportion","race","wild","stock","mirror","musician","rocket","represent","tie","complain","estimate","perspective","spot","beginning"],"title":"The Joy of Music","t":"From the **beginning** of human history, people have used music to **celebrate**, mourn and connect with one another. Archaeologists have found musical instruments more than 40,000 years old. Today, music remains a universal language that **represents|represent** our deepest emotions.\n\n**Statistics** show that most people listen to music every day. A recent survey found that the **proportion** of young people who play an instrument has risen in the past decade. A **musician** interviewed for the study said that music is like a **mirror**: it reflects who we are and how we feel.\n\nMusic also has a powerful effect on the brain. Scientists who **estimate** the benefits of musical training say it improves memory and concentration. **Whatever** our age, learning an instrument can keep our minds active. Some hospitals even use music to calm patients before surgery.\n\nNot everyone agrees on what counts as good music. A **critic** once **complained|complain** that pop songs all sound the same, while others defend them as fun and accessible. Such debates are a **race** without a finish line, since taste depends on personal **perspective**.\n\nMusic has an economic side too. Concert tickets, instruments and recordings are valuable **goods**, and the music industry's **stock** market value has grown rapidly. Some musicians **witness** **wild** success overnight, their careers rising like a **rocket**. Others work for years in a single **spot**, playing in small cafés. Yet many say the **tie** between musician and audience is the real reward."},"34":{"w":["couple","fan","final","fix","indicate","institute","nowadays","official","phenomenon","priority","proper","rapidly","threaten","ultimately","broad","conclude","delay","distance","distinguish","household"],"title":"The Changing Household","t":"**Nowadays**, the typical **household** looks very different from fifty years ago. Many young **couples|couple** choose to have fewer children, and more people live alone. Researchers at a national **institute** say that this **phenomenon** reflects **broad** changes in society.\n\nThe data **indicate** several causes. Housing has become expensive, especially in large cities, and many people **delay** marriage until their thirties. Work often requires long hours and travel over great **distances|distance**, leaving little time for family life. Some young people say that career development is their top **priority**.\n\nThese changes **rapidly** affect public policy. **Official** figures show that the population is ageing, and governments worry about future labour shortages. Some experts warn that falling birth rates could **threaten** economic growth. Others argue that society will **ultimately** adapt, as it has done before.\n\nIt is important to **distinguish** between choice and necessity. For some, living alone is a free and happy decision. For others, it results from financial pressure or a lack of **proper** support. Policy makers must understand these differences before they can **fix** the problem.\n\nResearchers **conclude** that there is no single \"right\" kind of family. A football **fan** who lives alone and a family of five may both lead fulfilling lives. The **final** goal, experts say, should be to give everyone the support they need."},"35":{"w":["judge","preference","rational","review","switch","talent","analyze","campaign","critical","extra","mark","sacrifice","smart","threat","urge","war","assess","evolve","item","discipline"],"title":"Talent and Discipline","t":"Is success the result of natural **talent** or hard work? This question has been debated for centuries, from the training of soldiers in times of **war** to the modern classroom. Recent research suggests that while talent matters, **discipline** often plays a more **critical** role.\n\nPsychologists who **analyze** the careers of top athletes, musicians and scientists have found a common pattern. Successful people practise deliberately, **assess** their weaknesses honestly and **review** their progress regularly. They are willing to **sacrifice** short-term pleasure for long-term goals. Many describe a strong **urge** to improve that drives them every day.\n\nOf course, being **smart** helps. But intelligence alone cannot guarantee success. Many gifted students fail because they never learn to work hard, while others with **extra** determination achieve remarkable results. Teachers say that a good **mark** in an exam is less important than a love of learning.\n\nOur **preference** for celebrating natural talent may even be harmful. When children are praised only for being clever, they may avoid challenges for fear of failure. A more **rational** approach is to praise effort and strategy.\n\nThe idea has spread beyond schools. Some companies **switch** their hiring focus from qualifications to attitude, and a national **campaign** encourages young people to develop practical skills. The greatest **threat** to success, experts say, is not a lack of ability but a lack of persistence. Skills **evolve** with practice, and every small **item** of progress counts. As one coach says, \"Don't **judge** yourself by your talent. Judge yourself by your effort.\""},"36":{"w":["genetic","possibility","bay","manufacturer","revolutionary","actor","gradually","hit","opposite","react","advise","analysis","application","closely","compete","differ","gather","helpful","reader","reform"],"title":"The Promise of Genetic Medicine","t":"In the past twenty years, **genetic** research has advanced at an astonishing speed. Scientists can now read a person's DNA in a few hours, opening the **possibility** of medicine designed for each individual. Many experts describe this as a **revolutionary** change in health care.\n\nThe **application** of this knowledge is already visible. Doctors can **advise** patients about their risk of certain diseases and recommend early tests. Drug **manufacturers|manufacturer** are developing treatments that target specific genes, and some patients **react** much better to these medicines than to traditional ones.\n\nYet the field also raises difficult questions. Some people fear that genetic information could be used against them, for example by insurance companies. Experts **differ** on how to protect privacy. Others argue that wealthy countries will benefit while poorer nations fall behind. A careful **analysis** of these risks is essential.\n\nResearchers are working **closely** with ethicists and governments to **reform** regulations. They **gather** opinions from patients, doctors and the public. Many believe that the best approach is to move **gradually**, testing new treatments carefully before using them widely.\n\nThe public's understanding is also changing. A popular film about a scientist, starring a famous **actor**, became a surprise **hit** last year, and many **readers|reader** have bought books explaining genetics in simple language. Universities **compete** to attract top researchers, and a new research centre has opened on the **bay** in San Francisco. Some critics hold the **opposite** view, warning that hopes are too high. Still, most agree that the research is **helpful** and worth pursuing."},"37":{"w":["survive","virtual","false","manner","responsible","upon","capacity","cold","commit","difficulty","excessive","freedom","kill","unnecessary","useful","employment","legal","mood","organize","transportation"],"title":"Freedom and Responsibility","t":"In modern societies, **freedom** is often seen as one of the highest values. People expect to choose their careers, express their opinions and live as they wish. But philosophers remind us that freedom comes with responsibility.\n\nConsider the internet. It gives us the freedom to share ideas instantly, yet it also spreads **false** information. Some users **commit** crimes online, such as stealing personal data, while many others **kill** hours scrolling through videos. Governments face great **difficulty** in deciding how much control is **unnecessary** and how much is essential. **Legal** experts argue that clear rules are **useful** for protecting both freedom and safety.\n\nThe same tension exists at work. Employees value the freedom to work from home, which became common in **virtual** offices during the pandemic. But some managers worry about **excessive** freedom reducing productivity. Others say that trust improves **mood** and motivation. Companies that **organize** flexible schedules carefully often see better results.\n\nFreedom also affects public life. Cities that improve public **transportation** give citizens more choices about where to live and work, and elderly people no longer have to wait in the **cold** for hours. Better **employment** opportunities can help young people become independent. In this **manner**, social policies expand personal freedom.\n\nUltimately, a free society depends **upon** citizens who are **responsible**. Each of us has the **capacity** to make choices that help or harm others. As a famous writer once said, freedom is not the right to do whatever we want, but the ability to do what we should. Without this balance, societies may not **survive** difficult times."},"38":{"w":["vital","fair","blood","destroy","gene","numerous","suppose","association","contribution","element","expand","immediate","relatively","survival","alter","enter","exert","mutual","persist","plenty"],"title":"The Secrets in Our Blood","t":"Every cell in the human body contains thousands of **genes|gene**, instructions that shape everything from eye colour to **blood** type. Scientists have identified **numerous** genes linked to disease, and their discoveries are changing medicine.\n\nOne **vital** area of research is the study of inherited illnesses. Some diseases **persist** in families for generations because of a single faulty gene. Researchers are now learning to **alter** these genes, offering hope to patients who once had little chance of **survival**. Such treatments are still **relatively** rare, but progress is rapid.\n\nInternational **association** has made this possible. Scientists in different countries share data and work together, and each **contribution** adds to the overall picture. A **mutual** commitment to open research has helped to **expand** knowledge quickly.\n\nHowever, genetic research also faces ethical challenges. Some people **suppose** that we could one day design \"perfect\" children, a prospect that worries many. Experts **exert** great effort to prevent the misuse of technology. They stress that genetic tools must be **fair** and available to everyone.\n\nThere is **plenty** of reason for hope. **Immediate** benefits include better diagnosis and more effective drugs. As new data **enter** the global research system, scientists hope to understand every **element** of the human genome. But they also warn that careless use could **destroy** public trust."},"39":{"w":["propose","remains","schedule","acquire","commercial","complicated","expense","predict","prior","requirement","sustain","continent","preserve","radical","sufficient","writer","adventure","collection","discovery","dispute"],"title":"Protecting Ancient Remains","t":"Across every **continent**, archaeologists are racing to **preserve** ancient sites before they disappear. Climate change, tourism and development threaten **remains** that have survived for thousands of years. Experts **predict** that many sites could be lost within decades.\n\nProtecting these places is **complicated**. Excavations require careful planning, special equipment and a fixed **schedule**. The **expense** is high, and governments often lack **sufficient** funds. A basic **requirement** for any project is the support of local communities, who may depend on the land for farming.\n\nSome researchers **propose** **radical** solutions. One idea is to create digital copies of sites, allowing visitors to explore them virtually without causing damage. Others suggest limiting tourist numbers and using the income to **sustain** conservation work. **Prior** experience shows that sites managed by local people are often better protected.\n\nOwnership can lead to **dispute**. Museums in wealthy countries hold many ancient objects, and some nations want their treasures returned. These debates are complex, involving history, law and **commercial** interests. Many museums now **acquire** new objects only with clear evidence of their origin.\n\nDespite the challenges, archaeology continues to inspire. Every new **discovery** adds to our understanding of human history. A famous **writer** once described archaeology as \"the greatest **adventure** of all.\" Some museums have even built a **collection** of children's books about ancient civilisations, encouraging young readers to care for the past."},"40":{"w":["super","particle","coal","feedback","otherwise","childhood","compel","consideration","regular","tendency","thrive","unlike","agency","annual","discussion","employ","inspire","player","recover","scholar"],"title":"Learning Through Play","t":"Many people believe that **childhood** should be a time of serious study. But educational **scholars|scholar** increasingly argue that play is just as important. Children who play freely, they say, develop creativity, social skills and a love of learning.\n\nResearch supports this view. An **annual** study by a national education **agency** found that children with regular free time performed better in reading and maths. Teachers who **employ** games in the classroom report that students are more engaged and **recover** faster from failure. Play, it seems, helps children **thrive**.\n\nUnfortunately, there is a growing **tendency** to replace play with homework and extra classes. Parents feel **compelled|compel** to give their children every advantage. **Otherwise**, they fear, their children will fall behind. But experts warn that too much pressure can harm mental health.\n\n**Unlike** traditional lessons, play allows children to learn from experience. A child building a tower of blocks learns about balance and gravity; a young **player** in a team game learns cooperation. Teachers can **inspire** curiosity by giving students time to explore. Regular **feedback** helps them reflect on what they have learned.\n\nThese ideas deserve serious **consideration** in education policy. In one school, teachers led a **discussion** with parents about the benefits of play, and many changed their views. Even simple activities, such as studying a **particle** of sand under a microscope or learning how **coal** forms, can feel **super** exciting when children discover them for themselves. A **regular** routine of play and study, experts say, offers the best of both worlds."},"41":{"w":["strongly","transport","truly","unless","carbon","comparison","devote","direction","diverse","feed","fellow","following","frustrate","historical","immediately","wisdom","alarm","ancestor","battle","born"],"title":"Lessons from Our Ancestors","t":"Every culture has stories about its **ancestors|ancestor**. These stories describe how people survived hard winters, fought in **battle** and learned to **feed** their families. Historians say such tales are more than entertainment; they contain practical **wisdom** that remains useful today.\n\nConsider traditional farming. In many regions, farmers once planted **diverse** crops in the same field. A **comparison** with modern methods shows that this approach protected soil and reduced pests. Scientists now **strongly** recommend similar techniques to cut **carbon** emissions and protect the environment.\n\n**Historical** records also reveal how communities organised **transport** and trade. Ancient merchants travelled great distances, sharing goods and ideas. Their journeys show that people were **truly** connected long before the internet. Each traveller depended on **fellow** traders for safety and information.\n\nOf course, not every old practice is wise. Some traditions caused harm, and modern knowledge can **frustrate** those who prefer the past. Experts warn that we should not **immediately** accept every ancient custom. The **following** principle is useful: learn from the past, but test its lessons carefully.\n\nMany people **born** in cities have little contact with these traditions. Schools and museums now **devote** time to teaching them. One museum uses an old village **alarm** bell to show how communities warned each other of danger. Its message is simple: **unless** we understand where we come from, we may lose our **direction**."},"42":{"w":["curiosity","extend","imitate","tradition","vehicle","artificial","bill","deed","dependent","detect","fiction","giant","regulation","african","court","muscle","platform","concert","rare","arrow"],"title":"The Age of Artificial Intelligence","t":"**Artificial** intelligence (AI) has moved from science **fiction** into everyday life. It recommends films, helps doctors **detect** diseases and even drives **vehicles|vehicle**. Some experts believe AI will be the most important technology of the century.\n\nAI systems learn by analysing huge amounts of data. They can **imitate** human language, recognise faces and predict behaviour. Technology **giants|giant** have invested billions in this field, and many companies now **extend** AI tools to customers through online **platforms|platform**.\n\nYet the rapid growth of AI raises serious concerns. Many workers fear that machines will replace them, and some people have become too **dependent** on technology. Governments are debating new **regulations|regulation** to protect privacy and prevent discrimination. In one well-known case, a **court** ruled that a company had used AI unfairly in hiring decisions.\n\nExperts stress that AI should support, not replace, human judgement. A doctor using AI to read X-rays, for example, still needs knowledge and experience. Technology cannot replace the human **curiosity** that drives discovery, nor the moral sense that guides good **deeds|deed**.\n\nAI is also changing culture. Some musicians use it to compose music for a **concert**, and a recent **African** film festival featured a story written with AI's help. Such projects are still **rare**, but they show new possibilities. Engineers compare AI to an **arrow**: it flies fast, but someone must aim it. Critics say that a careful **tradition** of public debate is needed, and a new **bill** in parliament may soon set the rules. Like a **muscle**, our ability to think independently must be exercised, or it will grow weak."},"43":{"w":["recruit","expose","highlight","overcome","stem","successfully","attitude","display","harmful","route","transform","aid","beneficial","efficient","emerge","emphasize","excite","fuel","host","prosperity"],"title":"Clean Energy Takes Off","t":"For more than a century, coal and oil have provided most of the world's energy. But burning these **fuels|fuel** releases gases that are **harmful** to the climate. Today, clean energy sources such as wind and solar power are beginning to **transform** the global economy.\n\nSeveral countries have **successfully** shifted away from fossil fuels. Denmark, for example, now produces about half of its electricity from wind. Experts **highlight** this as evidence that clean energy can support **prosperity**. New technologies have also made solar panels far more **efficient** and affordable.\n\nStill, there are challenges to **overcome**. Wind and sunlight are not always available, and storing energy is expensive. Some problems **stem** from old power networks that were designed for coal plants. Governments must invest in new **routes|route** for transmitting electricity across regions.\n\nPublic **attitude** is changing quickly. Many people are **excited|excite** by electric cars, and cities **display** charging stations in public squares. International organisations **aid** poorer nations in building solar farms, which are especially **beneficial** in remote areas.\n\nExperts **emphasize** that the transition must be fair. Workers in the coal industry need support and new skills, and companies may **recruit** them for clean energy projects. Last year, a major city agreed to **host** an international conference on renewable energy. New ideas continue to **emerge**, and many believe that clean energy will soon **expose** the true cost of fossil fuels."},"44":{"w":["recall","square","stage","strength","therefore","vast","wonder","appearance","convey","correct","electricity","era","explanation","finally","hardly","inform","internal","largely","meanwhile","ordinary"],"title":"Electricity and Everyday Life","t":"It is **hardly** possible to imagine modern life without **electricity**. It powers our homes, factories and phones, and its **appearance** in the late nineteenth century marked the start of a new **era**. **Ordinary** people suddenly had light at night and machines that saved hours of work.\n\nAt first, many people did not understand how electricity worked. Scientists struggled to **convey** their discoveries to the public, and some newspapers printed frightening stories. A clear **explanation** came **largely** from teachers and engineers who visited towns to demonstrate the new technology. Audiences watched in **wonder** as bulbs lit up on a **stage**.\n\nElectricity **finally** reached rural areas decades later. In China, a **vast** programme brought power to remote villages, and older villagers still **recall** the excitement. \"We gathered in the village **square** and cheered,\" one woman remembers. Meanwhile, industries grew rapidly, giving the country new economic **strength**.\n\nToday, electricity faces new challenges. Demand is rising, and much of it still comes from burning coal. Governments must **inform** citizens about energy saving and find **correct** solutions for the future. **Therefore**, many countries are investing in renewable energy and smarter grids.\n\nTechnology companies are also improving **internal** systems to reduce power use. **Meanwhile**, ordinary families can help by turning off lights and choosing efficient appliances. Small actions, repeated by millions, can make a real difference."},"45":{"w":["participate","president","visual","warn","crucial","dominate","inside","literature","native","poverty","privilege","series","suit","additional","advertisement","contract","fairly","filter","freeze","inevitable"],"title":"The Fight Against Poverty","t":"**Poverty** remains one of the most **crucial** challenges facing the world. Millions of people still lack clean water, education and health care. Yet experts say that real progress is possible when governments, businesses and communities **participate** together.\n\nOne successful approach is investing in education. Children who finish school earn more and are less likely to fall into poverty. Some countries provide **additional** support for girls, who often face greater barriers. A former **president** of a developing nation once said that education is \"the key that unlocks every door.\"\n\nCulture and identity matter too. **Native** communities often suffer more from poverty because they are excluded from economic opportunities. Programmes that respect local **literature**, language and traditions tend to be more effective. Experts **warn** that outside solutions rarely **suit** every community.\n\nBusiness also has a role. Large companies **dominate** many markets, and critics say they should share profits **fairly**. Some firms now sign a **contract** promising to pay suppliers a living wage. A recent **advertisement** campaign featured a **series** of short films showing farmers' lives from the **inside**.\n\nSome argue that inequality is **inevitable** in any economy. Others insist that being born rich should not be a **privilege** that decides a child's future. Many governments have chosen to **freeze** prices of basic food during crises. Clear **visual** data, such as maps of income levels, help citizens **filter** information and understand the problem."},"46":{"w":["kilometer","procedure","familiar","primitive","editorial","accelerate","necessarily","persuade","productive","properly","transformation","actual","behave","consult","definitely","everyday","intelligent","intend","massive","prospect"],"title":"Smart Machines in Everyday Life","t":"Many of the devices we use **everyday** are becoming more **intelligent**. Phones understand our voices, cars warn us of danger, and fridges remind us when we are running out of milk. Experts say this **transformation** will **accelerate** in the coming decade.\n\nThese changes bring **massive** benefits. Smart systems can make factories more **productive** and reduce energy waste. Doctors **consult** computer programmes to check diagnoses, and the **actual** time needed to analyse medical images has fallen dramatically. A city in Denmark even uses sensors to measure every **kilometer** of road for traffic and pollution.\n\nYet old worries remain. Not everyone is **familiar** with new technology, and older people may find it confusing. Critics warn that smart devices do not **necessarily** make life better. If machines **behave** in unexpected ways, the results can be dangerous. Clear **procedures|procedure** are needed to ensure that technology is used **properly**.\n\nSome writers compare our age to a **primitive** stage of a new civilisation. A recent newspaper **editorial** argued that we still do not fully understand the **prospect** of living with intelligent machines. Experts **definitely** agree on one point: people must remain in control.\n\nCompanies **intend** to introduce even more smart products, and they spend heavily to **persuade** consumers to buy them. Before following every new trend, however, we should ask a simple question: does this technology really improve our lives?"},"47":{"w":["remarkable","scene","abundant","afford","exchange","fee","honest","imagination","introduction","mix","okay","remind","salary","site","technique","turning","unable","active","advanced","arise"],"title":"A Remarkable Teacher","t":"Every school has one teacher whom students remember for a lifetime. At my old school, it was Mr. Wang, a physics teacher with a **remarkable** talent for making difficult ideas simple. His lessons were full of experiments, jokes and stories that sparked our **imagination**.\n\nMr. Wang was not interested in the most **advanced** technology. Instead, he used everyday objects to explain complex ideas. He would **mix** water and oil to show how liquids behave, or drop a ball down the stairs to explain gravity. His **technique** was simple: let students see, touch and wonder.\n\nHe was also honest about his own past. As a young man, he had been **unable** to **afford** university **fees|fee** and had worked in a factory for years. He earned a low **salary** but saved money to study at night. \"Hard times are not the end,\" he used to **remind** us. \"They are a **turning** point.\"\n\nHe encouraged us to be **active** learners. He organised an **exchange** programme with a school in another province and took us to visit a nuclear power **site**. Every class began with a short **introduction** to a famous scientist, and an **abundant** supply of questions followed.\n\nMr. Wang retired last year. At his farewell party, former students shared memories, and one **scene** made everyone laugh: a video of him dancing after his class won a science competition. Problems often **arise** in education, he once told us, but an **honest** teacher can solve most of them. \"**Okay**, now let's begin,\" he would say every morning, and we always did."},"48":{"w":["assumption","bond","confident","constant","convince","corporation","crack","device","directly","facility","infant","intellectual","loan","narrow","naturally","observation","punish","resolve","steady","tough"],"title":"The First Years of Life","t":"Scientists have long been fascinated by the minds of **infants|infant**. Through careful **observation**, researchers have discovered that babies learn far more in their first years than adults once believed. They recognise faces, respond to voices and form strong **bonds|bond** with their parents.\n\nEarly childhood is a period of **constant** change. A baby's brain makes millions of new connections every day, and **naturally**, the quality of care affects this process. Children who receive love and attention tend to grow up **confident** and curious. Those who face neglect may struggle with **intellectual** and emotional development.\n\nThese findings have influenced public policy. Some governments provide **loans|loan** and support to new parents, and large **corporations|corporation** offer longer parental leave. In some cities, every neighbourhood has a **facility** where parents can meet and learn about child care.\n\nExperts warn against one common **assumption**: that babies learn best from screens. In fact, research **directly** contradicts this. Babies learn language through real conversations, not through any electronic **device**. Parents are advised to talk, sing and read with their children every day.\n\nRaising children is **tough**, and many parents feel pressure to be perfect. Experts try to **convince** them that small, **steady** efforts matter more than expensive toys. They also advise parents not to **punish** children harshly, but to **resolve** problems patiently. The gap between rich and poor families can **narrow** when good support is available to everyone. As one researcher put it, \"Small **cracks|crack** in childhood can become large ones later. Early care is the best prevention.\""},"49":{"w":["unit","willing","assistant","deliver","federal","genius","keen","military","protection","sophisticated","stream","charity","drug","festival","hurt","insect","leadership","liberal","maintenance","phase"],"title":"Caring for Others","t":"Across the world, millions of people volunteer their time to help others. They work for a **charity**, organise community events or support people who are sick or **hurt**. Experts say that volunteering benefits both the helpers and those they help.\n\nConsider a volunteer **unit** in a small town in Yunnan. Its members **deliver** meals to elderly residents, plant trees by a mountain **stream** and protect a rare **insect** species from extinction. Their **leadership** comes from an energetic retired teacher with a **genius** for organising people.\n\nVolunteers must be **willing** to work hard. Some help in hospitals, where they assist doctors in giving out **drugs|drug** and comforting patients. Others work as an **assistant** in schools, helping children with reading. Many are **keen** to learn new skills, from first aid to the **maintenance** of community gardens.\n\nGovernments also play a role. In some countries, **federal** programmes fund volunteer work, and **military** personnel help during natural disasters. **Liberal** and conservative politicians alike praise volunteering, though they disagree on how much the state should support it.\n\nVolunteering has become more **sophisticated** in recent years. Online platforms match volunteers with organisations, and training programmes prepare them for each **phase** of their work. At the town's annual **festival**, volunteers are honoured for their **protection** of the environment and their care for others."},"50":{"w":["valley","regret","toy","treasure","horror","scent","wireless","contrast","competitive","globe","lifetime","neglect","notice","objective","occasionally","spirit","volume","widespread","anticipate","belong"],"title":"A Treasure in the Valley","t":"In a quiet **valley** in the south, archaeologists recently discovered a remarkable **treasure**: a collection of ancient pottery, coins and children's **toys|toy** buried more than two thousand years ago. The discovery has attracted **widespread** attention and drawn visitors from across the **globe**.\n\nLocal people had long told stories about the valley's **spirit**, but few had expected real treasure. \"We **occasionally** found old coins in the fields,\" one farmer said, \"but we never imagined this.\" Researchers now **anticipate** further discoveries in the area.\n\nThe find offers a striking **contrast** between ancient and modern life. Toys made of clay show that children played games similar to those of today. A small bottle that once held a **scent** made from flowers suggests that people valued beauty and pleasure. Historians say such objects **belong** to ordinary families, not just the rich.\n\nHowever, protecting the site has not been easy. Treasure hunters have damaged nearby areas, and the government has been accused of **neglect**. Some villagers **regret** that they did not report earlier finds. The local museum now uses **wireless** cameras to watch the site at night.\n\nThe discovery also sparked a **competitive** race between universities wanting to study it. An **objective** committee has been set up to decide who will lead the research. The huge **volume** of material will take years to analyse. Visitors who **notice** the care taken with every object often express wonder rather than **horror** at how much was once forgotten. For many, it is a once-in-a-**lifetime** experience."},"51":{"w":["domestic","favor","fund","journey","mass","obtain","substantial","symbol","treatment","attack","authority","blame","capable","champion","contain","core","deserve","estate","input","lift"],"title":"The Price of a Home","t":"In many large cities, buying a home has become a difficult **journey** for young people. Prices in the real **estate** market have risen sharply, and a **substantial** part of every salary goes to rent. For many families, a home is no longer just a place to live but a **symbol** of success.\n\nEconomists **blame** several factors. Low interest rates encouraged a **mass** of investors to buy property, and **domestic** demand grew faster than supply. Some investors **obtain** several flats and leave them empty, waiting for prices to rise. Critics **attack** this practice, saying that housing should not be treated like stocks.\n\nGovernments have responded in different ways. Local **authorities|authority** in some cities **fund** affordable housing projects, while others **lift** restrictions on building. A few cities offer special **treatment** to young professionals, such as lower deposits. Each policy **contains|contain** risks, and experts disagree on which works best.\n\nThe **core** problem, many argue, is inequality. People who already own property gain wealth, while those without property fall further behind. \"Everyone **deserves|deserve** a safe home,\" says one housing **champion**, who campaigns for tenants' rights. She believes the public should have more **input** into housing decisions.\n\nYoung people are adapting creatively. Some share flats with friends, and others move to smaller cities. Many are **capable** of building good lives without owning property. As one young engineer put it, \"I **favor** freedom over a mortgage. A home is where people care about you, not just where you sleep.\""},"52":{"w":["museum","perfectly","pleasant","proposal","rank","remedy","select","absolutely","accomplish","attractive","concerning","cycle","department","doubt","exposure","mile","prescribe","promise","repair","saving"],"title":"A Museum Comes Back to Life","t":"Ten years ago, the city **museum** was almost empty. Its rooms were dark, its displays old and its visitors few. Today, it **ranks|rank** among the most popular attractions in the region. How did this transformation happen?\n\nIt began with a **proposal** from a young curator. She suggested that the museum **select** a few powerful stories instead of showing thousands of objects. Her plan was **absolutely** clear: every room should help visitors feel something. The museum's **department** of education also created activities for children and families.\n\nChanges were made step by step. Old cases were **repaired|repair**, lights were improved and the café was made more **attractive**. The museum's leaders **promised|promise** to listen to visitors, and they introduced free entry on Sundays. The **savings|saving** from energy-efficient lighting helped pay for new exhibitions.\n\nOne exhibition, **concerning** the history of local medicine, proved especially popular. It showed how doctors once **prescribed|prescribe** strange **remedies|remedy**, from herbal soups to animal bones. Visitors also learned about the **cycle** of disease and recovery in ancient times. Some objects were displayed for the first time, giving them public **exposure** after centuries in storage.\n\nThere were doubters at first. Some critics expressed **doubt** that a small museum could **accomplish** such change. But visitors now travel many **miles|mile** to see it, and the atmosphere is **pleasant** and welcoming. \"The museum **perfectly** shows that history can be exciting,\" one visitor wrote in the guestbook."},"53":{"w":["stability","unexpected","campus","coast","division","guy","missing","passenger","version","apartment","button","flash","print","wolf","baggage","pigeon","convenient","attach","consist","exhaust"],"title":"Lost on the Coast","t":"Last summer, my friend Kai took a trip along the **coast** of southern China. He planned to stay in a small **apartment** near the sea, but the journey did not go as expected. Within hours of arriving, he discovered that his **baggage** was **missing**.\n\nThe airline staff were helpful but slow. They asked him to **print** a form, fill it in and **attach** a photo of his suitcase. The process seemed to **consist** of endless paperwork. Kai felt **exhausted|exhaust** after waiting for hours at the airport, surrounded by angry **passengers|passenger** with similar problems.\n\nThings soon improved. A friendly **guy** at the information desk lent him a phone charger and showed him a **convenient** bus route to his apartment. On the bus, Kai met a student from the local university who invited him to visit her **campus**. She showed him a hill where wild birds lived, including a rare **pigeon** with green feathers.\n\nKai later learned that the region has a rich wildlife. Researchers had even reported seeing a **wolf** in the hills, an **unexpected** discovery that attracted national attention. Scientists believe that the **stability** of the forest ecosystem has improved after years of protection.\n\nHis bag finally arrived three days later. Inside, everything was safe, though one **button** had come off his jacket. \"Next time, I'll keep a copy of everything,\" he said. He also learned that the airline's lost-baggage **division** had a newer **version** of its app that tracks bags in real time. \"In a **flash**,\" he joked, \"I became an expert in lost luggage.\""},"54":{"w":["frequent","heavily","ruin","adjust","alert","birth","capital","consistent","efficiency","endure","fresh","ingredient","insight","lecture","magazine","nevertheless","primarily","reasonable","reduction","shopping"],"title":"The Science of Cooking","t":"Cooking is not just an art; it is also a science. Every recipe depends on chemical reactions between **ingredients|ingredient**, and understanding these reactions can make us better cooks. A popular **lecture** series at a university in Beijing explores exactly this topic.\n\nThe lecturer, a chemistry professor, begins each class by cooking a simple dish. She explains why **fresh** vegetables taste sweeter and why bread rises. Her **insight** is that small changes in temperature or timing can **ruin** a dish or make it perfect. Students learn to **adjust** recipes based on scientific principles.\n\nThe course also addresses health. Many people rely **heavily** on processed food, which often contains too much salt and sugar. The professor argues that a **reduction** in such food would improve public health. \"Cooking at home is **primarily** about control,\" she says. \"You know what goes into your food.\"\n\n**Nevertheless**, she understands that modern life is busy. She suggests **reasonable** solutions, such as cooking larger meals and freezing portions. She also encourages students to be **alert** to marketing claims on food packaging. **Frequent** **shopping** at local markets, she says, is better than one big trip to a supermarket.\n\nThe course has become very popular. A national **magazine** described it as \"the most delicious class in the **capital**.\" Some students say it changed their lives, giving them **consistent** habits and greater **efficiency** in the kitchen. Many say they no longer **endure** stomach problems after every meal. One student even decided to open a restaurant after the **birth** of her first child, inspired by the course."},"55":{"w":["chain","contact","decrease","exciting","grant","implication","interpretation","isolate","minority","politician","possibly","previously","regulate","resistance","roughly","routine","stuff","substitute","trap","acceptance"],"title":"Working from Home","t":"Working from home has moved from a rare privilege to a common **routine** for millions of people. **Previously**, most companies expected employees to work in offices. Now, **roughly** a third of workers in some countries spend at least part of the week at home.\n\nThe **implications|implication** are significant. Workers save time on commuting and may enjoy greater flexibility. Many find the change **exciting** at first. However, some feel **isolated|isolate** without daily **contact** with colleagues. Researchers note that loneliness can **decrease** motivation and productivity over time.\n\nCompanies have responded in different ways. Some **grant** employees full freedom to choose where they work; others **regulate** home working strictly. A few large **chain** stores have even tried to **substitute** online meetings for most business trips. Not every employee shows **acceptance** of these changes, and some unions have expressed **resistance**.\n\nExperts warn of a common **trap**: working longer hours because the office is always at home. They recommend clear boundaries, such as a separate desk and fixed working times. \"Put work **stuff** away at the end of the day,\" one psychologist advises.\n\nThe debate has political dimensions too. A **politician** in one city proposed tax benefits for companies that allow home working. Critics argue that **minority** groups and low-income workers may not have suitable space at home. Each **interpretation** of the data seems to support different conclusions. It is **possibly** too early to judge the long-term effects."},"56":{"w":["bottom","confuse","conservation","expression","foundation","gold","grateful","hall","heat","investigate","mobile","motor","operation","orient","precious","presence","reflection","retrieve","shrink","ad"],"title":"Saving the Coral Reefs","t":"Coral reefs are among the most **precious** ecosystems on Earth. They cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they support a quarter of all marine species. But rising sea temperatures are causing many reefs to **shrink**, and scientists warn that some could disappear within decades.\n\nResearchers have begun to **investigate** the causes in detail. Ocean **heat** waves cause corals to lose their colour, a process known as \"bleaching.\" Pollution from farms and factories adds further damage. Divers who **retrieve** samples from the **bottom** of the sea report that some reefs look like underwater deserts.\n\n**Conservation** groups are working to protect them. One **foundation** in Australia uses **mobile** laboratories to grow young corals and replant them on damaged reefs. The **operation** is slow and expensive, but early results are encouraging. Scientists describe the healthy new corals as \"more valuable than **gold**.\"\n\nPublic **presence** matters too. Many tourists visit reefs, but **motor** boats and careless divers can harm them. Some resorts now help visitors **orient** themselves with clear rules about where to swim. A recent **ad** campaign encouraged tourists to choose eco-friendly tour companies.\n\nThe crisis has prompted deep **reflection** among scientists. \"We are **grateful** for every reef that survives,\" one researcher said at a conference **hall** in Sydney. Her **expression** was serious. \"But we must not **confuse** small successes with a solution. The real answer is to cut carbon emissions.\""},"57":{"w":["congress","construction","convenience","desert","gesture","guide","log","press","seal","section","temptation","tone","accommodation","architecture","audio","clothing","engineering","forecast","medium","nuclear"],"title":"Building for the Future","t":"Modern **architecture** faces a difficult challenge: how to create buildings that are beautiful, comfortable and good for the environment. **Construction** accounts for a large share of global carbon emissions, and many experts believe the industry must change rapidly.\n\nEngineers are developing new solutions. Some cities debate whether **nuclear** power or renewable energy should supply new districts, and many new buildings already have solar panels on the roof. Others are designed to stay cool in hot **desert** climates without air conditioning. One firm has developed a building that can **seal** itself automatically when a storm is **forecast**, saving energy and protecting residents.\n\n**Engineering** is not the only issue. Architects must also think about **convenience** and comfort. Good design can **guide** people naturally through a building, with each **section** connected by bright corridors and quiet spaces. Many cities now require new **accommodation** to include green areas and shared gardens.\n\nSome governments are taking action. A national **congress** in Europe recently passed laws to reduce emissions from construction. The **press** has praised these laws, though some builders resist the higher costs. The **tone** of the debate has sometimes become heated.\n\nNot all solutions are high-tech. Traditional builders in some regions use **log** walls and local stone, and some even recycle old wool **clothing** as insulation. A small **gesture**, such as planting trees around a building, can make a big difference. Some designers even include **audio** guides explaining the building's features. The **temptation** to build quickly and cheaply is strong, but a **medium**-sized eco-friendly building can save money over its lifetime."},"58":{"w":["province","rat","roof","venture","athlete","compound","leather","musical","imply","disappear","praise","render","strike","apart","arouse","contrary","cooperate","influential","readily","refuse"],"title":"The Rise of a Young Athlete","t":"At eighteen, Li Na became the youngest **athlete** from her **province** to win a national running championship. Her victory surprised many, since she grew up in a poor village with no proper sports facilities. She trained on dirt roads, wearing old **leather** shoes, and practised under a leaking **roof** when it rained.\n\nHer coach **readily** admits that he did not expect such success. \"She was talented but not obviously special,\" he says. \"What set her **apart** was her determination.\" Li Na would **refuse** to give up, even when injuries made training painful.\n\nHer story has **aroused|arouse** great public interest. Newspapers have **praised|praise** her as a role model, and an **influential** sports company has offered her a sponsorship deal. **Contrary** to expectations, however, she has decided to stay with her local team for another year. \"I want to **cooperate** with the people who helped me,\" she explains.\n\nLi Na's success also **implies|imply** wider changes in Chinese sport. Rural athletes now receive more support, and new training centres are being built. Some critics warn that commercial pressure could **render** young athletes vulnerable, and a business **venture** in sport is not always good for the athletes themselves.\n\nAway from the track, Li Na loves **musical** theatre and sings in a local choir. She jokes that the only thing she fears is the old **rat** that lives in her training hall. When asked about the future, she says that success can **disappear** quickly. \"Every race is a new start,\" she says. \"Lightning can **strike** anyone.\" Her training combines running with strength exercises and a special drink, a **compound** of herbs her grandmother prepares."},"59":{"w":["steal","accumulate","announce","award","calm","director","disagree","drop","external","flexible","injury","invite","justify","locate","loud","movement","occupation","onto","partly","relation"],"title":"Making a Film","t":"Last year, a young **director** named Zhou Yan released her first feature film. It tells the story of a family whose savings are **stolen|steal** by a cheat, and how they rebuild their lives. The film won a national **award**, and critics praised its **calm**, honest style.\n\nMaking the film was not easy. Zhou spent three years writing the script and trying to **locate** funding. Many investors **disagreed|disagree** with her vision, preferring a more commercial story. She had to **justify** every decision, from casting to music. \"At times I wanted to **drop** the whole project,\" she admits.\n\nThe shooting process brought new challenges. One actor suffered an **injury** during a difficult scene, and production stopped for two weeks. **External** factors, such as bad weather, also caused delays. Zhou learned to be **flexible**, adjusting her plans almost daily.\n\nHer **occupation** demands patience and teamwork. Zhou **invited|invite** her actors to share ideas, and she encouraged a close **relation** between cast and crew. Over time, the team began to **accumulate** a strong sense of trust. She was **partly** inspired by her grandmother, a quiet woman who survived great hardship.\n\nWhen the film was finished, the producers **announced|announce** a national release. At the premiere, the audience gave a **loud** round of applause. Zhou stepped **onto** the stage, holding back tears. Her film has since sparked a **movement** among young filmmakers who want to tell honest stories about ordinary lives."},"60":{"w":["reverse","seriously","title","trick","unfortunately","urgent","weak","boast","bound","combination","comfortable","commitment","creature","crowd","custom","description","destruction","found","impose","industrial"],"title":"Customs That Connect Us","t":"Every culture has its own **customs|custom**, from wedding ceremonies to ways of greeting friends. These traditions may seem strange to outsiders, but they help communities feel connected. Anthropologists say that customs are **bound** up with identity and history.\n\nSome customs date back thousands of years. In many villages, people still gather in large **crowds|crowd** for spring festivals, sharing food and music. A **description** of one such festival in an ancient text shows that the tradition was **founded|found** more than a thousand years ago. Villagers **boast** that their celebrations are the oldest in the region. On the last day, they **reverse** traditional roles: children give each adult a funny **title**, and visitors often become the target of a harmless **trick**.\n\nYet customs are not fixed. **Industrial** development and the movement of people to cities have changed many traditions. **Unfortunately**, some customs have disappeared completely, and their **destruction** represents a cultural loss. Experts say that preserving them is **urgent**.\n\nSome governments **impose** rules to protect traditional culture, but critics warn that forcing people to follow old customs can backfire. A more effective approach, they argue, is to encourage a **combination** of tradition and modern life. Many young people now take customs **seriously**, joining traditional music groups or learning old crafts.\n\nSome customs involve animals. In one southern village, every child receives a small wooden **creature** carved by elders, believed to protect them from evil. Visitors are sometimes surprised by such beliefs, but locals find them **comfortable** and meaningful. Preserving traditions requires real **commitment**. As one elder said, \"If we forget our customs, we become **weak**. If we remember them, we become strong.\""},"61":{"w":["joy","lay","obviously","overseas","package","philosopher","stretch","substance","target","ultimate","undergo","accurate","arrangement","collective","entertainment","equipment","excitement","marriage","pollute","portion"],"title":"The Philosophy of Joy","t":"What brings us real **joy**? An ancient Greek **philosopher** once argued that happiness comes from wisdom and friendship, not from wealth. Modern psychologists are now testing this idea, and their findings suggest that his view was largely **accurate**.\n\nIn one study, researchers followed people who had just **undergone|undergo** major life changes, such as **marriage**, moving **overseas** or losing a job. They found that people's happiness returned to roughly the same level within a year, whatever had happened. **Obviously**, money and success matter, but their effect is smaller than we expect.\n\nWhat lasts longer is a sense of meaning. People who **lay** strong foundations of friendship and purpose report greater well-being. **Collective** activities, such as singing in a choir or volunteering, provide deep satisfaction. In contrast, buying the latest **equipment** or **entertainment** **package** gives only short-term **excitement**.\n\nThe **ultimate** **target** of life, many philosophers argue, is not pleasure but understanding. Yet modern society often encourages the opposite. Advertising promises happiness through products, and we **stretch** our budgets to buy them. Meanwhile, we **pollute** the planet to produce goods we do not truly need.\n\nExperts suggest simple **arrangements|arrangement** to increase daily joy: spend a **portion** of each day outdoors, share meals with friends and focus on things of real **substance**. As the philosopher put it more than two thousand years ago, \"The secret of happiness is not in seeking more, but in enjoying less.\""},"62":{"w":["applicant","conserve","faith","indoor","neutral","officer","tower","canteen","orchestra","outdoor","punishment","shelf","sex","unlikely","educate","opening","plus","seldom","attribute","cancer"],"title":"A Day at the Community Centre","t":"Every Saturday, the community centre in our neighbourhood opens its doors to people of all ages. Regardless of age or **sex**, everyone is welcome. The **opening** event this year was a concert by a local youth **orchestra**, and hundreds of people filled the hall.\n\nThe centre offers a wide range of activities. Children play **outdoor** games in the courtyard, while older residents join **indoor** classes in painting and dancing. The **canteen** serves cheap, healthy meals, and a small library has a **shelf** of books in several languages. Volunteers work hard to **educate** visitors about healthy living, and doctors give free talks on **cancer** prevention.\n\nThe centre also helps people find jobs. Each month, a careers **officer** meets with every **applicant** to discuss their skills. It is **unlikely** that a job seeker will leave without useful advice. Some young people who once faced **punishment** for minor crimes have found new opportunities here.\n\nThe centre's director, a retired teacher, says she **seldom** takes a day off. She has great **faith** in the power of community. \"People need a **neutral** place to meet,\" she explains, \"where nobody judges them.\" She **attributes|attribute** the centre's success to its volunteers.\n\nThe building itself is an old **tower** that once belonged to a factory. Volunteers worked hard to **conserve** its original walls. \"It is a symbol of our history,\" the director says, \"**plus** a reminder that old things can have new life.\""},"63":{"w":["circle","cope","criticize","derive","desirable","discourage","equal","expansion","frame","luck","newly","origin","pride","primary","revenue","stimulate","suitable","superior","systematic","tempt"],"title":"The Origin of Good Luck","t":"Many cultures believe in **luck**. People carry special objects, avoid certain numbers or wear red to bring good fortune. But where do these beliefs come from? Historians say their **origin** often lies in ancient religion and daily life.\n\nTake the colour red in China. It has long been seen as **desirable**, a sign of joy and success. During festivals, families hang red decorations to **stimulate** good fortune and protect against evil. Some scholars **derive** this tradition from ancient stories about a monster that feared the colour red.\n\nPsychologists offer another explanation. Belief in luck helps people **cope** with uncertainty. When the future is unclear, rituals provide comfort and a sense of control. They also strengthen social bonds: families feel **pride** in sharing traditions. Critics **criticize** such beliefs as unscientific, but many people find them meaningful.\n\nSome businesses use luck to increase **revenue**. Shops sell lucky charms, and some companies choose \"lucky\" dates for the **expansion** of their business. Advertisers **tempt** consumers with products said to bring good fortune. **Newly** married couples often choose lucky wedding dates.\n\nExperts say there is no **systematic** evidence that lucky charms work. Yet believing in luck does not have to be harmful, as long as it does not **discourage** hard work. A **primary** school teacher I know keeps a small lucky **frame** on her desk. Inside is a drawing of a **circle** of children holding hands. \"It reminds me that every child is **equal**,\" she says. She believes that effort is always **superior** to luck, but the picture helps her find **suitable** words when students feel down."},"64":{"w":["upper","aside","attraction","capture","climb","coach","complaint","cross","definition","deny","engineer","excellent","exclusively","executive","grocery","impression","interpret","invent","lab","lag"],"title":"Inside a Science Lab","t":"For many students, a visit to a real science **lab** is an exciting experience. Last month, our class toured the research centre of a large technology company. The **executive** who welcomed us explained that the company works **exclusively** on new medical devices.\n\nWe first met an **engineer** who had helped **invent** a small sensor that measures blood sugar. She showed us how it could **capture** data every minute and send it to a phone. Her **definition** of a good invention was simple: \"It makes life better for ordinary people.\"\n\nThen we visited the **upper** floors, where scientists tested new materials. One researcher had to **climb** a ladder to reach a machine near the ceiling. Another showed us how to **interpret** data from an experiment. \"Science often **lags|lag** behind our hopes,\" he admitted. \"Progress is slow.\"\n\nThe visit left a strong **impression** on us. Our teacher, who also works as a football **coach**, said it was an **excellent** chance to see science in action. Some students had expected boring work, but the lab was full of life. Even the staff canteen, which sold fruit from a local **grocery**, seemed like an **attraction**.\n\nAt the end, a young scientist answered our questions. When asked whether her job was stressful, she did not **deny** it. \"There are days when I want to put everything **aside**,\" she said. \"But when an experiment works, the feeling is amazing.\" She enjoys the small daily victories, too: every solved problem is one more she can **cross** off her list. Patients rarely make a **complaint**, she added, because every device is tested so carefully."},"65":{"w":["length","master","mature","movie","nut","occasion","painting","police","reject","repeatedly","sand","sink","slip","soil","surprising","whenever","worthy","zone","abstract","admit"],"title":"Painting the Seaside","t":"Every summer, a group of amateur artists gathers on a quiet beach to practise **painting**. Their teacher, a **master** painter in his seventies, believes that nature is the best classroom. \"Look at the **sand**, the sea and the sky,\" he tells students. \"Everything you need is here.\"\n\nStudents of every age and **length** of experience join the class. Some are **mature** professionals looking for relaxation; others are teenagers who dream of becoming artists. The teacher **admits|admit** that he once **rejected|reject** abstract art, but he now encourages students to try **abstract** styles too.\n\nPainting by the sea has its challenges. Wind can **slip** paper from an easel, and the rising tide sometimes forces students to move. Once, a student's easel began to **sink** into wet sand. The teacher laughed and helped her move to a safer **zone** higher up the beach.\n\nThe class has become a local tradition. On special **occasions|occasion**, the artists display their work in the village hall, and local **police** officers help manage the crowds. **Whenever** a painting is sold, the money goes to a charity that protects the coastline and its fragile **soil**.\n\nThe results are often **surprising**. Students who **repeatedly** struggled with technique suddenly produce beautiful work. One woman painted a simple bowl of **nuts|nut** that won a regional prize. \"Every painting is **worthy** of attention,\" the teacher says, \"even if it isn't perfect.\" Some students later turned their paintings into a short **movie** about the class."},"66":{"w":["aggressive","automatic","basically","comprehension","consciousness","cue","dirt","fatigue","fertilizer","flat","headline","minimum","obstacle","ownership","port","reputation","security","slightly","teenager","weekly"],"title":"Why Teenagers Need More Sleep","t":"Every parent knows the scene: a **teenager** who cannot get out of bed in the morning. Many adults assume that young people are simply lazy, but scientists say the problem is **basically** biological. During adolescence, the body's internal clock shifts, making teenagers feel sleepy later at night.\n\nResearch shows that lack of sleep has serious consequences. Tired students show lower reading **comprehension**, and their **consciousness** of risks decreases. Chronic **fatigue** can also make teenagers more **aggressive** and anxious. Even **slightly** shorter sleep, repeated over weeks, affects school performance.\n\nSome schools have responded by starting classes later. The results have been encouraging: attendance has risen, and students say they feel more alert. A **headline** in a national newspaper called the change \"the simplest reform in education.\" However, schools face **obstacles|obstacle**, such as bus schedules and parents' working hours.\n\nExperts offer practical advice. Teenagers should keep phones out of the bedroom, since screen light gives the brain a false **cue** that it is still daytime. They should also follow a **weekly** routine, going to bed at roughly the same time. Doctors recommend a **minimum** of eight hours' sleep a night.\n\nParents can help, too. Some families create a \"sleep zone\" with no screens, and others establish rules about computer **ownership** in bedrooms. Sleep is not a luxury; it is a basic need, just like food or **security**. A good night's rest can protect a student's health and **reputation** at school. As one doctor put it, \"Sleep is not **automatic** for teenagers; it must be protected.\"\n\nSleep problems are not limited to teenagers, of course. Workers at a busy **port** who change shifts every week often feel exhausted, and families in a small **flat** beside a noisy road also sleep badly. In the countryside, farmers who rise before dawn to spread **fertilizer** face similar challenges. Doctors even advise cleaning bedrooms regularly, since **dirt** and dust can disturb breathing at night."},"67":{"w":["ban","essay","evolution","humor","lion","republican","roman","temporary","timber","tribe","tuition","wit","forgive","marine","output","pocket","possess","bat","carrot","exit"],"title":"Life in Ancient Rome","t":"The **Roman** Empire was one of the most powerful civilisations in history. At its height, it ruled territories from Britain to North Africa. Historians continue to study how Romans lived, worked and entertained themselves.\n\nRoman cities were remarkably advanced. They had public baths, roads and markets, and many buildings were made of stone and **timber**. Wealthy families **possessed|possess** large houses with gardens, while poorer citizens lived in crowded apartments. Some laws even placed a **ban** on building too high, for safety reasons. Archaeologists exploring tunnels beneath the city have found everything from coins to the bones of a **bat**.\n\nEntertainment played a central role in Roman life. Crowds gathered to watch chariot races and fights involving wild animals such as the **lion**, a cruelty that modern visitors find hard to **forgive**. Writers produced plays full of **humor** and **wit**, and poets wrote about love and politics. Students paid **tuition** to study with famous teachers, and many wrote an **essay** on philosophy or history.\n\nThe Romans traded across the Mediterranean, importing **marine** products such as fish sauce. The **output** of Roman farms was enormous, and simple vegetables like the **carrot** were common in daily meals. Traders carried coins in a leather **pocket** tied to their belts.\n\nRoman society was not a democracy in the modern sense, though early Rome was a **republican** state ruled by elected officials. Over time, it faced conflicts with every neighbouring **tribe** and many **temporary** alliances. Its **evolution** from republic to empire remains a major topic of study. Visitors to Rome today can still walk through ancient streets, **exit** through old city gates and imagine a world that, in many ways, shaped our own."},"68":{"w":["fridge","tag","van","concentration","hang","somewhat","breed","disadvantage","dislike","exhibit","folk","hardship","qualify","sake","sentence","shortage","software","utilize","altogether","approximately"],"title":"Running a Family Farm Today","t":"My cousin runs a small dairy farm in the north. He **breeds|breed** cows, grows vegetables and sells cheese at local markets. Life on the farm is **somewhat** romantic in films, but in reality it involves great **hardship**.\n\nThe biggest **disadvantage** of small farming is the unpredictable income. Prices change every month, and a **shortage** of rain can ruin a harvest. My cousin works **approximately** fourteen hours a day, **altogether** about a hundred hours a week. \"You do it for the **sake** of the land and your family,\" he says. \"Not for money.\"\n\nTechnology has helped. He uses **software** to track milk production and **utilize** resources more efficiently. Each cow wears an electronic **tag** that records its health. He keeps fresh cheese in a large **fridge** and delivers it in an old white **van**. These tools require **concentration** and training, and he had to **qualify** for a government programme to afford them.\n\nSome traditions remain. In the barn, he likes to **hang** old farming tools on the walls, and every autumn the village holds a **folk** festival to celebrate the harvest. Last year, his cheese was chosen to **exhibit** at a regional food fair.\n\nHe admits that he **dislikes|dislike** some parts of the job, especially paperwork. \"One **sentence** in a government form can take me an hour to understand,\" he jokes. But he would not choose any other life."},"69":{"w":["assure","chief","circumstance","conscious","contemporary","curious","decorate","delicate","distribution","ending","endless","escape","eventually","exclude","height","leap","lucky","mission","misunderstand","ought"],"title":"A Curious Mind","t":"Albert Einstein once said, \"I have no special talents. I am only passionately **curious**.\" Many scientists agree that curiosity is the **chief** source of discovery. Children are naturally curious, asking endless questions about the world. But as they grow older, many lose this habit. Studies of the **distribution** of curiosity across age groups suggest that it reaches its **height** in early childhood.\n\nResearchers have tried to understand why. One explanation is that schools often focus on correct answers rather than questions. Students learn to **exclude** wild ideas and follow rules. Under such **circumstances|circumstance**, curiosity may slowly disappear.\n\n**Contemporary** educators are trying to change this. Some teachers encourage students to ask **endless** questions, even if they have no answers. Others take students on field trips to observe nature. \"We **ought** to reward curiosity, not just correct answers,\" one teacher argues. She believes that a **conscious** effort can keep curiosity alive.\n\nCuriosity also matters beyond school. It helps adults **escape** boredom and adapt to change. Workers who are curious tend to learn new skills more quickly and **eventually** perform better. Some companies even **assure** employees that asking questions will not be punished.\n\nYet curiosity needs a **delicate** balance. Too much uncertainty can cause stress, and people may **misunderstand** each other's motives. Scientists say we should **decorate** our lives with small adventures: reading new books, visiting new places or learning a new language. As one researcher put it, \"A curious mind makes a **leap** of imagination that no computer can copy.\" Those who stay curious, she adds, are the **lucky** ones: they care less about a story's **ending** than about the questions it raises, and they see learning as a lifelong **mission**."},"70":{"w":["outlet","pose","promising","quantity","religion","religious","resolution","resort","rob","secure","setting","tap","touch","transfer","union","via","volunteer","weigh","accompany","acre"],"title":"A Weekend at the Mountain Resort","t":"Last month, my family spent a weekend at a mountain **resort** about three hours from the city. We travelled **via** a winding road through forests and farmland, passing small villages and fields of several **acres|acre**. The **setting** was beautiful, with lakes, waterfalls and old temples.\n\nThe resort is run by a local farmers' **union**, which uses the profits to support the village. Many staff are **volunteers|volunteer** from nearby towns. They **accompany** guests on walks and explain local customs. One guide told us about the area's **religious** history; several temples belong to a **religion** that has been practised there for centuries.\n\nThe resort is careful about safety. Guests' belongings are kept in **secure** lockers, and the staff warned us that thieves had once tried to **rob** a tourist on a mountain path. A small shop is an **outlet** for local crafts and honey, sold in a surprising **quantity** to visitors.\n\nOn the second day, we tried fishing. The guide showed us how to **tap** the water gently to attract fish, and how to **weigh** our catch on an old scale. My little brother was thrilled to catch a fish, though he refused to **touch** it. We also met a photographer who asked us to **pose** for pictures beside the lake.\n\nThe resort is a **promising** example of rural tourism. Its managers have made a **resolution** to protect the environment, limiting the number of guests and banning plastic bottles. Before we left, they helped us **transfer** our photos onto a USB stick as a souvenir. We plan to return next year."},"71":{"w":["anywhere","appliance","boss","branch","civil","density","disguise","exaggerate","fancy","grain","harvest","hunt","intense","limitation","lonely","merely","nearby","offend","outstanding","pain"],"title":"Harvest Time in the Village","t":"Every autumn, my grandparents' village celebrates the **harvest** with a week of festivals. Farmers bring in their **grain**, children help pick fruit and families gather to share food. For a few days, even the most **lonely** elderly villagers have company.\n\nMy grandfather loves this season. He tells stories about his youth, when villagers would **hunt** wild birds and work in the fields from dawn to dusk. Life was hard, and many people lived in constant **pain** from heavy labour. \"Young people today have no idea how difficult it was,\" he says, though my grandmother warns him not to **exaggerate**.\n\nThe village has changed a lot. Many families now own modern kitchen **appliances|appliance**, and some have opened guesthouses for tourists. A bank has opened a small **branch** in the main street, and a **nearby** town has built a new hospital. Still, life here has its **limitations|limitation**: young people must travel far to find good jobs.\n\nTourism has brought new challenges. Some visitors arrive in **fancy** cars and behave rudely, which can **offend** local people. Others try to **disguise** commercial activities as cultural events. The village committee works hard to keep tourism respectful and **civil**.\n\nDespite these problems, the harvest festival remains **outstanding**. The **intense** colours of autumn, the smell of fresh bread and the sound of folk music create an unforgettable atmosphere. It is not **merely** a celebration of food but of community. Even the village **boss**, a strict woman who manages the cooperative, dances with everyone. Experts note that the population **density** here is low, but the sense of belonging is strong. \"You won't find this **anywhere** else,\" my grandfather says proudly."},"72":{"w":["photograph","profitable","restore","restrict","skin","so-called","solid","specialize","strategic","thick","translate","wood","academy","actress","command","delivery","depress","district","draft","edge"],"title":"The Actress Who Became a Teacher","t":"Ten years ago, Lin Fang was a successful **actress**, appearing in films and on stage across the country. Then she surprised everyone by leaving her career to found a drama **academy** for children in a poor **district** of the city.\n\nThe decision was not **profitable**. The academy charges low fees, and Lin often uses her own savings to keep it running. Yet she believes her work has a deeper value. \"Drama helps children build **solid** confidence,\" she says. \"It teaches them to **command** attention and express themselves.\"\n\nHer teaching methods are unusual. Students begin each class by standing at the **edge** of the stage, describing what they see and feel. They **translate** their emotions into movement and voice. Lin often asks them to write a **draft** of their own short plays, which they later perform for parents. She keeps a **thick** notebook of their best ideas and reminds them that good acting is more than **skin** deep. Whenever a **delivery** of donated costumes arrives, the children rush to try them on.\n\nSome parents were doubtful at first. They worried that drama would **restrict** time for \"serious\" subjects. But many soon noticed improvements in their children's reading and speaking. One mother said that her **so-called** shy son had become talkative and happy.\n\nLin also wants to **restore** old theatres in the city. She has started a **strategic** partnership with local businesses, which help pay for repairs. In one theatre, workers replaced the rotten **wood** floor and repainted the walls. A local newspaper published a **photograph** of the reopening ceremony. \"Life on stage can **depress** you if you only chase fame,\" Lin says. \"Here, I see real joy.\" She now plans to **specialize** in teaching drama to children with disabilities."},"73":{"w":["export","king","molecule","plot","silence","spoil","technical","theatre","ton","welfare","council","jam","seed","variable","accent","runner","comprehensive","instantly","pace","pave"],"title":"A Night at the Theatre","t":"Last weekend, I went to the city **theatre** to see a new play about a **king** who loses his kingdom. The **plot** was simple, but the acting was powerful. In the final scene, the stage fell into complete **silence** as the king stood alone, holding a handful of **seeds|seed**.\n\nThe play's director explained afterwards that the seeds were a symbol of hope. \"Even after everything is lost, something can grow again,\" she said. Her **comprehensive** research into history and philosophy had shaped the story. She compared the kingdom to a **molecule**: break one bond, and the whole structure changes. Several actors spoke with a strong **accent** from the north, which made the play feel authentic.\n\nThe production was a **technical** achievement. Lights changed **instantly** to show the passage of time, and the stage could be raised or lowered by several **tons|ton** of machinery. The **pace** of the play was fast, and the lead actor, a former marathon **runner**, needed all his energy to keep the audience engaged for two hours.\n\nThe theatre has a long history. It was built by the city **council** in the 1950s to promote culture and public **welfare**. In recent years, it has struggled financially, and some feared it might close. But new funding has helped to **pave** the way for a revival.\n\nNot everyone enjoyed the play. One critic complained that too many special effects might **spoil** the story. Others felt that the ending was too **variable**, changing from night to night. Still, tickets sold quickly, and a traffic **jam** formed outside the theatre before every performance. The producers now hope to **export** the play to theatres abroad."},"74":{"w":["reaction","slowly","surely","wonderful","adequate","beach","comparable","confirm","construct","deliberate","distinct","exception","fade","fruitful","insufficient","integrate","investigation","joint","laboratory","latter"],"title":"A Day at the Beach Laboratory","t":"On a quiet **beach** in the south, scientists run a small marine **laboratory** that studies coastal pollution. Visitors are welcome, and last summer I spent a day there. It turned out to be a **wonderful** and surprising experience.\n\nThe lab's director explained that their **investigation** focuses on plastic waste. Every morning, researchers walk **slowly** along the shore, collecting samples. They test the water to **confirm** whether chemicals are present. The results are worrying: pollution levels are rising, and current protections are **insufficient**.\n\nThe team is working on solutions. Scientists have helped to **construct** floating barriers that collect rubbish at river mouths. They also **integrate** data from satellites and local fishermen to track pollution. This **joint** effort has been **fruitful**, reducing waste on some beaches by half.\n\nThe work requires **deliberate** planning. Each project must be **comparable** with international standards so that results can be shared. Researchers must also show that their methods are **adequate** for long-term monitoring. There is no **exception**: every sample is checked twice.\n\nThe director pointed out two **distinct** problems: plastic from cities and fertiliser from farms. The **latter** causes harmful algae to grow, which can kill fish. Public **reaction** has been strong, and many local residents now volunteer at beach clean-ups. \"Hope does not **fade** easily here,\" she said. \"We will **surely** win this fight if we work together.\""},"75":{"w":["load","lock","originate","preparation","prize","relative","relax","sensible","sight","southeast","span","stable","uncomfortable","absorb","accuracy","attain","broken","certainty","companion","component"],"title":"The Long Walk Home","t":"Last spring, my friend Chen walked from Beijing to his hometown in the **southeast**, a journey that would **span** two months. His **preparation** took half a year. He studied maps, trained his body and packed a light **load** of clothes and food. \"I wanted to **attain** something difficult,\" he said. Before leaving, he put a heavy **lock** on his flat and gave the key to a neighbour.\n\nThe journey was not always comfortable. On some days, his feet hurt so badly that every step felt **uncomfortable**. Once, a **broken** shoe forced him to stop in a small town. He learned to **relax** and accept setbacks rather than fight them. A **sensible** approach, he discovered, was to walk slowly and rest often.\n\nChen met many people along the way. A farmer invited him to stay for dinner, and an old woman told him stories about her youth. He found a loyal **companion** in a stray dog that followed him for a week. \"Travel teaches you that kindness is a key **component** of life,\" he says.\n\nWalking also changed how he saw the world. He began to **absorb** details he had never noticed: the **sight** of mountains at sunrise, the smell of rain, the sound of birds. He kept a diary, writing with great **accuracy** about each day. Some of his ideas **originate** from these quiet moments.\n\nWhen he finally arrived home, his family was overjoyed. His mother, a **relative** told me, cried for an hour. Chen's story even won a **prize** in a travel writing competition. He says the journey gave him **certainty** about what matters most: family, health and a **stable** sense of purpose."},"76":{"w":["costly","discount","discrimination","dismiss","distant","emphasis","enthusiasm","equip","extraordinary","forever","former","frequency","guard","guidance","halt","hate","illusion","incline","logic","magic"],"title":"The Magic of a Good Teacher","t":"Many people remember a teacher who changed their lives. For me, it was Ms. Zhou, my **former** maths teacher. She had an **extraordinary** ability to make **logic** feel like **magic**. Students who once **hated|hate** maths left her classes full of **enthusiasm**.\n\nHer secret was simple: she placed **emphasis** on understanding rather than memorising. When a student made a mistake, she never **dismissed|dismiss** it as foolish. Instead, she offered gentle **guidance** and asked questions until the student found the answer. \"Mistakes are not failures,\" she said. \"They are steps.\"\n\nMs. Zhou also fought against **discrimination** in the classroom. In our school, some teachers believed that girls were less **inclined|incline** to study science. She refused to accept this **illusion** and encouraged every student equally. Several of her female students later became engineers.\n\nShe worked hard to **equip** us with practical skills. Using a small telescope, she showed us how the **frequency** of light waves explains colour. She took us on trips to **distant** villages to measure rivers and hills. Such activities were **costly**, so she persuaded local shops to offer a **discount** on equipment.\n\nWhen Ms. Zhou retired, the whole school came to say goodbye. A former student, now a security **guard** at a museum, gave a short speech. \"She taught us that learning should never **halt**,\" he said. \"We will remember her **forever**.\""},"77":{"w":["mere","ocean","organism","politics","practically","release","secret","settle","shame","shoulder","signature","sphere","steep","unusual","usual","variation","virus","vote","wipe","wire"],"title":"Life in the Deep Ocean","t":"The deep **ocean** is one of the least explored places on Earth. It covers most of our planet, yet scientists know **practically** nothing about many of its creatures. Each new expedition reveals an **unusual** **organism**, from glowing fish to giant worms.\n\nLife in the deep sea is extreme. There is no sunlight, the water is freezing and the pressure is enormous. Yet animals have found ways to **settle** there. Some produce their own light; others survive on chemicals released from hot springs on the ocean floor. Scientists study the **variation** between species to understand how life adapts.\n\nExploring the deep ocean is far from **usual** scientific work. Researchers use robots connected to ships by a long **wire**, and each dive can take hours. On one trip, a robot descended a **steep** underwater cliff and filmed a creature no one had seen before. The scientists named it after the ship's captain, who added his **signature** to the official report.\n\nThe ocean also faces serious threats. Pollution, overfishing and climate change put many species at risk. A **mere** rise of two degrees in water temperature could **wipe** out entire ecosystems. Some scientists have even discovered a **virus** that spreads among deep-sea fish as waters warm.\n\nProtecting the ocean is a matter of **politics** as well as science. International organisations **vote** on fishing limits, and governments **release** reports on marine health. Experts say every country must **shoulder** responsibility. \"It would be a **shame** to destroy something we have barely begun to understand,\" one researcher says. Her **secret** hope is that the deep sea becomes a protected **sphere** of science, not exploitation."},"78":{"w":["acknowledge","ambition","approval","camp","cattle","chocolate","conference","conventional","deadly","disaster","disclose","economics","experimental","fate","formula","hole","import","leisure","navigation","outer"],"title":"The Economics of Chocolate","t":"Few foods are as popular as **chocolate**. People around the world enjoy it as a sweet treat or a gift. But behind every chocolate bar lies a complex story of **economics**, trade and the **fate** of millions of farmers.\n\nMost cocoa is grown in West Africa, where small farmers sell their beans at low prices. Large companies **import** the beans, process them and sell chocolate at high profit. Experts say this system is **conventional** but unfair. At a recent international **conference**, speakers called for higher payments to farmers.\n\nSome companies have begun to **acknowledge** the problem. They now **disclose** where their cocoa comes from and pay extra for fair-trade beans. Their **ambition** is to make chocolate both delicious and ethical. Consumers have shown **approval**, and fair-trade chocolate sales are rising.\n\nClimate change adds new risks. Droughts and floods can be **deadly** for cocoa trees, and a single bad season can be a **disaster** for farmers. Scientists are testing an **experimental** **formula** for fertiliser that helps trees resist drought. Others are using satellite **navigation** to track changes in rainfall.\n\nCocoa farming also competes with **cattle** ranching for land, and forests are often cleared, leaving a **hole** in the ecosystem. Some farmers have joined training **camps|camp** where they learn sustainable methods. For consumers, chocolate is a **leisure** product, but for farmers it is a matter of survival. Next time you break open the **outer** wrapper of a chocolate bar, it is worth thinking about its journey."},"79":{"w":["penny","pile","protein","publication","recreation","retire","shadow","similarly","strain","theoretical","upward","broadcast","fur","legislation","nationality","observer","poll","racial","recovery","telescope"],"title":"Looking at the Stars","t":"For thousands of years, people have looked at the night sky and wondered what lies beyond. Today, powerful telescopes allow us to see distant galaxies, but for many amateur **observers|observer**, a small **telescope** in the backyard is enough to bring great joy.\n\nMy neighbour, a **retired|retire** engineer, spends his evenings studying the stars. He began after a long illness, and he says that astronomy played a big part in his **recovery**. \"When you look **upward**, your problems seem small,\" he says. He keeps a **pile** of star maps beside his window.\n\nAstronomy is not just a **recreation**; it also contributes to science. Amateur astronomers have discovered comets and helped professionals confirm **theoretical** predictions. Their observations are often included in scientific **publications|publication**. **Similarly**, citizen science projects allow anyone to help classify galaxies online.\n\nSadly, light pollution makes stargazing harder in cities. Bright streetlights cast a **shadow** over the night sky, hiding all but the brightest stars. Some countries have passed **legislation** to reduce light pollution, and a recent opinion **poll** showed strong public support.\n\nAstronomy also brings people together, regardless of **nationality** or **racial** background. Many astronomy clubs welcome members from every culture. Last month, a local radio station began to **broadcast** a weekly programme about the stars. My neighbour says the hobby costs hardly a **penny** beyond the cost of equipment. On cold nights he wears a hat lined with **fur**, and he eats **protein** bars to stay warm. Despite the **strain** on his eyes, he never misses a clear night."},"80":{"w":["transparent","universe","vessel","wing","airline","drill","generator","hamburger","rag","stone","accustomed","anyway","architect","automatically","bureau","classify","clever","disappoint","duty","float"],"title":"Inside an Airport","t":"A modern airport is like a small city. Thousands of people pass through it every day, and hundreds of staff work behind the scenes. Last month, I visited a major airport to learn how it operates, guided by an **architect** who helped design it.\n\nThe terminal is built of glass and **stone**, with a **transparent** roof that lets in natural light. The architect explained that the design was meant to make passengers feel calm. \"Flying makes many people nervous,\" she said. \"We wanted the building to **float** like a cloud.\"\n\nWe visited areas that passengers rarely see. In one room, staff **classify** luggage using machines that work **automatically**, sorting bags by destination. In another, workers maintain a backup **generator** that supplies power in emergencies. Every week, staff take part in a fire **drill** to practise safety procedures.\n\nThe airport also houses offices for each **airline** and a customs **bureau**, where officers check imported goods and collect **duty**. Some passengers are **disappointed|disappoint** to discover how much tax they must pay. The officers are **accustomed** to complaints, though. \"It's our job,\" one said. \"**Anyway**, most people are polite.\"\n\nNear the end of the tour, we stopped at a café where travellers ate **hamburgers|hamburger** and coffee. A cleaner wiped tables with a **rag**, moving quickly between customers. Outside, a plane spread its **wings|wing** on the runway, ready for take-off. The architect smiled. \"Every airport is a **vessel** of dreams,\" she said. \"It connects people across the whole **universe** of human experience.\" It was a **clever** way to end the tour."},"81":{"w":["illustration","independence","infer","proof","proud","resemble","steadily","thoughtful","tremendous","typical","useless","victim","virtually","whereas","worst","accommodate","adhere","anger","arrange","assign"],"title":"Growing Up and Moving Out","t":"For many young people, leaving home is a symbol of **independence**. It is a **typical** step after university, though the timing varies greatly between cultures. In some countries, young adults move out at eighteen, **whereas** in others they live with their parents until marriage.\n\nResearchers have tried to understand the effects of this transition. One study found that young people who live alone improve their life skills **steadily**: they learn to cook, manage money and **arrange** their own schedules. Many say they feel **proud** of these achievements. But others feel lonely and anxious, and some describe their first year away from home as the **worst** of their lives.\n\nParents also experience **tremendous** change. Some feel sad or even experience **anger** when their children leave. Others welcome the new freedom. Psychologists say it is important for families to stay connected, perhaps through regular calls or **thoughtful** messages.\n\nHousing costs make independence difficult. In many cities, rents are so high that small flats can barely **accommodate** one person. Young workers become **victims|victim** of rising prices and may feel that their efforts are **useless**. Experts **infer** from the data that many young people now **virtually** depend on their parents for years after graduation.\n\nStill, independence is worth pursuing. It teaches young people to **adhere** to their own values and **assign** priorities wisely. As an **illustration**, one young woman described how living alone helped her discover a love of cooking. \"My kitchen **resembles|resemble** a science lab,\" she laughed, \"but every meal is **proof** that I can look after myself.\""},"82":{"w":["blind","burden","characterize","comfort","commonly","composition","compromise","confine","counter","declare","exploit","fabric","favorable","guarantee","ignorant","inexpensive","inquiry","instinct","latin","loyalty"],"title":"The Story of Fabric","t":"Clothing has been part of human life for thousands of years, and the history of **fabric** tells us much about culture and trade. The earliest fabrics were made from animal skins and plant fibres, but over time people learned to weave cotton, wool and silk.\n\nSilk is a famous example. For centuries, China guarded the secret of silk production, and merchants carried silk across the ancient trade routes. In Europe, wealthy families paid huge sums for it, and **Latin** texts describe it as a luxury of the gods. Its value was so high that some rulers **declared|declare** laws restricting who could wear it.\n\nThe modern textile industry is very different. Many clothes are now **inexpensive**, produced quickly in large factories. But critics argue that this system can **exploit** workers, who often face low pay and long hours. An **inquiry** by a human rights group found that some factories **confine** workers for twelve hours a day. Many consumers remain **ignorant** of these conditions.\n\nSome brands have begun to change. They **guarantee** fair wages and use sustainable materials. Others **characterize** themselves as \"slow fashion,\" producing fewer but better clothes. Finding a **compromise** between low prices and fair treatment is a heavy **burden** for the industry.\n\nConsumers also play a role. Many people buy clothes based on **instinct** or emotion rather than need. Experts suggest choosing quality over quantity and showing **loyalty** to ethical brands. **Commonly**, the most **favorable** choice is to repair old clothes rather than buy new ones. Some shops even offer a repair **counter**, where tailors fix clothes for free. As one designer says, \"Fashion should bring **comfort**, not guilt.\" The **composition** of our wardrobes reveals our values, and we should not be **blind** to its impact."},"83":{"w":["manual","miracle","mystery","noisy","obligation","odd","opponent","pack","permanent","poem","prompt","request","rid","rigid","rock","royal","sample","secondary","seize","selection"],"title":"The Mystery of the Royal Rock","t":"In the hills of northern Scotland stands a huge **rock** that local people call the \"**Royal** Stone.\" According to legend, ancient kings were crowned there. For centuries, its origin has remained a **mystery**, but recent scientific research has begun to reveal its secrets.\n\nScientists collected a **sample** of the stone and found that it came from a region hundreds of kilometres away. How did ancient people move such a heavy object? Some believe it was a **miracle**; others suggest that people used **manual** labour, wooden rollers and great patience. The research team proposed a third theory: the rock was carried by glaciers during the Ice Age.\n\nNot everyone accepted the findings. An **opponent** of the theory, a local historian, argued that the evidence was too **rigid** and ignored local traditions. He felt an **obligation** to defend the legend. The debate became so **noisy** that the local council received a **request** for a public meeting.\n\nThe rock attracts many visitors, especially in summer. Tourists **pack** the narrow roads, and local businesses are **prompt** to sell souvenirs. Some visitors leave **odd** objects at the site, such as coins and flowers. Authorities have tried to **rid** the area of litter, and some have threatened to **seize** items left illegally.\n\nThe council now plans to give the rock **permanent** protection. A poet who grew up nearby wrote a famous **poem** about it, describing it as \"the silent king of the hills.\" For locals, its history is of **secondary** importance; what matters is its place in their hearts. A **selection** of photos and stories is now displayed in the village museum."},"84":{"w":["singer","smooth","southern","specialist","stake","surgery","trace","uncover","unfair","unfortunate","unknown","utility","well-known","widen","altitude","artistic","battery","boundary","cancel","cast"],"title":"A Surgeon Who Sings","t":"Dr. Chen is a **well-known** heart **specialist** at a hospital in the **southern** city of Guangzhou. He has performed thousands of operations, but his other talent remained largely **unknown** until a video of him went viral: he is also a gifted **singer**.\n\nDr. Chen began singing as a child. His parents encouraged his **artistic** side, but they also wanted him to become a doctor. He chose medicine, but he never gave up music. Today, he sings in a local choir and performs at charity concerts. \"Music and **surgery** are similar,\" he says. \"Both need **smooth**, careful movements and deep concentration.\"\n\nHis work at the hospital is demanding. He often has to **cancel** plans because of emergencies. Once, during a long operation, the hospital's power failed, and doctors relied on a backup **battery**. Dr. Chen stayed calm, finishing the procedure safely. Such moments remind him that lives are at **stake** every day.\n\nHe also campaigns for fairer health care. He believes it is **unfair** that people in remote areas lack access to good hospitals. He has helped to **widen** a programme that sends doctors to mountain villages at high **altitude**. \"It is **unfortunate** that geography decides who gets treatment,\" he says. \"We must **uncover** the reasons and fix them.\"\n\nDr. Chen sees no **boundary** between his two passions. A local film director plans to **cast** him in a documentary about doctors who make music. Asked about the **utility** of singing for a surgeon, he laughs. \"It keeps my heart healthy,\" he says. \"And I can **trace** every happy memory to a song.\""},"85":{"w":["commission","committee","confusion","criticism","drain","ease","elsewhere","extensive","fame","favorite","formal","generous","genuine","glance","hostile","intensity","intention","kilogram","ladder","mathematical"],"title":"A Ladder to Fame","t":"Many young people dream of **fame**. Social media has made it seem easy: post the right video, and millions may watch it. But experts warn that the path to success is rarely smooth, and fame often brings **confusion** and stress.\n\nConsider the story of a young **mathematical** genius who became famous online for solving puzzles. At first, he enjoyed the attention. Fans praised his **genuine** passion and **generous** spirit, as he often taught others for free. But soon, **criticism** followed. Some users became **hostile**, posting unkind comments. \"I didn't expect such **intensity**,\" he said.\n\nHis experience reflects a wider problem. A government **commission** recently studied the effects of online fame on young people. Its report, presented to a parliamentary **committee**, found that many influencers feel pressure to perform constantly. The **extensive** study recommended better education about online safety.\n\nPsychologists say the key is to stay grounded. \"Fame can **drain** your energy,\" one expert warns. \"Remember why you started.\" She advises young people to build a career step by step, like climbing a **ladder**, rather than chasing sudden success. Many people are surprised to learn that a **formal** education still matters, even for online stars.\n\nThe young genius has learned to manage the attention with greater **ease**. He now spends less time online and more time studying and exercising; he has lost several **kilograms|kilogram** and sleeps much better. His **favorite** activity is teaching children in his hometown. His **intention** is to become a university professor. When asked about his future, he gives a quick **glance** at his notebook and smiles. \"There are more problems to solve **elsewhere**,\" he says."},"86":{"w":["measurement","mislead","moderate","modest","motive","neighborhood","notion","organic","paint","patience","payment","peninsula","pet","plain","plantation","prominent","reference","relate","reserve","romantic"],"title":"A Quiet Neighborhood","t":"Ten years ago, my parents moved to a small **neighborhood** on a **peninsula** in southern China. It is not **prominent** on any map, but for them it is the perfect place to retire. Life there moves at a **moderate** pace, and the people are friendly and **modest**.\n\nMy mother loves gardening. She grows **organic** vegetables in a small garden that was once part of an old tea **plantation**. She has endless **patience** with her plants, checking the soil every morning. Her **motive**, she says, is simple: \"Food tastes better when you grow it yourself.\"\n\nMy father spends his time differently. He likes to **paint** landscapes of the sea and sells his work at a local market. He accepts **payment** in cash or vegetables, depending on the buyer. His paintings are **plain** but beautiful, and neighbours often hang them in their homes.\n\nThe community has its own traditions. Every family keeps a **pet**, usually a cat or a dog, and children walk the animals along the coast. Local people **relate** stories about storms and fishermen, and an old library has a **reference** section full of local history books. The community centre **reserves|reserve** one evening a week for music and dance.\n\nSome outsiders hold the **notion** that such places are boring. My parents disagree. \"Don't let fast city life **mislead** you,\" my father says. \"Happiness can't be measured by any **measurement** of wealth.\" My mother finds their life deeply **romantic**: she and my father still walk on the beach every evening at sunset."},"87":{"w":["rough","salt","satellite","scatter","session","sharply","shy","spark","sunlight","tank","tip","viewpoint","voyage","wander","youth","blaze","cash","crystal","detection","inner"],"title":"Sailing Around the World","t":"Last year, my friend Lin completed a **voyage** around the world in a small sailing boat. The journey took eleven months and covered more than forty thousand kilometres. As a **shy** child, she had never imagined such an adventure. \"Travel changed my **viewpoint** on life,\" she says.\n\nThe trip was not easy. Seas were often **rough**, and storms sometimes forced her to change course. She relied on a **satellite** phone to receive weather reports and talk to her family. Her fresh water came from a **tank** that she refilled at each port. During long nights, she would watch the stars and let her thoughts **wander**.\n\nLin learned many lessons along the way. A fisherman in Indonesia gave her a useful **tip**: always carry extra **salt** and dried food in case of emergencies. In Africa, she watched local guides **scatter** seeds on farmland after the rainy season. She kept her **cash** in different places on the boat to reduce risk.\n\nThere were frightening moments too. Once, a fire broke out in the kitchen, and a small **blaze** spread **sharply** before she put it out. Another time, early **detection** of a leak saved her from disaster. \"The ocean doesn't forgive mistakes,\" she says.\n\nLin has now returned home and gives talks about her journey. In every **session**, she describes the **crystal** blue water of the Pacific and the warm **sunlight** of the Indian Ocean. Her stories **spark** curiosity in young listeners. \"My **youth** taught me fear,\" she tells them. \"The sea taught me courage, and my **inner** strength grew with every wave.\""},"88":{"w":["instrument","invention","lunar","mercury","minister","monument","scare","scratch","sew","solar","spin","toss","vitamin","wooden","beam","berry","collapse","competent","owl","refrigerator"],"title":"A Night at the Science Museum","t":"Last weekend, our city science museum held a special \"night of discovery\" for families. Visitors could explore the exhibits after dark, using torches to find hidden displays. It was one of the most exciting events of the year.\n\nThe museum began with a section on space. Children looked through an old **instrument** used by astronomers centuries ago and learned about the **lunar** cycle. A model of the **solar** system showed the planets **spinning|spin** slowly around the Sun. One display explained how scientists measured temperature with **mercury** before modern thermometers.\n\nAnother room focused on great **inventions|invention**. Visitors saw a **wooden** printing machine, an early **refrigerator** and the first light **beam** used in cinema. A guide explained that many inventions began with mistakes. \"Even a **competent** scientist sometimes fails,\" she said. \"But failure can **collapse** old ideas and open new ones.\"\n\nThe museum also explored nature. In a dark room, a stuffed **owl** seemed to watch visitors from a tree. Children learned which **berry** is safe to eat and why the body needs each **vitamin**. A sudden noise from a hidden speaker was designed to **scare** visitors, and many children screamed with delight.\n\nAt the end of the night, families gathered at the museum's **monument** to famous scientists. A government **minister** gave a short speech about the importance of science education. Children were invited to **toss** coins into a fountain and make a wish. Before leaving, many stopped at a craft table, where they could **sew** small badges onto their bags or **scratch** patterns into bookmarks as souvenirs."},"89":{"w":["tide","affair","angle","assist","bar","brave","calculate","chemistry","collaborate","concentrate","craft","cure","curse","desperate","devise","distribute","emit","enthusiastic","error","evil"],"title":"The Chemistry of Teamwork","t":"Why do some teams succeed while others fail? Researchers who study workplace **affairs|affair** say the answer often lies in \"team **chemistry**\": the way people **collaborate** and support each other. Even talented individuals can fail if they cannot work together.\n\nA recent study examined teams in hospitals, factories and technology companies. Researchers **calculated|calculate** how often team members communicated and **distributed|distribute** tasks. They found that teams who met regularly made fewer **errors|error**. Members were more **enthusiastic** and better able to **concentrate** on difficult problems.\n\nGood leaders **assist** this process. They encourage open discussion and help team members see problems from a different **angle**. They also know how to **cure** conflict before it spreads. A manager who is **brave** enough to admit mistakes often earns greater trust.\n\nSome teams face extreme pressure. In emergencies, workers may feel **desperate** and exhausted. A team of firefighters described how they **devise** plans in seconds, relying on years of shared experience. They **emit** calm even in dangerous situations, a skill that comes from practice.\n\nNot everyone believes in team chemistry. Some critics call it a **curse** of modern management, a vague idea that hides poor planning. Others argue that the real **evil** is a culture of blame. In one company, the managers set a high **bar** for teamwork by rewarding groups rather than individuals. Experts compare good teams to the **tide**: steady, powerful and impossible to stop. Teamwork, they say, is a **craft** that must be learned."},"90":{"w":["existence","fierce","fleet","forth","gaze","handful","illustrate","insist","invisible","kingdom","leverage","likelihood","literary","modify","northern","noticeable","obscure","oneself","outline","overlook"],"title":"The Power of Stories","t":"Stories have been part of human **existence** for as long as we can remember. In every **kingdom** and culture, people have told tales to entertain, teach and **illustrate** moral lessons. Even today, stories shape how we see the world and **oneself**.\n\n**Literary** scholars point out that stories share a common **outline**. A hero faces a challenge, struggles against **fierce** obstacles and finally changes. This structure appears in ancient myths and modern films alike. A **handful** of basic plots, some say, explain almost every story ever told.\n\nStories also have practical power. Business leaders **leverage** stories to inspire employees, and politicians use them to win votes. Research shows that people remember facts better when they are part of a story. The **likelihood** of recalling information increases **noticeably|noticeable** when it is linked to emotion.\n\nSome stories are **obscure**, passed down in small villages and almost forgotten. Researchers in **northern** Europe have travelled to remote areas to record them before they disappear. One storyteller described a **fleet** of ghost ships that sailed **forth** each winter. Another told of an **invisible** forest spirit who protects children.\n\nWriters often **modify** old stories for modern audiences. They **insist** that the heart of a story never changes, even when details do. As readers, we should not **overlook** the quiet power of stories. When we **gaze** at the stars or listen to a grandparent's tale, we join a tradition as old as humanity itself."},"91":{"w":["peak","pool","possession","presumably","reinforce","representative","sadly","shake","shut","silent","skilled","slight","soft","strict","surround","vanish","vocabulary","absent","acceptable","administration"],"title":"Learning a New Language","t":"Learning a foreign language is one of the most rewarding challenges an adult can take on. It opens doors to new cultures, careers and friendships. Yet many learners give up early, discouraged by grammar rules and endless **vocabulary** lists.\n\nLanguage teachers say that success depends on regular practice. Students who use the language daily, even for a few minutes, make steady progress. **Skilled** teachers **reinforce** new words through conversation and games. They are **strict** about practice but **soft** in their correction, helping students feel relaxed.\n\nMotivation tends to **peak** in the first few weeks and then decline. **Presumably**, this is because early progress is fast, while later progress feels **slight**. Experts advise learners to set small goals and celebrate each one. Joining a language exchange can also help, as learners **pool** their knowledge and support each other.\n\nSome learners face particular difficulties. Shy students often remain **silent** in class, afraid of making mistakes. Others are frequently **absent** because of work or family duties. **Sadly**, many adult learners believe that their chance to learn has **vanished|vanish**. Teachers try to **shake** this belief, showing that it is never too late to learn.\n\nLanguage schools now offer flexible courses. Some are run by local governments, and a **representative** of the city's education **administration** says demand is rising fast. Being **surrounded|surround** by native speakers is the fastest way to learn, but online lessons are an **acceptable** alternative. Teachers remind students never to **shut** the door on mistakes. A new language, they say, is a precious **possession** that lasts a lifetime."},"92":{"w":["affection","affluent","alcohol","anxious","applicable","astronaut","awful","besides","border","bounce","brilliant","channel","chaos","classic","classmate","club","commerce","convention","coordinate","creep"],"title":"Life at an International School","t":"Last year, I spent a semester teaching at an international school near the **border** of two countries. My **classmates|classmate** from university thought it would be an easy job, but the reality was **brilliant** and exhausting at the same time.\n\nThe school had students from more than thirty countries, and the atmosphere was sometimes close to **chaos**. Lessons had to **coordinate** different languages, religions and customs. Teachers followed a strict **convention**: every message to parents was sent in two languages through the school's online **channel**.\n\nThe students were curious and energetic. Some came from **affluent** families, while others were the children of refugees. A few felt **anxious** in their new environment, and teachers worked hard to show them **affection** and patience. One girl dreamed of becoming an **astronaut**, and she spent hours reading about space.\n\nThe school offered many after-school activities. Students joined a drama **club**, a football team and a business society that studied international **commerce**. We also read **classic** novels together, and students discussed how each story was **applicable** to their own lives.\n\nNot everything was easy. The food in the canteen was often **awful**, and **besides**, the heating system broke down in winter. Teachers warned older students about the dangers of **alcohol** and late-night parties. Some days, I felt problems **creep** up faster than I could solve them. But the students' energy helped me **bounce** back every time."},"93":{"w":["criminal","dawn","delicious","despair","distinction","dynamic","elaborate","electric","elementary","enforce","engine","entertain","entrance","faculty","freely","frighten","fundamental","gradual","grand","heir"],"title":"The Electric Car Revolution","t":"For more than a century, cars have relied on petrol **engines|engine**. Today, the **electric** car is changing the industry at a remarkable pace. Many experts see this as a **fundamental** shift, comparable to the move from horses to motor vehicles.\n\nThe change has been **gradual** but steady. At first, electric cars were expensive and had limited range. Now, improvements in batteries have made them more practical. Governments **enforce** stricter emission rules, encouraging manufacturers to invest in clean technology. Some cities even ban petrol cars from their centres.\n\nEngineers describe the industry as **dynamic** and competitive. Universities have expanded their engineering **faculty** to train new specialists, and students study **elementary** physics alongside advanced design. A **grand** new research centre in Shanghai opened last year, with a huge glass **entrance** that attracts visitors.\n\nThere are challenges, too. Some drivers still **frighten** easily at the idea of running out of power on long journeys. Critics warn that electric cars depend on rare metals, and mining them can cause environmental harm. Experts make a clear **distinction** between short-term costs and long-term benefits.\n\nCar companies are also finding new ways to **entertain** drivers, with screens, music and **elaborate** voice controls. Security experts warn that **criminal** hackers could target such smart cars, but the risk has led not to **despair** but to better protection. One company's young founder, the **heir** to a family business, described his first test drive as \"more **delicious** than any meal.\"\n\nYet the greatest change may be environmental. When cars move **freely** through cities without polluting the air, people will enjoy cleaner streets. As one engineer said at **dawn** on launch day, \"This is the start of a new era.\""},"94":{"w":["horizon","hunger","impress","junior","knit","learned","manufacture","miserable","monthly","moreover","overnight","passive","peace","penetrate","phrase","protest","pure","purely","raw","ray"],"title":"The Power of Peaceful Protest","t":"Throughout history, ordinary people have used **protest** to demand **peace**, justice and change. From marches against unfair laws to campaigns for clean air, protests have shaped the modern world. Historians say that peaceful methods are often more effective than violent ones.\n\n**Learned** scholars have studied this pattern carefully. One study found that peaceful protests were twice as likely to succeed as violent ones. **Moreover**, they tend to attract wider support, including from people who are usually **passive**. When protesters remain calm, their message can **penetrate** even hostile audiences.\n\nProtests often begin with a simple **phrase** or slogan that captures public feeling. In one famous case, young people demanded \"clean air for every child.\" The movement grew **overnight**, spreading across cities through social media. Its leaders were careful to keep the protests **purely** peaceful, and they earned wide respect.\n\nYet protest is not easy. Participants may face **hunger**, cold and fear, especially during long campaigns. Some protesters live in **miserable** conditions while they wait for change. Organisers hold **monthly** meetings to plan their next steps, and older supporters often **knit** scarves for young protesters during winter campaigns.\n\nExperts say that young people are increasingly involved. **Junior** members of student groups often lead campaigns, and their energy can **impress** older generations. Protests have even begun to **manufacture** new forms of art, such as songs and paintings. For many, the goal is not **raw** anger but a **pure** hope for a better future. As one activist said, \"Every **ray** of light starts somewhere,\" and she believes the **horizon** is bright."},"95":{"w":["refuge","register","relief","reluctant","reply","reproduce","resistant","retail","roll","rude","selfish","sensitive","severe","sharp","shock","shortly","silver","sixty","somehow","spacecraft"],"title":"A Lesson in Kindness","t":"Last winter, a severe storm hit our city. Roads were blocked, power lines were down, and thousands of people were left without heat. The city opened schools and community centres as places of **refuge**, and volunteers rushed to help.\n\nI volunteered at a local school, where I helped people **register** for food and blankets. At first, some people were **reluctant** to accept help, feeling embarrassed. Others were **rude** and impatient, which was a **shock** to me. But most were grateful, and their **relief** was clear when they received hot meals.\n\nOne elderly man, around **sixty**, told me he had worked in **retail** for forty years. \"I've never asked for help before,\" he said. I gave him a blanket and a **roll** of bread, and he thanked me warmly. **Somehow**, that small moment made me feel proud.\n\nThe experience taught me that kindness is not always easy. It requires patience and **sensitive** attention to others' feelings. **Selfish** behaviour, such as taking more than one's share, made the work harder. But volunteers learned to **reply** calmly and fairly to everyone.\n\n**Shortly** after the storm, life returned to normal. Engineers worked to make power lines more **resistant** to extreme weather. A local artist planned to **reproduce** photos of the volunteers in a public exhibition. My own memories of that week remain **sharp**. I still keep a small **silver** medal the city gave volunteers, and I look at it whenever I feel discouraged. Even a **spacecraft**, my little brother joked, couldn't have reached us faster than the volunteers did. The storm's damage was **severe**, but the kindness we saw was stronger."},"96":{"w":["split","sprinkle","statistical","strictly","strip","surgeon","surprisingly","sway","sympathetic","tear","tension","territory","theme","thrill","tomato","transplant","truck","undergraduate","upset","vacuum"],"title":"A Day in the Life of a Surgeon","t":"Dr. Huang is a heart **surgeon** at a large hospital in Beijing. Her days are long and intense, but she says the work brings a unique **thrill**. \"Every operation is a challenge,\" she explains. \"You hold someone's life in your hands.\"\n\nHer morning begins early. Before each operation, she reviews **statistical** data on the patient's condition and discusses the plan with her team. Rules in the operating room are followed **strictly**; even a small mistake can be dangerous. The team works in silence, and the **tension** is high.\n\nOne of her specialities is heart **transplant** surgery. These operations can last for hours, and **surprisingly**, she rarely feels tired until they are over. Afterwards, she meets patients' families, who are often **upset** and anxious. She tries to be honest and **sympathetic**, explaining what happened in simple words. Some families burst into **tears|tear** of relief.\n\nDr. Huang also teaches **undergraduate** medical students. She encourages them to stay curious. She warns them not to **split** their attention between patients and phones, and she **sprinkles|sprinkle** her lectures with stories from her career. A favourite **theme** is the importance of listening to patients. \"Medicine is a **territory** without borders,\" she likes to say.\n\nAt home, she enjoys simple pleasures. She grows **tomato** plants on her balcony and cleans her flat with an old **vacuum** cleaner. Once a month, she drives a borrowed **truck** to deliver donated supplies to rural clinics. She jokes that she has had to **strip** her life down to essentials. \"But I would never let anyone **sway** me from this job,\" she says."},"97":{"w":["wherever","wise","withdraw","alive","appetite","boil","butterfly","captain","cent","charming","courage","crash","daylight","defend","dimension","entitle","fortune","fox","frog","gulf"],"title":"The Captain's Island","t":"For thirty years, **Captain** Liu sailed cargo ships across every ocean, from the **Gulf** of Mexico to the South China Sea. He survived storms, a ship **crash** near Africa and many lonely nights at sea. Now retired, he lives on a small island where he grows vegetables and watches **daylight** fade over the water.\n\nThe island is **charming** and peaceful. **Wherever** you walk, you see colourful **butterflies|butterfly**, and at night you hear a **frog** chorus from the pond. Occasionally, a wild **fox** appears near the houses, searching for food. The captain says the island feels more **alive** than any city.\n\nHe often tells stories to visitors. He believes that **courage** is not the absence of fear but the ability to act despite it. He also talks about luck. \"Some sailors made a **fortune**,\" he says, \"while others lost every **cent**.\" He believes that a **wise** sailor respects the sea and never takes it for granted.\n\nThe captain enjoys cooking. He likes to **boil** fresh seafood with herbs from his garden, and visitors say his meals improve any **appetite**. He also writes memoirs, exploring the human **dimension** of life at sea. His first book is **entitled|entitle** *A Life Between Waves*.\n\nRecently, a developer wanted to buy part of the island for a hotel. The captain refused to **withdraw** his objections, and he joined local residents to **defend** the island's natural beauty. \"Some places should stay wild,\" he says."},"98":{"w":["harmony","identification","intentional","intimidate","magnetic","mayor","mode","optimistic","owe","paradise","permission","pond","pour","purify","rush","semester","sequence","significance","soul","supplement"],"title":"A Mayor with a Plan","t":"When Ms. Wu became **mayor** of a small industrial city, she found it in poor condition. The air was polluted, the river was dirty and young people were leaving. She promised to turn the city into a \"green **paradise**,\" a goal that many residents thought impossible.\n\nHer first step was to **purify** the river. Factories were required to treat waste water, and those that broke the rules were fined. Some business owners tried to **intimidate** her, but she refused to change course. \"Pollution is not **intentional**, but it is our responsibility,\" she said. Within two years, fish returned to the river, and children could swim in a nearby **pond**.\n\nMs. Wu also changed the city's **mode** of transport. She introduced electric buses and built new cycle paths. During the morning **rush**, many residents now ride bikes instead of driving. She asked for public **permission** before making each major change, holding open meetings in every district.\n\nHer approach has a clear **sequence**: first listen, then plan, then act. She is **optimistic** but realistic. \"We **owe** future generations a clean city,\" she says. Critics admit that her personality is **magnetic**; people trust her because she keeps her promises.\n\nThe city's transformation has attracted attention. University students visit each **semester** to study its success. Experts say its **significance** goes beyond one city, showing that **harmony** between industry and nature is possible. Funding came from national grants that **supplement** local taxes. Residents now carry **identification** cards that give them free bus travel, and many say the city has regained its **soul**. Today, the river **pours|pour** clean water into the sea."},"99":{"w":["swell","tropical","wave","workshop","calendar","cloth","comedy","flame","indoors","industrialize","iron","librarian","opera","ounce","quiz","wreck","acceleration","anchor","apologize","apology"],"title":"The Library That Never Sleeps","t":"In a small coastal town, the public library has become the heart of the community. Its **librarian**, Mrs. Chen, has worked there for twenty-five years. She believes a library should be more than a place to borrow books; it should be a place to learn, meet and create.\n\nThe library offers activities for every age. Children take part in weekly **quiz** competitions, and teenagers attend a writing **workshop** led by local authors. On Friday evenings, the library hosts film nights, showing everything from **comedy** to **opera**. A large **calendar** at the entrance lists upcoming events.\n\nThe building itself has an interesting history. It was once an old **iron** factory, built when the region began to **industrialize** in the nineteenth century. Workers made tools and **cloth** there. Today, the old furnaces have been replaced by reading rooms, but a metal **anchor** from the factory still stands outside.\n\nNot everything has gone smoothly. Ten years ago, a **tropical** storm caused a huge **wave** that damaged the building. The library looked like a **wreck**, and books were soaked. Mrs. Chen remembers feeling her heart **swell** with emotion when hundreds of volunteers came to help. She still keeps a burnt book saved from a small **flame** that broke out during the storm.\n\nMrs. Chen has seen the **acceleration** of digital change. People now read e-books and listen to audiobooks, and fewer come **indoors** to read. But she remains positive. \"Every **ounce** of effort to keep the library alive is worthwhile,\" she says. When a visitor once made a mistake and damaged a rare book, he wanted to **apologize** for hours. She accepted his **apology** with a smile."},"100":{"w":["apparent","arbitrary","ash","assignment","badly","barely","barrier","biology","cap","consent","county","crush","deepen","deliberately","dense","dip","document","dramatic","drought","dust"],"title":"Life After the Drought","t":"Two years ago, a terrible **drought** struck a farming **county** in northern China. For months, there was **barely** any rain. Rivers dried up, fields turned to **dust** and the air was full of **ash** from distant fires. Many farmers were **badly** affected, losing almost all their crops.\n\nThe effects were **dramatic**. Some families had to sell their animals, and others left to find work in cities. Local officials faced a difficult **assignment**: to help farmers survive and prepare for the future. Their first task was to **document** the damage, visiting every village to record losses.\n\nScientists explained that the drought was not **arbitrary**. Changing weather patterns, combined with **dense** farming and poor water management, had made the region vulnerable. A professor of **biology** warned that soil quality had declined and that recovery would take years. \"The **apparent** cause is lack of rain,\" she said, \"but the deeper problem is how we use land.\"\n\nThe county introduced new measures. Farmers who gave **consent** were trained in water-saving methods, and the government placed a **cap** on water use for factories. Engineers built a **barrier** to protect wetlands, and new wells were dug to **deepen** water supplies. Some critics said that the plans were **deliberately** slow, but most farmers welcomed them.\n\nToday, the region is recovering. Crops are growing again, and farmers **dip** their hands into the soil with hope. The crisis did not **crush** their spirit. \"We learned that nature is not our enemy,\" one farmer said. \"We must work with it.\""},"101":{"w":["echo","emergency","episode","equality","evident","expend","fantasy","flourish","fog","fond","fortunate","framework","furthermore","gallery","gear","gravity","hazard","headache","heal","inch"],"title":"The Mountain Rescue Team","t":"High in the mountains of Sichuan, a small volunteer team responds to every **emergency** on the hiking trails. Its members are farmers, teachers and shopkeepers who know every path and cliff. \"We are **fortunate** to live in such a beautiful place,\" says the team leader, \"but beauty brings **hazards|hazard**.\"\n\nRescues are often difficult. Thick **fog** can cover the mountains in minutes, and lost hikers may be only an **inch** from danger. The team carries special **gear**, including ropes, radios and warm blankets. They also follow a clear **framework** of safety rules, developed over twenty years of experience.\n\nOne **episode** last winter showed the team's courage. A young hiker fell on an icy slope and injured his leg. The rescuers heard the **echo** of his calls and followed the sound for hours. **Furthermore**, they had to fight against **gravity** as they carried him up a steep cliff. \"It was a terrible **headache**,\" one volunteer joked, \"but we never give up.\"\n\nThe team believes in **equality**: every member, whatever their background, has an equal voice. They **expend** great energy training new volunteers each spring. It is **evident** that their work saves lives; injuries on the trails have fallen by half.\n\nMany hikers are **fond** of the team and send letters of thanks. Some hikers come with romantic **fantasy** ideas about the mountains, unprepared for real danger. The team now runs a small **gallery** in the village, displaying photos of rescues and mountain scenery. They hope tourism will help the region **flourish**. As the leader says, \"Our goal is not only to rescue people but to help the land and its people **heal**.\""},"102":{"w":["inhabit","installation","instant","insult","interfere","kick","lady","lane","lawyer","lazy","leaf","lens","loyal","merit","necessity","nervous","net","occupy","operator","overtake"],"title":"A Lawyer's Life","t":"Mr. Zhao has worked as a **lawyer** for twenty years in a small city in eastern China. His office is on a narrow **lane** near the court, and his door is always open. Clients come with every kind of problem, from business disputes to family conflicts.\n\nHe believes that good lawyers must be patient and **loyal** to their clients. \"People who come here are **nervous** and often desperate,\" he says. \"My job is to help them understand their rights.\" He judges each case on its **merit**, never on the client's wealth or status.\n\nHis work is demanding. Cases **occupy** most of his time, and he often works late at night. He drinks **instant** coffee to stay awake and reads documents through thick glasses with scratched **lenses|lens**. His wife jokes that he is never **lazy**, even on holidays.\n\nSome cases are emotional. Once, he defended an elderly **lady** whose landlord had tried to **interfere** with her right to stay in her home. The landlord shouted an **insult** at her in court, but the judge ruled in her favour. Mr. Zhao says such victories make his work feel like a **necessity**, not just a job.\n\nThe legal system is changing. New technology allows the **installation** of online systems that let clients file documents from home. A phone **operator** at the court now helps people book appointments. Mr. Zhao worries that some lawyers will be **overtaken|overtake** by technology, but he believes human judgement remains essential.\n\nOutside work, he keeps life simple. He plays football with friends on weekends and can still **kick** a ball harder than men half his age. He loves gardening, saying that every **leaf** reminds him that small things matter. Families who **inhabit** the old part of the city call him \"our lawyer,\" and he says the **net** result of his career is not money but trust."},"103":{"w":["panel","pitch","pop","rein","ripe","scarce","scrape","simplicity","smash","smoothly","southwest","spite","spur","static","stir","suspend","synthetic","tale","temper","thereby"],"title":"The Simple Life","t":"In an age of constant noise and technology, some people are choosing a life of **simplicity**. They move to the countryside, grow their own food and spend less time online. Psychologists say this trend reflects a deep desire for peace.\n\nMy friend Mei made this choice three years ago. She left her job in a busy city and moved to a small farm in the **southwest**. At first, life did not go **smoothly**. The water pipes broke, and she had to **scrape** ice from the windows on winter mornings. In **spite** of these problems, she never regretted her decision. Every summer, she even chooses to **suspend** her phone contract for a month to enjoy the silence.\n\nMei grows vegetables and keeps a small orchard. When the fruit is **ripe**, she invites neighbours to help pick it. She also makes jam, stirring the pot slowly over a wood fire; to **stir** it too fast, she says, ruins the flavour. She avoids **synthetic** chemicals and uses natural methods to protect her plants.\n\nLife is not always peaceful. Once, a storm **smashed|smash** her greenhouse. She felt her **temper** rise, but she soon calmed down and began repairs. She keeps her horse's **rein** hanging by the door, ready for rides into the hills. Water is **scarce** in summer, so she collects rainwater carefully.\n\nMei now runs workshops for city visitors who want to experience rural life. A local **panel** of experts praised her work, and her story has become a popular **tale** in the region. A **pop** song she wrote about the farm spread online, and a publisher has asked her to **pitch** an idea for a book. \"Moving here was a **spur** to change my whole life,\" she says. Her success has encouraged others, **thereby** helping to bring new energy to the village. When asked whether she misses the city, she laughs. \"Life there was **static**,\" she says. \"Here, every day is different.\""},"104":{"w":["trial","usage","vigorous","winner","worthless","abuse","accuse","agent","alike","amplify","bathroom","bean","bee","belt","bitter","bless","breathe","brief","bubble","bulk"],"title":"The Winner Who Gave It Away","t":"Last year, a young factory worker won a national cooking competition. The **winner**, a shy woman named Li Hong, surprised everyone by donating her entire prize to a children's charity. \"Money without kindness is **worthless**,\" she said in a **brief** speech.\n\nLi Hong grew up in a poor village where her family kept a cow and grew **beans|bean**. Her childhood was **bitter** in many ways: her father struggled with alcohol **abuse**, and the family often went hungry. Yet her grandmother taught her to cook and to **bless** every meal. \"Food can heal,\" she told her.\n\nThe competition was a **trial** of skill and nerve. Contestants had to cook three dishes in two hours, using a **bulk** supply of ingredients. Some competitors **accused|accuse** each other of cheating, but Li Hong stayed calm. \"I just tried to **breathe** and focus,\" she said. Her honey cake, made with honey from her grandmother's **bee** hives, impressed the judges.\n\nHer victory attracted wide attention. A talent **agent** offered her a television contract, but she refused. \"Rich and poor people are **alike** in one way: everyone needs good food,\" she explained. Instead, she plans to open a cooking school for village children.\n\nExperts say her story shows how the media can **amplify** acts of kindness. In a world full of bad news, her decision created a **bubble** of hope. She still works at the factory, wearing her old leather **belt** and simple clothes. When journalists visited her small flat, they found a tidy kitchen and a tiny **bathroom** with a cracked mirror. Her **vigorous** energy and modest lifestyle have inspired many. The **usage** of her recipes on social media has grown rapidly."},"105":{"w":["bury","canoe","carpet","civilization","classification","compile","cow","defence","departure","deposit","diary","dictate","disorder","disposal","dissatisfy","downward","eager","elastic","embarrass","exclusive"],"title":"A Diary from Ancient Times","t":"Archaeologists in northern China recently discovered an ancient **diary** **buried|bury** beneath the floor of an old house. Written on bamboo strips more than two thousand years ago, it describes the daily life of a farmer and his family. Historians say it offers a rare view of early **civilization**.\n\nThe diary records everyday events. The farmer describes his **cow**, the planting of crops and a long journey by **canoe** to a market town. He also mentions a flood that caused great **disorder** in the village. Some pages **dictate** rules for his children, such as respecting elders and working hard.\n\nResearchers worked carefully to **compile** a full translation. They also developed a **classification** system for the different types of entries: farming, family and religion. The project required great patience, since some strips were broken and had to be reconnected.\n\nThe diary reveals the farmer's emotions too. He writes about being **dissatisfied|dissatisfy** with high taxes and **embarrassed|embarrass** by his poverty. Yet he remains **eager** to improve his family's life. One entry describes the **departure** of his son for military service, a moment of deep sadness. Another describes the village's **defence** against bandits.\n\nToday, the diary is displayed in a museum, in an **exclusive** room with special lighting. Visitors walk on a soft **carpet** to protect the ancient floor beneath. The museum plans careful **disposal** of damaged materials and safe **deposit** of the strips in a climate-controlled vault. Curators say the strips are surprisingly **elastic**, made from high-quality bamboo. As one historian put it, \"This diary shows that human worries never change; only the details move upward or **downward** with time.\""},"106":{"w":["fare","fashionable","fatal","fault","flow","furniture","hardware","headquarters","hell","honesty","hut","immigrant","indifferent","inhabitant","inherit","interference","interior","interrupt","inventor","layer"],"title":"The Inventor's Workshop","t":"In a small **hut** at the edge of a mountain village lives an old **inventor** named Mr. Sun. He has spent fifty years creating machines from scrap metal, old **furniture** and broken **hardware**. His workshop is full of strange devices, and visitors are amazed by what he has built.\n\nMr. Sun **inherited|inherit** his love of invention from his father, a village blacksmith. As a boy, he would take apart clocks and radios to see how they worked. His father never **interrupted|interrupt** him, saying that curiosity is a gift. \"If something broke, it was not my **fault**,\" Mr. Sun laughs. \"It was research.\"\n\nHis most famous invention is a small machine that uses the **flow** of a mountain stream to produce electricity. It powers lights in several homes, saving villagers money on bills. A technology company from the city once invited him to its **headquarters** to discuss buying the design, but he refused. \"It belongs to everyone,\" he said.\n\nNot everyone has supported him. Some officials were **indifferent** to his work, and others saw it as **interference** in government projects. He also faced **fatal** risks: once, an electrical fault nearly killed him. Yet his **honesty** and generosity have earned great respect. Even a few **fashionable** young engineers from the city now visit him to learn.\n\nThe village has changed over the years. Many young **inhabitants|inhabitant** have moved to cities, and an **immigrant** family from another province now runs the local shop. Bus **fares|fare** have risen, making it harder for visitors to come. But Mr. Sun's workshop remains a symbol of creativity. Its walls are covered in a thick **layer** of drawings and notes. \"Without curiosity, life would be **hell**,\" he says with a smile. \"With it, even a simple **interior** like this becomes a world of possibility.\""},"107":{"w":["listener","loose","mate","measurable","occasional","orbit","overtime","paragraph","peaceful","percentage","petroleum","philosophy","pill","pole","pound","profession","prosperous","rail","ratio","reap"],"title":"A Philosophy of Work","t":"In many modern economies, working **overtime** has become normal. Employees stay late to finish projects, answer emails at night and work on weekends. But a growing number of experts question whether this culture is healthy or even productive.\n\nResearchers have found that productivity is **measurable**, and it does not always increase with longer hours. One study compared the **percentage** of tasks completed by workers in different countries. The **ratio** of hours to output was surprisingly similar, suggesting that extra time does not always bring better results.\n\nSome companies have adopted a different **philosophy**. They encourage employees to rest, take **occasional** breaks and leave work on time. In one company, managers ask every new employee to write a short **paragraph** describing their goals for a balanced life. The results have been positive: workers are happier and less likely to need a **pill** for stress or sleep problems.\n\nThis shift reflects deeper changes in society. In the past, a **prosperous** economy depended on heavy industries such as **petroleum** and **rail** transport. Today, many jobs depend on creativity and teamwork, which require a **peaceful** mind. Every **profession**, from teaching to engineering, can benefit from a healthier pace.\n\nExperts suggest simple changes. Talk to a work **mate** about stress rather than keeping it inside. Be a good **listener** when colleagues need support. Wear **loose**, comfortable clothes when working from home. \"You can't **reap** the rewards of hard work if you're exhausted,\" one psychologist says. Even astronauts in **orbit** around the Earth follow strict rest schedules. Whether you earn in yuan or **pounds|pound**, health matters more than money, and rest is not the opposite **pole** of success but part of it."},"108":{"w":["relieve","remark","respectful","resume","scenery","scheme","senate","severely","sharpen","shot","sin","smog","spontaneous","stack","stain","storage","structural","submit","sunset","surplus"],"title":"Restoring an Old Theatre","t":"In the centre of an old European city stands a theatre built more than two hundred years ago. For decades it was **severely** damaged by neglect, its walls covered in **stains|stain** and its roof leaking. Last year, a team of architects began an ambitious **scheme** to restore it.\n\nThe first stage was a **structural** survey. Engineers checked every wall and beam, and they found that some were close to collapse. They decided to **stack** old bricks carefully in a **storage** area so that they could be reused. Painters worked to restore the beautiful stage **scenery**, painted by local artists in the nineteenth century.\n\nThe project was funded partly by the city and partly by private donations. A **surplus** in the city budget made it possible to **resume** work after a pause during the winter. Local politicians debated the project in the city **senate**, and most supported it. A few made critical **remarks|remark** about the cost, but public opinion was strongly in favour.\n\nThe restoration has been **respectful** of history. Architects have tried to keep original details, from wooden chairs to painted ceilings. Air pollution and **smog** from nearby traffic had damaged the stone, and experts used special methods to clean it. They had to **sharpen** their skills and **submit** detailed reports at every step.\n\nWhen the theatre reopens next year, the first performance will be an old play about **sin** and forgiveness. Audiences will enter at **sunset**, and the evening will begin with a photographer's **shot** of the full hall. Many residents feel **relieved|relieve** that the building has been saved. Their reaction, says the director, has been **spontaneous** joy."},"109":{"w":["thorough","thread","toilet","troublesome","unbearable","violence","vitally","wagon","weaken","weave","yearly","youngster","algebra","arrest","blend","cafe","ceremony","comprise","crew","inferior"],"title":"A Wedding in the Village","t":"Last autumn, I attended a traditional wedding **ceremony** in a mountain village. The celebration lasted three days and **comprised|comprise** music, dancing and endless meals. It was a fascinating **blend** of old customs and modern life.\n\nPreparations were **thorough**. Women **weave** colourful cloth for the bride's dress, using **thread** dyed with natural plants. Every **youngster** in the village helped decorate the houses, and men repaired the wooden **wagon** that would carry the bride. A **crew** of cooks worked day and night in a temporary kitchen.\n\nNot everything was easy. The village has only one public **toilet**, and the queues were **unbearable** at times. Heavy rain made the roads muddy and **troublesome** for guests arriving from the city. But nobody complained; the mood was too joyful.\n\nThe wedding also revealed changes in village life. Older people worried that modern habits would **weaken** traditions, and some described city life as **inferior** to village values. A young teacher who teaches **algebra** at the local school disagreed, saying that both have value. Guests spent the evening at a small **cafe** opened recently by a returning villager.\n\nVillage elders explained that such celebrations are **vitally** important for community bonds. The village holds a **yearly** festival where these traditions are passed on. They proudly noted that there has been no **violence** in the village for decades. When a visitor once tried to steal a gift, the villagers caught him before the police could **arrest** him, and he apologised."},"110":{"w":["landlord","maths","memorial","nest","physician","rainbow","reliance","sponsor","statue","tablet","tutor","voluntary","abnormal","absolute","accessory","accord","accordance","appoint","approve","arrival"],"title":"The Statue in the Square","t":"In the centre of our town stands a bronze **statue** of a **physician** who served the community for fifty years. Born in 1920, she was the first woman doctor in the region. After her death, residents raised money for a **memorial**, and the statue was unveiled on the anniversary of her **arrival** in the town.\n\nHer story is remarkable. She came to the town as a young doctor, **appointed|appoint** by the provincial government. At that time, there was no hospital, and people relied on traditional healers. She worked tirelessly, treating patients in their homes and training local nurses. During the **abnormal** floods of 1975, she even rowed a small boat to reach her patients. In **accordance** with her wishes, her clinic still offers free care to the poor.\n\nShe also cared deeply about education. In her spare time, she worked as a **tutor**, helping children with **maths** and reading. She encouraged **voluntary** work among young people, and many of her students later became doctors and teachers. Her own **landlord** once said she was the most generous tenant he had ever had.\n\nWhen the town council decided to build the memorial, there was **absolute** agreement. A local business agreed to **sponsor** the project, and the government **approved|approve** it quickly. The statue shows her holding a medical bag, with a bird's **nest** carved at her feet as a symbol of care. Each year, schoolchildren paint a **rainbow** around the base on her birthday.\n\nToday, the town has a modern hospital, and doctors use a **tablet** instead of paper records. Yet residents still remember her values; their **reliance** on her advice taught them to trust science. Her old stethoscope, an **accessory** she never left home without, is displayed in the town museum. By common **accord**, the statue stands as a symbol of service."},"111":{"w":["asleep","assemble","astonish","avenue","aviation","awake","bachelor","bark","bath","beforehand","behalf","bet","bicycle","blanket","blast","bleed","bold","bow","burst","cake"],"title":"The Night the Dog Barked","t":"Last winter, my neighbour Mr. Li was **asleep** in his flat on a quiet **avenue** when his old dog began to **bark** loudly. It was three in the morning. Annoyed, he got out of bed, wrapped himself in a **blanket** and went to see what was wrong.\n\nHe was **astonished|astonish** by what he found. Smoke was coming from the flat next door, where a young couple lived. Suddenly, he heard a loud **blast** as a gas pipe **burst** in their kitchen. Mr. Li shouted to wake them, banging on the door until they were **awake**. Within minutes, the whole building had **assembled|assemble** in the street.\n\nFirefighters arrived quickly and put out the fire. Nobody was seriously hurt, though the young husband had cut his hand and began to **bleed**. A neighbour wrapped it in a towel. The couple were grateful and later came to **bow** to Mr. Li in thanks, a traditional gesture of respect.\n\nMr. Li is a retired engineer who once worked in **aviation**, designing parts for aircraft. He is a **bachelor** who lives alone with his dog. He says he never imagined being a hero. \"I just did what anyone would do,\" he said modestly. \"But I **bet** my dog deserves the credit.\"\n\nThe building's residents organised a small party to thank him. They baked a large **cake** and gave his dog a new collar. On **behalf** of all the families, the building manager presented him with a **bicycle**, since his old one had been stolen. The manager also gave a **bold** speech about fire safety, reminding everyone to check gas pipes **beforehand**. That night, the dog enjoyed a warm **bath** and a long sleep."},"112":{"w":["calculation","careless","carve","catalog","chamber","chart","chase","cheer","chest","chill","civilize","clock","clue","coat","collar","comparatively","compose","compute","concrete","condemn"],"title":"Inside a Detective's Mind","t":"Detective Zhou has worked for the city police for twenty years. Her colleagues admire her ability to find the smallest **clue** and turn it into a solution. \"Every crime leaves something behind,\" she says. \"You just have to look carefully.\"\n\nHer methods combine careful **calculation** and intuition. She keeps a detailed **chart** of every case on her office wall, connecting people and events with coloured lines. She also uses computers to **compute** probabilities and search police records, which are organised in a huge digital **catalog**.\n\nOne famous case began with a single piece of evidence: a dog **collar** found near a riverbank. Zhou noticed that the collar had been **carved|carve** with initials, which led her to the owner. A long investigation followed, involving a **chase** through the city's old streets. When the suspect was finally caught, the whole police station began to **cheer**.\n\nZhou believes that **careless** mistakes ruin many investigations. \"**Comparatively** few cases fail because of a lack of evidence,\" she says. \"Most fail because someone misses a detail.\" She **condemns|condemn** officers who take shortcuts, and she insists that every report be **concrete** and accurate.\n\nHer work can be stressful. Long nights in a cold interview **chamber** sometimes leave her with a **chill** that lasts for days. She often works against the **clock**, racing to solve cases before evidence disappears. To relax, she writes poetry and likes to **compose** short songs on the piano. She keeps an old wooden **chest** of notebooks at home and wears the same grey **coat** to every crime scene; her colleagues call it her lucky charm. \"Solving crimes helps **civilize** society,\" she says. \"That makes every long night worthwhile.\""},"113":{"w":["conjunction","conscience","consequently","considerable","continuous","conversely","convert","correspond","crazy","dairy","dash","decent","delete","democratic","depth","destructive","determination","diligent","discard","distress"],"title":"The Dairy Farmer's Choice","t":"For forty years, the Li family has run a small **dairy** farm in northern China. They raise cows, produce fresh milk and sell cheese to local shops. But in recent years, the business has faced **considerable** challenges, and the family has had to make difficult decisions.\n\nThe main problem is competition. Large companies produce milk on a massive scale, **consequently** pushing prices down. Small farms struggle to earn a **decent** income. **Conversely**, consumers increasingly want natural, local products, and this demand offers hope. The family decided to **convert** part of the farm into a tourist attraction, where visitors can learn about milk production.\n\nMr. Li's son, a **diligent** young man who studied agriculture, led the change. He worked with great **determination**, building a small museum and a café. He also had to **delete** old customer records and **discard** outdated equipment. \"Sometimes you must let go of the past to survive,\" he says.\n\nNot everyone in the family agreed. Mr. Li's wife worried that tourists would cause **distress** to the animals, and that busy weekends would be **destructive** to the farm's peaceful atmosphere. But after long discussions, the family reached a **democratic** decision: they would try the plan for one year.\n\nThe results have been encouraging. Visitors **dash** around the farm with excitement, and children are **crazy** about feeding the calves. The family's income has risen, and their products now **correspond** to the growing demand for organic food. Mr. Li says he follows his **conscience**: \"We treat our animals well, and we are honest with customers.\" In **conjunction** with local schools, the farm also runs **continuous** education programmes, so that visitors understand the **depth** of work behind every glass of milk."},"114":{"w":["dozen","dwelling","eastern","elder","elimination","enroll","envy","equivalent","exact","exceedingly","execute","explode","explosion","extension","extinct","fearful","feasible","fever","file","finger"],"title":"The Volcano Village","t":"In a remote region of **eastern** Indonesia, a small village sits at the foot of an active volcano. About a **dozen** families live there, in simple **dwellings|dwelling** made of wood and stone. Every few years, the volcano **explodes|explode**, filling the sky with ash.\n\nDespite the danger, villagers refuse to leave. \"This land belongs to our ancestors,\" says one **elder**. The soil is rich and fertile, allowing farmers to grow coffee and vegetables. To outsiders, living here may seem **exceedingly** risky, but for the villagers it is home.\n\nScientists monitor the volcano closely. They measure **exact** temperatures and gas levels, and they **file** reports every week. When signs of an **explosion** appear, the government sends warnings. Officials have developed a plan for the **elimination** of risk, but they admit that complete safety is not **feasible**.\n\nSome villagers are **fearful** of the next eruption, while others accept it calmly. Children **enroll** at a school in a nearby town, and some families have moved there. Yet many residents **envy** neither city life nor its comforts. \"We have everything we need,\" one farmer says, pointing a **finger** at the green hills.\n\nThe region also attracts researchers interested in ancient species. Many plants that once grew here are now **extinct**, destroyed by past eruptions. Experts **execute** careful surveys to protect the remaining ones. Recently, a scientist caught a **fever** during fieldwork and had to rest for a week. The government is considering an **extension** of the protected area, which would be **equivalent** to two hundred football fields."},"115":{"w":["flock","forbid","formation","fraction","frank","fry","geography","germ","glimpse","graceful","grasp","grave","gray","grip","gross","hammer","harness","harsh","hence","horrible"],"title":"Life on a Sheep Farm","t":"On the **harsh** grasslands of Inner Mongolia, a herder named Mr. Bao looks after a large **flock** of sheep. His family has raised animals here for generations. The winters are **horrible**, with freezing winds and heavy snow, but he says he would not live anywhere else.\n\nMr. Bao has a deep **grasp** of the land. He knows the **geography** of every hill and river, and he can predict the weather by watching the sky. In autumn, he leads the sheep to winter pastures in a long **formation**, like a moving river of white and **gray**.\n\nLife on the grassland is changing. Young people leave for cities, and only a **fraction** of herders' children choose to continue the work. Mr. Bao is **frank** about the challenges. \"Our way of life is in **grave** danger,\" he says. \"If we lose it, we lose our culture.\" **Hence**, he teaches his children traditional skills, even if they plan other careers.\n\nTechnology has arrived too. Mr. Bao now uses solar panels to **harness** energy and a mobile phone to check market prices. The **gross** income of herders has risen in recent years, partly because of tourism. Visitors come to catch a **glimpse** of traditional life and watch **graceful** horses gallop across the plains.\n\nThe government has tried to **forbid** overgrazing, which damages the grassland. Mr. Bao supports the rules but worries about their effect on small herders. In the evening, his wife **fries|fry** mutton and bread over a fire. Children **grip** their warm cups of milk tea, listening to stories. Mr. Bao repairs his tools with an old **hammer** and warns his grandchildren to wash their hands, since a single **germ** can make the animals sick."},"116":{"w":["humble","impatient","incorrect","index","indignant","indispensable","infect","infinite","injure","inquire","jewel","jungle","justice","kindness","landing","laundry","lawn","leak","liable","liberation"],"title":"Kindness in the Jungle","t":"Dr. Wei is a doctor who spent two years working in a small clinic deep in the **jungle** of Southeast Asia. The village could only be reached by boat, and the nearest airport **landing** strip was a day's journey away. She went there believing that medical care is a basic form of **justice**.\n\nThe work was difficult. Many patients had been **infected|infect** by diseases carried by insects, and some had been **injured|injure** in farming accidents. The clinic's equipment was old, and medicine sometimes ran out. Supplies were **indispensable**, and Dr. Wei learned to plan carefully, keeping an **index** of every medicine and its expiry date.\n\nShe also learned to be **humble**. Local healers knew plants that she had never heard of, and she often **inquired|inquire** about their uses. When an early diagnosis of hers proved **incorrect**, she admitted the mistake openly. The villagers respected her honesty and treated her with great **kindness**.\n\nNot everything was easy. Sometimes she felt **impatient** with slow supply deliveries, and once she was **indignant** when officials ignored her request for help. The roof had a **leak** above her bed during the rainy season, and her **laundry** never seemed to dry. She also worried that she might be held **liable** if anything went wrong.\n\nYet the experience gave her **infinite** satisfaction. She remembers a mother who gave her a small **jewel** as a thank-you gift, which she still wears. On her last day, villagers gathered on the **lawn** in front of the clinic to sing for her. \"I learned more than I taught,\" she says. She describes the experience as a kind of **liberation** from her busy life in the city."},"117":{"w":["lime","loop","lover","magnificent","maid","mankind","married","mask","masterpiece","mathematics","mechanical","mess","metric","minor","monkey","mortal","mould","mount","mouse","mud"],"title":"The Monkey and the Masterpiece","t":"Last summer, a famous art museum in Europe faced an unusual problem. A small **monkey** escaped from a nearby zoo and found its way into the gallery. It climbed onto a frame holding a **masterpiece** by a seventeenth-century painter and began to play with the canvas. Visitors watched in shock as the animal made a complete **mess** of the room.\n\nMuseum staff acted quickly. One guard put on a protective **mask** and gloves, while another tried to tempt the monkey with a slice of **lime**. Eventually, a zookeeper arrived and calmly led the animal out. Fortunately, the painting suffered only **minor** damage.\n\nThe incident made headlines around the world. Experts noted that the museum's security system, based on **mechanical** alarms, had failed to detect the animal. The director promised to install new sensors. \"Even a **mouse** could have entered,\" she joked, \"and we wouldn't have noticed.\"\n\nThe painting itself has a fascinating history. It shows a **magnificent** palace, a young **maid** and a **married** couple walking in a garden. Art historians believe it reflects the painter's ideas about **mankind** and **mortal** life. Some even use **mathematics** to analyse its perfect proportions, measuring them in **metric** units.\n\nRestorers worked carefully to repair the damage. They removed traces of **mud** left by the monkey's feet and treated a small patch of **mould** on the back of the canvas. The painting will soon be **mounted|mount** again on the gallery wall. Art **lovers|lover** say the story has made the painting even more popular. Some visitors now walk in a **loop** around the room just to see the famous \"monkey painting.\""},"118":{"w":["mysterious","namely","nonsense","obey","oblige","occurrence","pacific","painter","parliament","partial","personnel","physicist","pilot","pink","poet","portable","portrait","postman","postpone","preceding"],"title":"Letters Across the Pacific","t":"For thirty years, a **postman** in a small coastal town delivered letters between two families separated by the **Pacific** Ocean. One family lived in China, the other in Canada. The story of their friendship has become one of the most **mysterious** and moving tales in the town.\n\nIt began in 1960, when a young Canadian **pilot** crashed near the town during a storm. A local **poet** and his wife found him and cared for him until he recovered. After returning home, the pilot sent a thank-you letter. Thus began a correspondence that lasted for decades.\n\nThe letters were full of life. The poet wrote about village events, the weather and his daughter's dream of becoming a **physicist**. The pilot described his family, his work and his love of painting. Once, he sent a **portrait** of the poet, painted from an old photograph. It was painted on **pink** paper, and the poet hung it proudly in his living room.\n\nThere were difficult times too. During periods of political tension, letters were delayed, and some were lost. The poet was **obliged|oblige** to **obey** strict rules about foreign contact. Rumours that he was a spy were complete **nonsense**, but they caused him great pain. A government **personnel** officer even asked him to **postpone** his replies.\n\nThe families finally met in 1995, when the pilot's son visited China. The meeting was described in the local newspaper as a rare **occurrence** of friendship across borders. Many of the letters are now kept in one place, **namely** the town's history museum, though only a **partial** collection survives. In the **preceding** years, the story had even been mentioned in the national **parliament** as an example of cultural exchange. A local **painter** created a mural based on the letters, and a **portable** exhibition now travels to schools."},"119":{"w":["precision","prejudice","prohibit","pronounce","proportional","protective","provision","puzzle","quarterly","rage","refute","reign","reliability","reporter","residence","restrain","retain","revenge","revise","ridiculous"],"title":"Behind the Headlines","t":"Every day, a team of journalists at a national newspaper works to bring readers accurate news. Ms. Lin, a senior **reporter**, has worked there for twenty years. She believes that good journalism requires **precision**, honesty and a strong sense of responsibility.\n\nHer work involves careful research. Before publishing a story, she checks every fact and **revises|revise** her articles several times. She tries to **restrain** her personal opinions and avoid **prejudice**. \"Our job is to inform, not to take **revenge** on people we dislike,\" she says.\n\nJournalism has faced many challenges. Fake news spreads quickly online, and some readers believe **ridiculous** stories without question. Ms. Lin spends much of her time trying to **refute** false claims. She admits that the task can feel like an endless **puzzle**.\n\nThe newspaper also faces economic pressure. Advertising income has fallen, and the paper now publishes a **quarterly** magazine to attract new readers. Managers work hard to **retain** talented staff and improve the paper's **reliability**. Some laws **prohibit** journalists from revealing certain information, and editors must follow these rules carefully.\n\nMs. Lin has covered many major stories. She once interviewed an elderly king about his long **reign** over a small island nation, and she has reported on public **rage** after unpopular decisions; one of her articles led the government to improve its **provision** of emergency aid. Colleagues say she can **pronounce** almost any foreign name correctly. Her **residence** is a small flat filled with books. \"A free press is **protective** of democracy,\" she says. \"Its power must be **proportional** to its responsibility.\""},"120":{"w":["rival","rotate","row","safely","scan","scream","servant","sheet","shelter","shield","shoot","simplify","singular","solely","sometime","sparkle","specify","specimen","spiritual","staircase"],"title":"The Old Mansion","t":"On a hill above our town stands an old mansion that has been empty for decades. Local children say it is haunted, and nobody dares to go inside after dark. Last summer, my friends and I decided to explore it **sometime** before school started.\n\nWe approached **safely** in daylight, carrying torches and a camera. The front door was locked, so we climbed through a broken window. Inside, everything was covered in dust. A huge **staircase** curved up to the second floor, and white **sheets|sheet** covered the old furniture.\n\nWe moved slowly from room to room. In one, we found a painting of a woman with a **spiritual** expression, her eyes seeming to **sparkle** in the torchlight. In another, a cabinet held a strange **specimen** in a glass jar. My friend tried to **scan** the room with his phone's camera. Suddenly, we heard a loud **scream**. We all froze — but it was only a bird trapped in the chimney.\n\nLater, we learned the mansion's history. It had belonged to a wealthy merchant whose **rival** had ruined his business. The merchant's **servant** stayed in the house for years after his death, using it as a **shelter** during storms. Local records **specify** that the family once owned a large estate, and their coat of arms showed a lion on a **shield**.\n\nThe adventure taught us something **singular**: many frightening stories have simple explanations. We **simplify** fears by understanding them. The town now plans to restore the mansion, and workers will **rotate** shifts to finish the job quickly. Film crews have already asked to **shoot** a movie there. We hope to sit in the front **row** at the opening. Our adventure was **solely** for fun, but it gave us a story we will never forget."},"121":{"w":["stare","startle","stomach","stupid","sufficiently","sum","suspect","suspicion","sweat","tenant","terrible","tire","tongue","trail","twist","uncertain","undoubtedly","unload","unpleasant","unwilling"],"title":"The Mountain Trail","t":"Last autumn, my brother and I decided to hike a famous mountain **trail** in the west of China. We had read that the route was difficult, but we were confident. We packed food, water and warm clothes, and we set off early in the morning.\n\nThe first few hours were pleasant. But as the path grew steeper, we began to **tire**. The trail began to **twist** sharply between rocks, and our clothes were soon soaked with **sweat**. My **stomach** started to hurt, and my **tongue** felt dry. We wondered whether we had prepared **sufficiently** for such a long climb.\n\nIn the afternoon, the weather changed. Dark clouds gathered, and a **terrible** storm began. We sheltered under a rock, feeling **uncertain** about what to do next. A sudden crack of thunder **startled|startle** us both, and we began to **suspect** that we had taken the wrong path.\n\nLuckily, we met an old farmer leading a donkey down the mountain. At first, he looked at us with **suspicion**, perhaps thinking we were foolish tourists. Then he smiled. \"Don't **stare** at the sky,\" he said. \"Follow me.\" He helped us **unload** our heavy bags onto his donkey and guided us to a small village.\n\nThat night, we stayed with a **tenant** farmer and his family. They refused to accept money, though we offered them a small **sum**. \"It would be **stupid** to let you sleep in the rain,\" the farmer laughed. My brother was **unwilling** to leave the next morning. The experience was **unpleasant** at times, but it was **undoubtedly** one of the best adventures of our lives."},"122":{"w":["valid","vice","visible","vivid","weakness","wheel","yell","admission","aluminum","automobile","band","beloved","bend","bone","bush","cave","cease","communist","conductor","container"],"title":"The Band from the Cave","t":"In a small town in Eastern Europe, a group of teenagers formed a rock **band** in an old **cave** outside the town. During the time when the country was a **communist** state, the cave had been used to store food. Now it became their rehearsal room, full of music and **vivid** paintings on the walls.\n\nThe band's leader, a shy boy named Piotr, wrote all the songs. His **beloved** grandmother had taught him to play the guitar. He admitted that his greatest **weakness** was stage fright. Before every performance, he would **bend** over his guitar, unable to look at the audience. Yet when he began to sing, his fear seemed to **cease**.\n\nThe band faced many challenges. They had little money and practised with old, broken instruments. Their only transport was a rusty **automobile** with a broken **wheel**. Once, they had to perform with a guitar made partly from **aluminum** and wood. But their passion was **visible** to everyone who heard them.\n\nTheir big break came when a famous orchestra **conductor** heard them play at a local festival. He offered them **admission** to a music competition in the capital. They won second prize, and their song was played on national radio. Fans began to **yell** their names in the streets.\n\nToday, the band still practises in the cave. A local business donated a shipping **container** to store their equipment, and they planted a **bush** of roses at the entrance. A teacher in the town once warned that music was a **vice** that would distract students. Now, he admits that he was wrong. \"Their success is **valid** proof that passion matters,\" he says. Piotr's grandmother says she feels it in her **bones|bone**: the band has a bright future."},"123":{"w":["corridor","cotton","crawl","cubic","dialect","dispose","ditch","dorm","dormitory","doubtful","drama","drift","electrical","enormous","entry","evacuate","evaluate","exhaustion","famine","flu"],"title":"Life in a Student Dormitory","t":"For many university students, moving into a **dormitory** is their first experience of living away from home. At my university, four students share a small room, and dozens of rooms open onto a long **corridor**. Life in the **dorm** is noisy, crowded and full of **drama**.\n\nMy first weeks were difficult. My roommates came from different provinces, and each spoke a different **dialect**. At first, communication was confusing, and I felt **doubtful** about whether we would get along. Over time, we learned each other's languages and habits. Now we are close friends.\n\nThe dormitory had its problems. The **electrical** system often failed, and once a fire alarm forced us to **evacuate** the building at midnight. We stood outside in the cold, wrapped in **cotton** blankets, while firefighters checked the building. Thankfully, it was a false alarm.\n\nExams brought their own challenges. During the final weeks, students studied late into the night, and **exhaustion** was common. Some caught the **flu**, and the university clinic was full. My roommate would **crawl** into bed at three in the morning after hours of study. Teachers advised us to **evaluate** our study habits and rest more.\n\nThere were happy moments too. On weekends, we cooked together, using a tiny stove and an **enormous** pot, and we learned to **dispose** of rubbish properly. Behind the building, some students dug a small **ditch** and grew vegetables. One friend's story about a historical **famine** won first prize as an **entry** in a writing contest, while another spent hours calculating **cubic** volumes for his maths class. Looking back, I realise those years helped me **drift** gently into adulthood."},"124":{"w":["funny","furious","gang","govern","greeting","gun","horizontal","housewife","illegal","impressive","indication","inefficient","install","jazz","lately","liberate","logical","mercy","mild","mushroom"],"title":"The Jazz Club","t":"In an old street in Shanghai, there is a small **jazz** club that has been open since the 1930s. Its walls are covered with photographs of famous musicians, and its atmosphere is **mild** and relaxed. Visitors from around the world come to listen to music and enjoy the club's **impressive** history.\n\nThe club's owner, Mr. Wang, is a cheerful man who welcomes every guest with a warm **greeting**. He tells **funny** stories about musicians who played there decades ago. He says that the club survived difficult periods, when jazz was considered foreign and even **illegal**. \"Music cannot be **governed|govern** by politics,\" he says.\n\nThe club has faced other challenges. **Lately**, high rents and **inefficient** heating systems have increased costs. Mr. Wang had to **install** new equipment and repair the old wooden floor. Some neighbours were **furious** about the noise, but he reached an agreement with them, promising to close earlier on weekdays.\n\nThe music itself is full of life. Musicians **liberate** themselves from strict rules, improvising freely. A **housewife** who sings at the club on Fridays has become a local star. Her songs, full of emotion and **mercy**, move audiences to tears. Critics say there is every **indication** that jazz is becoming popular again among young people.\n\nMr. Wang's philosophy is simple and **logical**: good music brings people together. He compares the growth of jazz to a **mushroom**: once it finds the right conditions, it spreads quickly. A **horizontal** banner above the stage reads \"All are welcome.\" He jokes that the club has seen everything, from **gang** fights in the 1930s to marriage proposals. \"We even had a film crew shoot a scene with a fake **gun** once,\" he laughs."},"125":{"w":["nitrogen","oral","ore","overdue","pan","parcel","paste","photographic","picnic","pillar","pit","prayer","pretend","proceed","radiation","rear","receiver","renew","reservation","rotation"],"title":"A Summer Picnic Gone Wrong","t":"Last summer, my family planned a **picnic** in a park near a lake. My mother packed sandwiches, fruit and a **pan** of fried noodles. My father carried a heavy **parcel** containing blankets and cups. We expected a peaceful afternoon, but things did not go as planned.\n\nFirst, we arrived late because our car needed repairs; its service was long **overdue**. Then, as we unpacked, my little brother **pretended|pretend** to be a pirate and fell into a muddy **pit** near the lake. We had to clean him with water and a tissue. My mother laughed, saying it was a perfect **photographic** moment.\n\nWe decided to **proceed** with the picnic anyway. We sat under an old stone **pillar** that once belonged to a temple. My grandmother whispered a short **prayer** before eating, a tradition she has kept all her life. My father spread **paste** on the bread for sandwiches, and we enjoyed the food despite everything.\n\nLater, a park ranger stopped to talk with us. He explained that the lake had been polluted by fertiliser containing **nitrogen**, and the park was trying to **renew** its water system. He also mentioned that the area once had iron **ore** mines, and that scientists still check the soil for **radiation** from an old power plant nearby. The **rotation** of crops on nearby farms had improved soil quality.\n\nOn the way home, my father realised we had forgotten to cancel our dinner **reservation**, and he called the restaurant with the phone **receiver** pressed between his shoulder and ear. My brother sat in the **rear** seat, laughing about every mistake. A week later, he gave an **oral** report about the day at school, and his classmates loved it. Despite everything, it was one of the funniest days of the summer."},"126":{"w":["sale","settlement","shallow","shell","shrug","skim","sleepy","soak","soda","soup","sour","squeeze","stripe","stroke","subsequent","subway","sudden","technician","thief","throat"],"title":"The Seaside Café","t":"In a small fishing **settlement** on the east coast, there is a café run by a woman named Mrs. Gao. It sits beside the **shallow** bay, where children collect **shell** after shell at low tide. Her café is famous for its fish **soup**, cooked slowly with herbs and a little **sour** plum sauce.\n\nMrs. Gao was not always a cook. For twenty years, she worked as a **technician** in a factory in the city, repairing machines. Then, after a **sudden** illness — a mild **stroke** that left her weak for months — she decided to change her life. \"I wanted to live by the sea,\" she says with a **shrug**. \"Life is too short.\"\n\nThe café opened ten years ago. At first, business was slow, and she struggled to **squeeze** a profit from the few customers. But **subsequent** years brought tourists, who arrived by train and **subway** from the city. Now, on busy weekends, the café is full from morning to night.\n\nMrs. Gao loves her customers. Fishermen come in early, still **sleepy**, for hot tea. Children order cold **soda** after swimming, and visitors **soak** up the sunshine on the terrace. Each table has an umbrella with red and white **stripes|stripe**. When she has time, she likes to **skim** the newspaper and chat with regulars.\n\nThere have been a few problems. Once, a **thief** stole money from the cash box, and another time a storm damaged the roof. But Mrs. Gao stays positive. She holds a special **sale** every autumn, offering half-price meals to local families. Her voice is often tired by evening, and she drinks honey tea to soothe her **throat**. \"The sea gives me strength,\" she says."},"127":{"w":["tobacco","tolerance","triumph","trumpet","tune","velvet","verb","vinegar","violet","waiter","whatsoever","wheat","wrist","yawn","accidental","accordingly","acquaintance","acquisition","acute","admire"],"title":"The Old Musician","t":"In a small town in the south, an old musician named Mr. Zhao plays the **trumpet** every evening in the main square. He has done this for forty years, and his music has become part of the town's life. Even those who do not know his name recognise his favourite **tune**.\n\nMr. Zhao's story is full of **triumph** and struggle. As a young man, he played in a famous orchestra, wearing a **velvet** jacket on stage. But an **accidental** injury to his **wrist** ended his career. **Accordingly**, he returned to his hometown and became a music teacher. \"I lost one dream but found another,\" he says.\n\nHis students **admire** his patience and **tolerance**. He never criticises harshly, even when they play badly. He compares music to language: each note is like a **verb**, giving action to a song. Many former students have become professional musicians.\n\nMr. Zhao lives a simple life. He grows **wheat** and vegetables in a small garden and keeps a bunch of **violet** flowers by his window. He rarely eats out, but when he does, he chats with every **waiter** in the town's only restaurant. He avoids **tobacco** and drinks a little **vinegar** mixed with warm water, a local remedy he believes keeps him healthy.\n\nHis daily performance is a local ritual. Children **yawn** after long school days, then gather around him as he begins to play. He has no **acquisition** of wealth **whatsoever**, but he has gained the respect of an entire town. A new **acquaintance** once asked whether he regretted leaving the orchestra. \"Not at all,\" he said with **acute** honesty. \"Here, my music belongs to everyone.\""},"128":{"w":["advisable","alloy","aloud","amaze","amuse","ant","appointment","arithmetic","army","ascribe","assembly","atmospheric","atom","atomic","attentive","auto","autobiography","bake","balloon","bang"],"title":"The Science Fair","t":"Every spring, our city holds a science fair for secondary school students. Hundreds of young people present projects in a large **assembly** hall, and visitors walk between tables, amazed by the creativity on display. This year's fair was the biggest yet.\n\nThe projects covered every subject. One student built a model of an **atom** using colourful balls and wire. Another explained **atomic** energy, showing how power stations work. A team studied **atmospheric** pollution, measuring air quality near busy roads. Their results, they said, would **amaze** anyone who thinks city air is clean.\n\nSome projects were simple but clever. A girl studied how an **ant** colony finds food, observing the insects for weeks in a glass box. A boy demonstrated basic **arithmetic** using a homemade machine. Another student tested a light **alloy** for building bicycles. He worked so hard on his **auto** design that he called it \"my second home.\"\n\nThe judges were **attentive** and kind. They asked thoughtful questions and encouraged each student to explain their ideas **aloud**. One judge, a retired engineer who had served in the **army**, said he **ascribed|ascribe** the students' success to their curiosity. He also read passages from his **autobiography** about his own childhood experiments.\n\nThe fair also included fun activities. Children decorated a giant **balloon**, and volunteers **baked|bake** cakes for visitors. Suddenly, a loud **bang** made everyone jump; a student's experiment had exploded harmlessly. Everyone laughed. Organisers said it was **advisable** for students to book an **appointment** with a mentor before next year's fair. Many students said they were **amused|amuse** and inspired by the day."},"129":{"w":["bare","baseball","bathe","beard","beast","beg","beneath","bind","bite","bomb","boot","brake","breadth","brisk","bull","bunch","cable","cafeteria","cage","camera"],"title":"A Day at the Farm Show","t":"Every autumn, farmers from across the region gather for the annual farm show. It is a **brisk**, cheerful event, full of animals, music and food. Visitors walk among rows of tents, admiring prize animals and buying fresh produce.\n\nThe highlight of the show is the cattle competition. Farmers proudly lead their animals into the ring, where judges examine them carefully. One farmer, a tall man with a long grey **beard**, showed a huge **bull** that won first prize. The animal looked like a gentle **beast**, though nobody dared to touch it.\n\nChildren love the show. They watch **baseball** games, play in the hay and feed baby animals kept in a **cage** of wood. One girl tried to **beg** her parents for a puppy. Another child was warned not to let a goat **bite** him. A photographer with a large **camera** captured all the fun. Some children even ate lunch at the show's crowded **cafeteria**, which served hot soup and bread.\n\nNot everything went smoothly. A sudden storm damaged a power **cable**, and the lights went out for an hour. A tractor's **brake** failed on a slope, though nobody was hurt. Organisers had to **bind** loose tents with rope to keep them from blowing away. Later, workers discovered an old **bomb** from the war buried **beneath** a field; experts removed it safely.\n\nBy evening, the sun was setting, and families went home with a **bunch** of flowers or a bag of apples. Farmers removed their muddy **boots|boot** and relaxed. Some rolled up their sleeves, arms **bare** in the warm evening air, and went to **bathe** in the river. One farmer said the show displayed the full **breadth** of rural life."},"130":{"w":["candy","cannon","carriage","carrier","chairman","chapter","cherry","chess","chicken","childish","choke","cigarette","circuit","circulate","civilian","clap","clarify","cliff","colonel","column"],"title":"The Old Fort on the Cliff","t":"On a **cliff** above the sea stands an old fort built in the nineteenth century. Its walls are made of stone, and two rusty **cannons|cannon** still point towards the water. Today, the fort is a museum, and visitors come from across the country to learn about its history.\n\nThe museum's guide, a retired **colonel**, tells stories with great enthusiasm. He explains how soldiers lived in the fort, how messages were carried by horse-drawn **carriage** and how **civilian** families supplied food. Each room is a new **chapter** in the fort's history.\n\nThe fort has a surprising past. During one war, a supply ship, a large **carrier** of food and weapons, was trapped in the harbour. Soldiers had to **circulate** messages secretly, and rumours spread quickly. Some soldiers smoked a **cigarette** each night to calm their nerves, while others played **chess** to pass the time.\n\nThe museum now offers modern displays. An electrical **circuit** powers interactive screens, and a **column** of light shines from the tower at night. Visitors **clap** when the guide fires a small replica cannon. Children enjoy feeding the **chicken** in the courtyard and eating **cherry** pies from the museum café. Some even buy **candy** shaped like cannonballs.\n\nNot everyone approves of the museum. The local council **chairman** once called some exhibits **childish**, saying they did not take history seriously. The colonel smiled and tried to **clarify** his aims: \"History should be fun, not something that makes you **choke** with boredom.\""},"131":{"w":["comb","comparative","compass","compress","condense","conquer","considerate","contest","continual","contradiction","copper","cord","corresponding","cousin","cream","crime","crown","cruel","damp","dare"],"title":"The Treasure Hunt","t":"Last summer, my **cousin** organised a treasure hunt for the children in our village. It was a **contest** between four teams, each armed with a map, a **compass** and a list of clues. The prize was a small **crown** made of cardboard and painted gold.\n\nThe clues were clever. One sent the children to an old **copper** bell in the temple; another hid a note inside a jar of **cream** at the village shop. Teams had to **comb** through gardens, fields and the riverbank. Some clues contained a deliberate **contradiction**, forcing the children to think carefully. \"If the clue says go north but the map points east, which do you trust?\" my cousin asked.\n\nThe weather was **damp**, and the children's shoes soon became muddy. But nobody complained. One team found a long **cord** tied to a tree, leading to a hidden box. Another discovered that the clues were placed in **corresponding** positions on the map, like a puzzle. The older children helped to **condense** long instructions into short notes for the little ones.\n\nMy cousin is a **considerate** organiser. She made sure that no child felt left out, and she stopped the game whenever someone got tired. When one boy said that losing was **cruel**, she reminded him that the goal was fun, not victory. \"You don't have to **conquer** everyone,\" she said. \"Just enjoy the adventure.\"\n\nThe hunt ended at the village square. The winning team proudly wore the crown, though everyone received a small prize. My cousin even joked that she had committed a **crime** by eating half the prize sweets beforehand. The children laughed. Many said they would **dare** to try an even harder hunt next year. The **continual** laughter that afternoon showed how much fun everyone had, and my cousin later managed to **compress** all the photos into one short video for the parents. **Comparative** studies, she told me, suggest that such games improve children's teamwork."},"132":{"w":["deceive","deduce","defeat","definite","delegate","delight","descend","dial","diamond","disable","disapprove","disgust","displease","dissolve","divorce","dock","downstairs","drag","drown","drum"],"title":"The Diamond Thief","t":"Last year, a valuable **diamond** disappeared from a jewellery shop near the **dock** in an old port city. The owner was shocked; nobody had seen the thief, and the security cameras showed nothing unusual. Police were called, and a young detective was assigned to the case.\n\nThe detective began by examining every detail. She noticed that the shop's alarm had been **disabled|disable** shortly before the theft. From this, she was able to **deduce** that the thief knew the shop well. \"Someone tried to **deceive** us,\" she said. \"It wasn't a random crime.\"\n\nHer investigation led her to the owner's former business partner, who had recently gone through a bitter **divorce**. He had **disapproved|disapprove** of the owner's decisions and was **displeased|displease** about losing his share of the business. When the detective visited his flat, she heard someone hurrying **downstairs**. The suspect tried to escape, but officers caught him near the river.\n\nThe suspect denied everything at first. But the detective had **definite** proof: his fingerprints were on the shop's phone, which he had used to **dial** a secret number. Finally, he confessed. He admitted that he had planned to **dissolve** the diamond's setting and sell the stone abroad. The court treated the case seriously, and a **delegate** from the jewellers' association attended the trial.\n\nThe case brought great **delight** to the shop owner, who had feared the stone was lost forever. The detective admitted feeling **disgust** at the partner's betrayal; he had even tried to **drag** the jewellery box into the river to **drown** the evidence. His arrest was a clear **defeat** for organised crime in the city, and officials say crime rates continue to **descend**. To celebrate, the police band gave a special **drum** performance at the station's annual party."},"133":{"w":["drunk","dumb","dump","duration","dying","eagle","earthquake","edition","elect","election","electronics","elevator","enlarge","equation","establishment","evaporate","examination","excess","expel","farther"],"title":"The Night of the Earthquake","t":"Two years ago, a strong **earthquake** struck a city in southwest China at midnight. Buildings shook, windows broke and people rushed into the streets in their pyjamas. The **duration** of the main shock was less than a minute, but it felt like hours.\n\nMy aunt lived on the tenth floor of an apartment block. When the shaking began, the **elevator** stopped working, and she had to climb down the stairs in darkness. \"I thought I was **dying**,\" she remembers. Outside, an **eagle** that lived on the roof flew away into the night.\n\nThe city's emergency services responded quickly. Engineers checked buildings, and workers began to **dump** broken bricks and glass into trucks. Scientists later explained that the earthquake followed a simple **equation**: energy built up along a fault line until the rock broke. Some people asked whether the disaster could have been predicted.\n\nThe earthquake also had political effects. In the following **election**, citizens voted for leaders who promised better building standards. The newly **elected|elect** mayor ordered an **examination** of every school and hospital. Several construction companies were **expelled|expel** from public projects because they had used poor materials. Officials also announced the **establishment** of a new safety agency.\n\nToday, the city has recovered. A new **edition** of the national building code requires stronger designs, and engineers use advanced **electronics** to monitor buildings. The government plans to **enlarge** parks so that people have safe open spaces, and it warns against **excess** construction in dangerous areas. My aunt has moved **farther** from the city centre. She says the fear has **evaporated|evaporate**, but she will never forget that night. Neither will her neighbour, a man who was **drunk** when the shaking began and had to be helped outside. Some people thought it was **dumb** to keep sleeping in tents for weeks afterwards, but many families did."},"134":{"w":["feast","feather","feeble","fertile","fisherman","fist","flee","flesh","fluent","fold","fool","foolish","foremost","fortunately","fragment","frontier","furnish","garbage","gasoline","gauge"],"title":"The Fisherman's Story","t":"Old Mr. Lin has been a **fisherman** for fifty years. Every morning, he sails out from a small harbour on the southern **frontier** of the province, returning at noon with his catch. His hands are rough, and his **fist** is as strong as a younger man's.\n\nHe loves to tell stories. \"When I was young,\" he says, \"the sea was full of fish. We held a **feast** every evening.\" Today, the catch is smaller, and he worries about the future. He blames pollution and **garbage** from cities, as well as boats that burn too much **gasoline** and damage the sea.\n\nMr. Lin is wise but humble. He never calls anyone a **fool**, though he admits he was **foolish** as a young man. **Fortunately**, an old fisherman taught him patience and respect for nature. \"The sea is like **fertile** land,\" he says. \"If you take too much, it becomes **feeble** and empty.\"\n\nHis daily life is simple. He mends nets, **folds|fold** his sails carefully and cooks fresh fish **flesh** with ginger. He keeps a seagull's **feather** in his hat for luck. He is not **fluent** in modern technology, but his grandson has helped him **furnish** his small house with solar lights. He uses an old **gauge** to check the weather.\n\nMany young people **flee** the village for city jobs, but Mr. Lin's grandson plans to stay. \"Protecting the sea is our **foremost** duty,\" the young man says. Recently, he found a **fragment** of an ancient pot on the beach, which a museum identified as two thousand years old. For Mr. Lin, it was proof that people have lived from the sea for centuries."},"135":{"w":["gentle","gently","glitter","glorious","glow","glue","goat","golden","golf","grandfather","greet","habitual","hatch","heading","heap","heaven","heel","herd","hero","hesitate"],"title":"My Grandfather's Farm","t":"My **grandfather** has lived on the same farm in the hills for seventy years. He is a **gentle** man with a warm smile, and he moves **gently** among his animals, speaking to each one by name. His farm is small, but it is full of life.\n\nEvery morning, he wakes before dawn to milk the **goat** and feed the chickens. He keeps a **herd** of cows in the field behind the house. In spring, he helps the hens **hatch** their eggs, and in autumn, he collects a huge **heap** of apples from the orchard. \"Farming is not a job,\" he says. \"It is a way of life.\"\n\nMy grandfather is a **habitual** early riser and rarely **hesitates|hesitate** to work hard. Yet he also knows how to enjoy simple pleasures. In the evening, he sits outside watching the sun **glow** over the hills, turning the fields **golden**. \"This is my **heaven**,\" he says. The stars **glitter** above the farm, and the night is silent.\n\nHe is something of a local **hero**. During a flood ten years ago, he helped neighbours move their animals to safety. Newspapers wrote about him under the **heading** \"The Farmer Who Saved a Village.\" He laughs at such praise. \"I just did what anyone would do,\" he says.\n\nVisitors often come to **greet** him and hear his stories. He fixes broken tools with **glue** and wire, and he walks long distances despite pain in his **heel**. He tried playing **golf** once with a city friend and found it boring. \"The farm is more **glorious** than any golf course,\" he jokes."},"136":{"w":["highway","holy","honey","hook","hopeful","hopeless","horn","hostess","humid","identical","idle","immense","incident","indirect","inn","insert","inspection","instruct","insurance","insure"],"title":"The Inn by the Highway","t":"On a quiet stretch of **highway** in the mountains of Yunnan stands a small **inn** run by a cheerful **hostess** named Mrs. Bai. Truck drivers, tourists and travelling sellers stop there for meals and rest. Her honey cakes, made with **honey** from local bees, are famous along the road.\n\nThe inn is simple but welcoming. Each room has an **identical** wooden bed, and a **hook** on the door for coats. The climate is **humid** in summer, so Mrs. Bai keeps fans in every room. Outside, drivers sound their **horns|horn** to greet her as they pass.\n\nMrs. Bai's life has not always been easy. Ten years ago, an **incident** on the highway nearly destroyed her business: a truck crashed into the building at night. Without **insurance**, she would have lost everything. Fortunately, she had decided to **insure** the inn the year before. \"I was **hopeful** that I would never need it,\" she says.\n\nThe government now carries out a regular safety **inspection** of buildings along the highway. Inspectors **instruct** owners on fire safety, and some old houses have been closed. Mrs. Bai welcomes the rules. \"Being **idle** about safety is dangerous,\" she says.\n\nThe inn has an **immense** view of the valley below. Nearby stands a **holy** mountain where pilgrims climb each spring. Some guests leave notes of thanks, which Mrs. Bai **inserts|insert** into a large photo album. She rarely feels **hopeless**, even during hard times. \"Kindness has an **indirect** reward,\" she says. \"People remember you.\""},"137":{"w":["intensive","interruption","interval","intimate","invade","invasion","invitation","irregular","jealous","joyful","keeper","keyboard","lamb","laser","layout","leading","lean","lemon","lest","lever"],"title":"The Zookeeper","t":"Mr. Zhou has worked as a **keeper** at the city zoo for twenty years. He looks after animals from around the world, from tiny birds to a young **lamb** born last spring. His job is demanding, but he describes it as **joyful**.\n\nHis days follow an **intensive** routine. He feeds the animals at regular **intervals|interval**, cleans their homes and checks their health. Some animals have **irregular** eating habits, so he records everything on a computer **keyboard** in his small office. \"Every animal has its own personality,\" he says.\n\nMr. Zhou has a deep and **intimate** understanding of animal behaviour. He knows when a monkey is **jealous** of another, or when an elephant is unhappy. Once, a monkey stole his lunch, a **lemon** and a sandwich, and refused to give it back. \"That was a lesson in patience,\" he laughs.\n\nThe zoo has changed over the years. Its old **layout** of small cages has been replaced by large, natural areas. Scientists from a **leading** university use **laser** technology to measure animals' movements without disturbing them. A simple **lever** system opens doors between enclosures.\n\nNot everything is peaceful. Some animals **invade** each other's territory, and keepers must separate them carefully. During one storm, a tree fell and caused an **invasion** of water into the reptile house, leading to a long **interruption** of visits. Mr. Zhou often has to **lean** over fences to check on animals, **lest** they become sick without anyone noticing. Last month, he received an **invitation** to speak at an international conference about animal care."},"138":{"w":["liar","liberty","license","lid","likewise","limb","lip","liquor","loaf","loosen","lord","magnet","mainland","marble","marry","mat","materialism","maximum","meadow","meantime"],"title":"A Weekend in the Meadow","t":"Last spring, my friends and I spent a weekend camping in a wide **meadow** near a mountain village. We brought tents, a picnic **mat**, a **loaf** of bread and some fruit. The weather was perfect, and wild flowers covered the ground.\n\nOn the first evening, we met an old shepherd who lived nearby. He told us stories about the **mainland** and his travels as a young man. He said he had once worked for a wealthy **lord** in a big house with **marble** floors. \"Money never brought him happiness,\" he said. \"**Likewise**, it won't bring you happiness either.\"\n\nThe shepherd was full of opinions. He disliked modern **materialism** and believed that people should live simply. He also said that every **liar** eventually reveals himself. When my friend asked about his family, he smiled and said that he had decided not to **marry**. \"**Liberty** is my wife,\" he joked.\n\nThe next day, we explored the hills. My friend hurt a **limb** when he slipped on a rock, and my lower **lip** cracked in the dry sun. We put a **lid** on our cooking pot to keep insects out and used a small **magnet** to hold our map to the metal box. In the **meantime**, the shepherd prepared tea over a fire.\n\nBefore we left, the shepherd showed us his old fishing **license**, issued fifty years ago. He offered us a glass of local **liquor**, but we politely refused. As we packed, I had to **loosen** the ropes of our tent, which had been tied too tightly. That weekend taught us to value simple things. We walked the **maximum** distance our legs could manage, and we slept better than we had in months."},"139":{"w":["mechanically","melt","metal","microphone","midday","midnight","midst","minus","moan","moisture","motion","multiply","naked","neat","nerve","normally","northeast","northwest","notify","nowhere"],"title":"A Midnight Concert","t":"Last summer, our town held a special concert that began at **midnight** and ended at sunrise. It was an unusual idea, but hundreds of people came. Musicians played on a stage in the middle of a park, surrounded by trees and lanterns.\n\nPreparations took weeks. Engineers set up lights, cables and a **microphone** for every singer. The stage was built of **metal** and wood, and workers checked it carefully to avoid accidents. Organisers had to **notify** residents in the **northeast** and **northwest** of town about the noise.\n\nThe concert itself was magical. In the **midst** of the darkness, the music seemed to **melt** into the night air. Some songs were slow and sad, and some listeners began to **moan** along with the singers. Others danced with energy, their bodies in constant **motion**.\n\nThere were small problems. The night was humid, and **moisture** in the air affected some instruments. The temperature fell to almost **minus** five degrees near dawn, and people wrapped themselves in blankets. A child accidentally walked onto the stage, and musicians laughed as he waved at the crowd.\n\nBy **midday** the next day, the park was clean again, thanks to volunteers. Organisers said the event helped to **multiply** community spirit. \"**Normally**, people go to bed early,\" one organiser said. \"This time, they stayed up together.\" The concert's design was **neat** and well organised, and the stage crew worked almost **mechanically** through the night. Some performers admitted that their **nerves|nerve** were tested by the late hour. Stars were visible to the **naked** eye, and many people said they had never seen such a beautiful sky. \"There's **nowhere** I'd rather be,\" one woman said."},"140":{"w":["nuisance","nursery","objection","omit","onion","operational","optical","organ","outlook","outward","owing","oxygen","painful","pants","parade","parallel","partially","paw","pea","pearl"],"title":"Life on the Pearl Farm","t":"On a quiet island in the south, a family runs a small farm that produces **pearls|pearl**. The farm has existed for three generations, and today it is one of the few remaining traditional pearl farms in the region. Visitors come to learn about the delicate process of growing pearls.\n\nThe work is slow and careful. Workers place a tiny bead inside each oyster, which slowly forms a pearl around it. The oysters need clean water and plenty of **oxygen**, so the family monitors the sea every day. The process can be **painful** for the oysters, and the family tries to treat them gently.\n\nThe farm faces challenges. Rising sea temperatures have damaged some beds, and **owing** to pollution, the farm's output has fallen. The family's **outlook** is cautiously positive, however. They have built a small **nursery** to raise young oysters and protect them from disease.\n\nVisitors enjoy the experience. Children watch workers open oysters, and some even find a pearl themselves. The farm's café serves soup made with **onion** and **pea**, and the family's dog waves its **paw** at visitors. **Partially** hidden behind the trees is a small museum that shows the history of pearl farming.\n\nSome local residents have raised an **objection** to tourism, saying that crowds of visitors are a **nuisance**. The family has listened and now limits group sizes, and guides are careful not to **omit** information about protecting the sea. The farm's **operational** costs are high, but visitor support keeps it open.\n\nEach spring, the island holds a **parade** to celebrate the harvest. Children march in simple **pants** and sandals, and the family's eldest son plays the **organ** at the island church. The family's work also runs **parallel** to university research on the **optical** properties of pearls, which reflect light in unusual ways. A pearl's **outward** appearance is simple, the family says, but it hides years of patient work."},"141":{"w":["pepper","permanently","permit","physics","pie","pipe","plough","plunge","poetry","poison","poisonous","porridge","pot","pray","precise","preliminary","prevail","princess","principal","prison"],"title":"The School Principal","t":"Mr. Xu has been the **principal** of a small village school for twenty years. Every morning, he arrives early, makes a **pot** of tea and walks around the classrooms to greet students. He believes that a school should feel like a family.\n\nBefore becoming principal, Mr. Xu taught **physics** and **poetry**, two subjects he says are more alike than people think. \"Both require **precise** thinking and imagination,\" he explains. He still teaches one class a week, and students love his lessons.\n\nThe school faces many challenges. Its old water **pipe** often breaks in winter, and heavy rain can **plunge** the playground into mud. Last year, a **preliminary** survey found that some paint in the classrooms contained **poisonous** chemicals. Mr. Xu immediately closed the rooms and arranged repairs, explaining that even a small amount of **poison** could harm children.\n\nHe works closely with local farmers, many of whom still **plough** their fields by hand. Parents bring gifts of vegetables, red **pepper** and homemade **pie**. In winter, the school kitchen serves hot **porridge** to students who walk long distances.\n\nMr. Xu believes that kindness will **prevail** over difficulty. He tells students stories about famous people who overcame hardship, from a **princess** who fought for education to a writer who studied in **prison**. Some students **pray** for good results before exams, but he reminds them that effort matters most. This year, the local government agreed to **permit** the building of a new library, which will be **permanently** open to everyone in the village."},"142":{"w":["probable","proclaim","progressive","punctual","purple","queen","quote","rabbit","radar","radioactive","railroad","rainy","realm","reception","recorder","refresh","refreshment","repetition","reproach","republic"],"title":"A Journey by Rail","t":"Last spring, I travelled across the country by **railroad**, a journey that took five days. My friends thought I was crazy; flying would have been much faster. But I wanted to see the land slowly, through a train window, and I thought it **probable** that the trip would change my view of travel.\n\nThe journey began on a **rainy** morning. I sat by the window with a voice **recorder**, planning to record my thoughts each day. The train moved through fields, forests and villages, and I watched farmers working and children playing. At every station we received a warm **reception** from local vendors offering food and drinks.\n\nThe train was full of interesting people. An old man told me stories about the years before the country became a **republic**, when it was still ruled by a **queen**, and a young engineer explained how **radar** systems track trains. A woman in a **purple** coat said she was travelling to visit her daughter, who studies **radioactive** materials at a research centre.\n\nI enjoyed the **repetition** of daily routines: waking up, drinking tea, watching the landscape. Each morning, the attendant served a light **refreshment** of fruit and bread, which never failed to **refresh** me. The train was always **punctual**, arriving at stations on time. I even saw a wild **rabbit** running beside the tracks.\n\nAt the end of the journey, I felt I had entered a new **realm** of understanding. A sign at the final station **proclaimed|proclaim** \"Welcome to the Land of Progress,\" reflecting the region's **progressive** spirit. My mother later gave a gentle **reproach**, asking why I hadn't called more often. In reply, I could only **quote** an old saying: \"The journey is the destination.\""},"143":{"w":["rescue","resign","respective","respectively","restless","restriction","retell","retreat","revolt","rhythm","rider","ridge","roar","rotten","rubber","sacred","sadness","sandwich","sandy","scarcely"],"title":"The Mountain Rescue","t":"Last winter, a group of hikers became lost on a high mountain **ridge** during a storm. The wind began to **roar**, and snow covered the trail. One hiker sent a message for help just before her phone battery died. Within an hour, a local team set off to **rescue** them.\n\nThe team was led by a former horse **rider** who had climbed the mountain for thirty years. He wore old boots with thick **rubber** soles and remained calm despite the danger. \"Panic is our enemy,\" he said. The team moved in a steady **rhythm**, checking each step on the icy rocks. The two youngest members carried the radio and the medical kit **respectively**, while the others took on their **respective** roles in navigation and first aid.\n\nStrict **restrictions|restriction** on night climbing meant the team had to stop before dark. They slept in a small **retreat** built by monks centuries ago, where the monks, who consider the mountain **sacred**, welcomed them with hot soup. It was a **restless** night; the wind never stopped, and **scarcely** anyone closed their eyes.\n\nAt sunrise, they found the hikers sheltering behind a wall of rock, cold and hungry but alive. Their food had gone **rotten** after a bag tore open, so each of them was handed a **sandwich** and a warm drink. Then the whole group began to **retreat** slowly down the mountain, along a dry **sandy** riverbed that avoided the deep snow.\n\nBack in the village, the hikers thanked their rescuers with tears of relief, though there was **sadness** too: the leader announced that he would **resign** and let younger people take over. Years earlier, villagers had nearly **revolted|revolt** when the town planned to close the team to save money. Now the hikers **retell** the story to anyone who will listen."},"144":{"w":["scholarship","scope","secondly","segment","seller","seminar","seventeen","sexual","shade","shilling","shore","shower","sightseeing","sincere","sketch","ski","slave","slavery","sleeve","slice"],"title":"A Seminar on History","t":"Last month, I attended a history **seminar** at a museum in Liverpool, a city once at the centre of the transatlantic trade in enslaved people. The speaker, a professor who had won a **scholarship** to study at Oxford, explained the history of **slavery** with great care. Her talk was **sincere** and moving.\n\nShe began by describing how a **slave** was treated as property, bought and sold like goods. Ships carried millions of Africans across the ocean in terrible conditions. She showed a **sketch** made by a sailor in the eighteenth century, depicting chained people below deck. The audience fell silent.\n\n**Secondly**, she discussed how the trade affected different **segments|segment** of society. Merchants grew rich, and the money helped to build grand houses along the **shore**. A typical slave **seller** might earn thousands of pounds in a single voyage, at a time when a worker earned only a few **shillings|shilling** a week.\n\nThe professor also explained how people fought against slavery. Campaigners collected signatures, and formerly enslaved people wrote powerful books. She connected this history to modern struggles against discrimination, including racial and **sexual** discrimination in the workplace. The **scope** of her talk was broad, but her message was clear: history shapes our present.\n\nAfter the seminar, I walked along the waterfront. Tourists were **sightseeing**, and a sudden rain **shower** sent people running for **shade** under the trees. I bought a **slice** of cake and sat by the water, thinking about the talk. A group of **seventeen** students from a local school had attended too, and they talked excitedly about **ski** trips and holidays. I rolled up my **sleeve** and wrote notes in my diary, determined to remember."},"145":{"w":["slide","slogan","sob","spear","spell","spill","splendid","spray","stadium","steel","steer","steward","stool","storm","stove","string","stumble","subsidy","subtract","suburb"],"title":"The Old Stadium","t":"In the **suburb** where I grew up, there was an old football **stadium** built of **steel** and concrete. Every Saturday, crowds gathered to watch the local team play. Fans waved flags and sang a **slogan** that everyone knew by heart: \"Never give up!\"\n\nMy grandfather worked at the stadium as a **steward**, guiding fans to their seats. He kept a small **stool** near the entrance and a **stove** in his office to make tea. When I was a child, he let me sit beside him and watch the games. I remember the **splendid** colours of the crowd and the noise of cheering.\n\nThe stadium had its share of drama. During one game, a sudden **storm** forced players to stop. Rain began to **spill** over the stands, and some fans would **slide** on the wet steps. My grandfather helped them, making sure nobody would **stumble** and fall. Once, a young boy began to **sob** after losing his parents in the crowd, and my grandfather comforted him until they were found.\n\nYears later, the city decided to close the stadium. A government **subsidy** was offered to build a modern one elsewhere. Many people were sad. My grandfather said that you cannot **subtract** memories from a place. \"This stadium has a magic **spell**,\" he said. \"It holds our history.\"\n\nToday, the old stadium is a park. A sculpture shaped like a **spear** stands at its centre, and workers **spray** water on the flowers each morning. Children fly kites on long **string** where players once ran. Watching them, I still feel my grandfather beside me, helping me **steer** through life."},"146":{"w":["suddenly","summary","sunshine","superficial","surrender","surroundings","swallow","swear","sweep","swing","tame","tape","tedious","telegraph","temple","tent","terminal","terrific","terror","thickness"],"title":"A Night Beside the Temple","t":"Last summer, my sister and I spent a weekend camping near an old mountain **temple**. We took a bus to the last **terminal** on the line and then walked for two hours, enjoying the **sunshine** and the cool forest **surroundings**. By the time we reached the temple, our legs were aching, but the view was **terrific**.\n\nSetting up the **tent** proved harder than expected. The instructions were **tedious** to read, and we had forgotten the **tape** we needed to repair a small hole. In the end, a monk who was **sweeping|sweep** the courtyard came over to help. He fixed the hole with a strip of cloth and laughed when my sister said she would **swear** never to go camping again.\n\nOver tea, the monk told us about the temple's history. During a war a century ago, soldiers had surrounded it, demanding that the monks **surrender** their food. A message sent by **telegraph** brought help just in time, and the soldiers left. \"People felt **terror** then,\" he said, \"but the walls survived.\" He showed us the **thickness** of the stone, nearly a metre wide.\n\nThat evening, **suddenly**, a strong wind began to blow. The trees started to **swing** wildly, and we watched a **swallow** dart back to its nest under the roof. A **tame** cat from the temple came into our tent and slept between us.\n\nBefore we left, I wrote a short **summary** of the trip in my notebook. A **superficial** visitor might see only old buildings, I wrote, but anyone who stays a night will find a place full of stories."},"147":{"w":["tight","timetable","timid","toast","tolerate","tour","transmit","tremble","trim","tube","tumble","turbine","unconscious","undertaking","undo","uneasy","unhappy","uniform","unkind","unstable"],"title":"Inside the Wind Farm","t":"Our school organised a **tour** of a wind farm on the coast last spring. The **timetable** was **tight**: the bus left at seven, and we had only two hours on site. Still, nobody complained, because few of us had ever stood beneath a real wind **turbine**.\n\nOur guide was a young engineer in a blue **uniform**. She explained that each turbine turns wind into electricity, which is then **transmitted|transmit** through cables to the national grid. Inside the tower, a narrow metal **tube** with a ladder led up to the top. When she offered to take volunteers up a short way, my friend Ming, who is rather **timid**, began to **tremble**. Nobody laughed; it would have been **unkind**.\n\nThe engineer said that building the farm had been a huge **undertaking**. The ground near the sea was **unstable**, so workers had to dig deep foundations. Some local residents had been **uneasy** at first and **unhappy** about the noise, but most have learned to **tolerate** it, especially since electricity prices fell.\n\nShe also told us a story from her first winter on the job. During a storm, a technician slipped on ice, **tumbled|tumble** down a slope and lay **unconscious** for several minutes. Since then, every worker must wear a safety rope and check that each knot is secure before climbing. \"If you can **undo** it with one hand, it isn't safe,\" she said.\n\nOn the way home, the teacher **trimmed|trim** our long reports into one shared article for the school newspaper. That evening, at a small party, we raised our glasses of juice and made a **toast** to clean energy."},"148":{"w":["unsuitable","unusually","upright","upstairs","utmost","utter","vacant","vague","vanity","veil","veteran","vibrate","vibration","victory","vine","violin","warmth","weapon","weary","weed"],"title":"The Old Soldier's Violin","t":"Mr. Hale lives in the flat above mine. He is a **veteran** of a war that ended long before I was born, but he still walks perfectly **upright** and greets everyone with great **warmth**. Every evening, the sound of his **violin** drifts down from **upstairs**.\n\nWhen I first moved in, the flat next to his was **vacant**, and the building felt quiet and lonely. I found the music **unusually** comforting. One day, I knocked on his door to thank him. He invited me in and showed me the instrument. \"Feel how the strings **vibrate**,\" he said, placing my hand on the wood. The gentle **vibration** surprised me.\n\nHe rarely talks about the war, and his answers to my questions are often **vague**. Once, however, he told me that he had carried a **weapon** for three years but never wanted to fire it. \"There is no glory in **victory**,\" he said, \"only relief that it is over.\" He had come home **weary** of fighting and determined to make something beautiful instead.\n\nMr. Hale has no **vanity** about his playing. He says he is an amateur who simply does his **utmost** each day. He also loves his small balcony garden, where he grows grapes on a **vine** and spends an hour each Sunday pulling out every **weed**. He told me the flat was **unsuitable** for a garden, but he made one anyway.\n\nLast week, his niece got married, and he played at the wedding. As she lifted her **veil**, he began a slow, sweet tune. The guests did not **utter** a word until the final note faded."},"149":{"w":["weekday","westward","whale","whisky","wicked","wine","worm","wrap","x-ray","youthful","zealous","aboard","accustom","ache","acid","adjective","adverb","agony","aircraft","airplane"],"title":"Whales and Words","t":"Every summer, my uncle, a **zealous** teacher of English writing, takes a break from marking essays and travels **westward** to the coast. Last year, I went with him. On a **weekday** morning, we went **aboard** a small boat to look for whales.\n\nThe sea was rough, and it took me a while to **accustom** myself to the rolling waves. My stomach began to **ache**, and for an hour I was in real **agony**. My uncle, who seemed much more **youthful** than his age, simply laughed and **wrapped|wrap** a blanket around my shoulders.\n\nThen, suddenly, a huge grey **whale** rose beside the boat. Everyone gasped. My uncle grabbed his notebook. \"Quick,\" he said, \"give me an **adjective**.\" I said \"enormous.\" \"Now an **adverb**.\" I said \"slowly.\" He wrote: \"The enormous whale rose slowly from the sea.\" Even on holiday, he could not stop teaching.\n\nThe guide told us that whales face many dangers. Pollution makes ocean water more **acid**, which harms the tiny creatures they eat. Noise from ships and low-flying **aircraft** also disturbs them. Some fishermen, he said, still view whales as **wicked** competitors for fish, though this belief is slowly changing.\n\nBack on land, I slipped on the wet steps and hurt my wrist, so we spent the afternoon at a clinic waiting for an **x-ray**. Luckily, nothing was broken. That night, my uncle ordered a glass of **wine**, while the old fisherman beside us drank **whisky** and told stories about giant sea **worms|worm**. On the **airplane** home, my uncle read my diary and corrected every adjective."},"150":{"w":["alphabet","ambulance","ampere","angel","ankle","announcer","anyhow","apparatus","applause","approximate","ashamed","aural","auxiliary","await","awfully","awkward","ax","axis","backward","badminton"],"title":"The Badminton Final","t":"The school **badminton** final was held in the sports hall on a Friday afternoon. The stands were full, and the student **announcer** read out the players' names in **alphabet** order. I was playing against Lena, last year's champion, and I was **awfully** nervous. My **aural** English exam that morning had gone well, but I could hardly remember a single question; my mind was already on the court.\n\nThe first set went badly. My movements felt **awkward**, and I kept stepping **backward** when I should have moved forward. Lena won it easily. My coach told me to stay calm and **await** the right chance to attack.\n\nIn the second set, I began to find my rhythm. The **applause** grew louder with each point. Then, while reaching for a difficult shot, Lena twisted her **ankle** and fell. The hall went silent. Teachers rushed over, and someone called an **ambulance** just in case.\n\nWhile we waited, I sat beside her. She said she felt **ashamed** of crying, but I told her there was nothing to be ashamed of. Our physics teacher, who was helping as an **auxiliary** referee, tried to cheer us up with a joke about the earth's **axis** and why players always spin the wrong way. **Anyhow**, it made her smile.\n\nLuckily, the injury was not serious. The match was stopped, and we were declared joint champions, while Lena called me her **angel** for staying by her side. Later, the school decided to **ax** the old rule that an injured finalist must give up the title.\n\nThe following Monday, the same physics teacher asked us to work out the **approximate** current, in **amperes|ampere**, flowing through a simple electrical **apparatus**. Lena, her ankle still wrapped, whispered that badminton was easier."},"151":{"w":["balcony","banner","barber","bargain","barn","barrel","basin","beggar","beginner","bench","betray","bible","biscuit","bitterly","blade","blank","bloom","blossom","bolt","booth"],"title":"The Spring Fair","t":"Every April, when the apple trees are in full **blossom**, our village holds a spring fair. A long **banner** hangs across the main street, and families decorate every **balcony** with flowers. This year, the weather had been **bitterly** cold until the week before, but by the day of the fair, everything was in **bloom**.\n\nThe fair takes place in an old **barn** at the edge of the village. Inside, farmers sell honey from wooden **barrels|barrel**, and children line up at a **booth** where an old woman sells homemade **biscuits|biscuit**. Bargain hunters search the tables for second-hand books, and I found a real **bargain**: a beautiful old family **Bible** for only five pounds. Its first page was **blank** except for a name written in faded ink.\n\nThe most popular stall belonged to Mr. Ellis, the village **barber**. Once a year he gives free haircuts on a **bench** outside the barn, using a **blade** so sharp that he claims he could split a hair in two. He also offers lessons to any **beginner** who wants to learn the trade.\n\nNot everything at the fair is cheerful. A local charity collects money for people in need, and its volunteers told me that no one in our village has had to become a **beggar** since the fund began. \"People here would never **betray** a neighbour,\" one of them said.\n\nAt sunset, the farmers close the barn doors and slide the iron **bolt** across. Children wash their sticky hands in a stone **basin** by the well, and the whole village walks home under the blossoming trees."},"152":{"w":["bore","bosom","bough","bowl","brandy","brass","breast","breath","breeze","brick","brighten","brim","bristle","brittle","bronze","brood","brook","broom","brow","bruise"],"title":"Grandfather's Brook","t":"My grandfather lived in a red **brick** farmhouse beside a small **brook**. When I was a child, I spent every summer there. In the mornings, I would wake to a cool **breeze** coming through the window and the sound of water running over the stones.\n\nGrandfather was a quiet man who liked to **brood** over old memories. When he was thinking, he would rub his **brow** and stare at the hills. But whenever I came into the room, his face would **brighten**, and he would take my small hand and hold it close to his **bosom**, as if I were his greatest treasure.\n\nHe kept many treasures in the house: a **bronze** statue of a horse, an old **brass** bell and a box of letters so **brittle** that they almost broke when I touched them. On cold evenings, he drank a small glass of **brandy** by the fire and told me stories. I was never **bored|bore**, even when I had heard a story three times.\n\nEach morning, I swept the kitchen floor with a straw **broom**, and he gave me a **bowl** of warm milk filled to the **brim**. Then we walked to the brook. Birds sat on every **bough** of the old oak tree, and a robin with a bright red **breast** sang above us. One day, I slipped on a stone and got a large **bruise** on my knee. Grandfather's old dog came running, its fur **bristling|bristle** with worry.\n\nYears later, when I visited the farmhouse after he had died, I stood by the brook and took a deep **breath**. The water still sounded exactly the same."},"153":{"w":["brute","bucket","bud","bulb","bullet","bundle","butcher","butter","cabbage","cabin","cabinet","calculator","camel","canal","candle","canvas","captive","cargo","carpenter","cart"],"title":"Life on the Canal","t":"For three months last year, I lived on a narrow boat on an old **canal** in England. The boat had a tiny **cabin** with a bed, a stove and a wooden **cabinet** for food. At night, I read by the light of a **candle** or a single electric **bulb** powered by a small battery.\n\nTwo hundred years ago, canals were the motorways of Britain. Horses pulled boats loaded with coal, cloth and other **cargo** from city to city. Today, most boats carry tourists. Still, I met an old **carpenter** who repairs the wooden boats by hand. He showed me how to cover the roof with **canvas** to keep out the rain. \"Machines use **brute** force,\" he said, \"but wood needs patience.\"\n\nLife on the canal is simple. Every week, I pushed a **cart** to the nearest village to buy food. The **butcher** there always saved me a piece of good meat, and a farmer sold me **cabbage**, fresh **butter** and a **bundle** of firewood. I carried water from the tap in a **bucket**, and I used a **calculator** to make sure my money lasted until the end of the month.\n\nThe villages along the canal are full of history. In one, an old wall still shows **bullet** holes from a battle in the seventeenth century. In another, a small zoo keeps a **camel**, which the children love; some visitors feel sorry for the animal, which has spent its whole life **captive** far from the desert.\n\nWhen I left in spring, the trees along the water were covered in green **buds|bud**. I promised myself I would return."},"154":{"w":["cassette","castle","casual","cautious","ceiling","cellar","cement","centigrade","centimetre","certificate","chalk","cheat","cheek","cheerful","cheese","chemist","cheque","chew","chimney","chin"],"title":"The House on the Hill","t":"When my aunt bought an old house on a hill near a ruined **castle**, everyone said she was crazy. The roof leaked, the **chimney** was blocked, and water dripped from the **ceiling** whenever it rained. But my aunt, a retired **chemist**, is a **cheerful** woman who loves a challenge.\n\nShe is also **cautious** with money. Before buying the house, she hired an engineer to check every room. He measured each crack to the nearest **centimetre** and found that the walls were strong. Only the **cellar** needed serious work: its floor had to be covered with new **cement**. When the work was finished, he gave her a **certificate** saying the house was safe, and she paid him by **cheque**.\n\nI spent last summer helping her. The temperature rarely rose above twenty degrees **centigrade**, which made the hard work easier. In the cellar, we found a box of old music on **cassette** and a wall covered in **chalk** drawings made by children long ago. My aunt laughed and said one of the drawings showed a boy with a huge **chin** and round red **cheeks|cheek**.\n\nAt lunchtime, we sat in the garden with bread and **cheese**, and she told me stories. As a student, she said, she had once caught a classmate trying to **cheat** in a chemistry exam. \"I told him that honest work is the only kind worth doing,\" she said.\n\nMy aunt's attitude to the project is surprisingly **casual**. \"A house is like a hard piece of bread,\" she says. \"You just have to **chew** it slowly.\""},"155":{"w":["chop","church","circular","circumference","clasp","claw","clay","clerk","cloak","clothe","clumsy","coarse","cock","coil","collision","colony","commander","communism","complicate","comrade"],"title":"A Museum of Small Things","t":"Our town museum is housed in an old **church** near the market. It is not famous, and most visitors stay less than an hour. But I have always loved it, because it tells history through small objects rather than great events.\n\nThe first room tells the story of the town's early years as a trading **colony**. Settlers lived in houses made of **clay** and wood, and they had to **chop** wood, grow food and **clothe** their families with what the land provided. A glass case holds a rough cloak made of **coarse** wool, next to a fine silk **cloak** once worn by a wealthy merchant. The difference between rich and poor could not be clearer.\n\nAnother room covers the twentieth century. One display explains the rise and fall of **communism** in Eastern Europe, using letters written by ordinary people. In one letter, a soldier calls his friend \"my dear **comrade**\" and describes his **commander** as a kind but strict man. Another shows a **circular** poster announcing a public meeting.\n\nThe children's room is my favourite. Visitors can try making pots, though most of them are so **clumsy** that the clay ends up on the floor. There is also a model of a wooden **cock** that crows when you turn a handle, and a stuffed bear with long **claws|claw**. Children learn to measure the **circumference** of an old cart wheel with a **coil** of rope.\n\nThe museum's only **clerk**, Mrs. Doyle, sits at the entrance. She will **clasp** your hand warmly as you leave and tell you about the **collision** between two horse carts outside in 1850. Her stories sometimes **complicate** the official history, but they make it come alive."},"156":{"w":["conceal","confess","congratulate","congratulation","conquest","constitution","contempt","conversion","cordial","corn","correction","correspondent","cottage","cough","coward","crane","cricket","cripple","crow","crude"],"title":"The Correspondent's Cottage","t":"For thirty years, Martin Reed worked as a foreign **correspondent**, reporting on wars, elections and disasters around the world. When he retired, he bought a small stone **cottage** at the edge of a **corn** field and promised himself a quiet life.\n\nThe cottage needed work. Its **conversion** from an old farm building had been done badly, and much of the furniture was **crude**: a rough wooden table, two hard chairs and a bed that creaked every night. Martin did not mind. He had slept in far worse places.\n\nIn his first spring there, he learned to notice small things. A **crow** woke him every morning from the roof, a **crane** stood in the stream below the field, and at night a **cricket** sang outside his window. Neighbours were **cordial**, often bringing eggs or bread and staying for tea.\n\nSlowly, Martin began to write a book about his career. It was not easy. He had to **confess** that he had not always been brave. Once, in a city under attack, he had hidden in a hotel for three days instead of going out to report. \"I felt like a **coward**,\" he wrote, \"and I tried to **conceal** it for years.\" He also wrote about leaders whose dreams of **conquest** had brought ruin, and about people who looked at those leaders with silent **contempt**.\n\nWriting took a toll on his health. He had a strong **constitution**, but a bad winter **cough** kept him in bed for weeks, and an old knee injury threatened to **cripple** him. Still, he finished. His editor sent a long list of **corrections|correction**, then called to **congratulate** him. The book became a bestseller, and the letters of **congratulation** filled a whole box."},"157":{"w":["crust","cube","cucumber","cunning","cupboard","curl","curtain","curve","cushion","dam","daring","darling","deaf","decay","deceit","deck","deer","defect","delegation","democracy"],"title":"The Dam Debate","t":"Last year, a plan to build a **dam** across the river near our town divided the community. Supporters said it would bring cheap electricity and protect homes from floods. Opponents argued that it would flood the forest where **deer** live and destroy a beautiful **curve** of the valley.\n\nThe town held a public meeting in the old theatre. Someone drew back the red **curtain**, and people crowded into the seats, some sitting on a **cushion** they had brought from home. \"This is what **democracy** looks like,\" the mayor said. A **delegation** from the energy company presented their plan, using a wooden **cube** to show the size of the power station.\n\nThe debate was lively. One engineer admitted that the first design had a serious **defect**. An old farmer accused the company of **deceit**, saying it had hidden the true cost. A **daring** young student stood up and asked the company director whether he would like his own house to be flooded. The audience laughed and cheered.\n\nMy grandmother, who is almost **deaf**, sat in the front row with her hand behind her ear. She grew up in a cottage by the river, and she still calls the valley \"my **darling**.\" She worries that the old houses will **decay** if the young people leave. \"The company is **cunning**,\" she told me, \"but we are stubborn.\"\n\nAfter the meeting, we went home and made sandwiches with **cucumber**, cutting off the **crust** for my little brother. Grandmother took a jar of jam from the **cupboard**, then **curled|curl** up in her chair, tired but happy. Next month, the town will vote. Whatever happens, she says, we must stay on **deck** and keep watching."},"158":{"w":["depart","deputy","despise","devil","dew","diagram","diameter","dictation","digest","dim","discharge","dishonour","disk","disturb","dive","donkey","dose","dot","doubtless","dragon"],"title":"The Dragon Boat Race","t":"Every summer, our river town holds a **dragon** boat race, and this year I joined a team for the first time. Practice began at dawn, when **dew** still covered the grass and the light was **dim**. Boats **dotted|dot** the river like small coloured lines.\n\nOur coach was strict, and his **deputy**, a former champion, was even stricter. Before each session, she drew a **diagram** on a board showing how we should sit and move our paddles. She spoke slowly and clearly, as if giving a **dictation**, and we copied every word. \"Speed comes from rhythm, not strength,\" she said. She would **despise** anyone who stopped trying, but she never shouted at beginners.\n\nThe work was hard. Sometimes we practised right after breakfast, before we had time to **digest** our food. An old **donkey** that lived near the river would watch us from the bank, and the drummer joked that it was our only fan. Once, a boy lost his balance and fell in, making an unplanned **dive** into the cold water. We pulled him out laughing.\n\nA week before the race, our best paddler hurt her shoulder. The doctor gave her a small **dose** of medicine and said she could **discharge** herself from the clinic the next day, but she could not race. She was afraid that losing would bring **dishonour** to the team. \"**Doubtless** you will be back next year,\" the coach told her.\n\nOn race day, we tried not to **disturb** the other teams' preparations. When the starting signal sounded, we pulled like the **devil**. As we crossed the line, the sun was a red **disk** low over the water, its **diameter** seeming to grow as it sank. We finished third. Then, at last, it was time to **depart** for home."},"159":{"w":["drawer","dread","drip","duck","dull","durable","dusk","dye","earnest","economical","eighteen","eighty","elbow","electron","elephant","emperor","empire","enclose","enemy","envelope"],"title":"Letters in a Drawer","t":"After my grandfather turned **eighty**, he asked me to help him clear out his desk. In the bottom **drawer**, under old bills, we found a bundle of letters, each in a yellowed **envelope**. He picked one up, and his face changed.\n\n\"I was **eighteen** when I wrote these,\" he said. He had been a soldier far from home, and the letters were to my grandmother. In one, he described the **dread** he felt before his first battle. He wrote that the **enemy** soldiers were boys just like him, sent to fight by leaders who dreamed of building an **empire**. \"No **emperor** is worth a single life,\" he wrote.\n\nNot all the letters were sad. Many were funny, written in **earnest** but full of jokes. He described a camp cook who could make even the most **dull** food taste good, and a night when water began to **drip** through the roof of his tent and he had to **duck** under a table to sleep. He even wrote about seeing an **elephant** carrying supplies through the forest.\n\nGrandfather had always been **economical**. He wrote on both sides of the paper and reused every envelope. He would often **enclose** a small drawing or a pressed flower. The paper was cheap, but it was surprisingly **durable**; seventy years later, the words were still clear.\n\nWe read until **dusk**. Then he rested his **elbow** on the desk and told me about my grandmother: how she used to **dye** her old dresses to make them look new, and how she studied physics at night, filling notebooks with drawings of the **electron**. \"She was smarter than me,\" he said, smiling."},"160":{"w":["erect","eve","exceed","exclaim","excursion","exhibition","expenditure","explosive","exterior","eyesight","fahrenheit","faint","faithful","farewell","fasten","father-in-law","faulty","fence","fetch","fireman"],"title":"The Fireman's Farewell","t":"My **father-in-law**, Tom, worked as a **fireman** for thirty-five years. On the **eve** of his retirement, the fire station held a **farewell** party, and the whole family went along. Tom, a **faithful** servant of the town, was clearly moved.\n\nThe station had prepared a small **exhibition** of photographs from his career. In one, Tom stood in front of a burning factory full of **explosive** chemicals. In another, he was carrying a child over a garden **fence**. The temperature inside that building, a colleague said, had **exceeded|exceed** a thousand degrees **Fahrenheit**. \"How did you survive that?\" my son **exclaimed|exclaim**.\n\nTom's colleagues told stories about him. He always insisted on checking every piece of equipment, because one **faulty** hose could cost lives. He taught young firemen to **fasten** their helmets properly and to never run into a building without checking the **exterior** first. Once, he even had to **fetch** a cat from the top of a tall tree while half the town watched.\n\nThere were sad moments too. Tom spoke about friends who had been injured and about how his **eyesight** had grown weaker after years of smoke. He also complained, with a smile, about the town's low **expenditure** on fire safety. \"We need more trucks, not more speeches,\" he said, and everyone laughed.\n\nAt the end of the evening, the station chief announced that the firemen would take Tom on an **excursion** to the mountains next month. A new flagpole would also be **erected|erect** outside the station in his honour. Tom's voice grew **faint** as he thanked them. Then he put on his old helmet one last time and walked out into the cool night."},"161":{"w":["flag","flare","flour","fluid","flush","footstep","forehead","foreigner","fork","fortnight","freight","friction","frost","frown","funeral","furnace","gallon","garage","gardener","gaseous"],"title":"The Village Gardener","t":"When old Mr. Hughes died last winter, the whole village came to his **funeral**. The **flag** above the town hall flew at half height, and the morning **frost** still covered the churchyard. For forty years, he had been the village **gardener**, and nearly everyone had a story about him.\n\nBefore he took up gardening, Mr. Hughes worked at a steel factory, feeding coal into a huge **furnace**. He also spent a few years loading **freight** trains at the station. \"Hard work, but honest,\" he used to say, wiping his **forehead** with the back of his hand.\n\nHe kept his tools in an old **garage** behind his cottage: spades, a garden **fork** and a watering can that held two **gallons|gallon** of water. Every morning, villagers heard his slow **footsteps|footstep** on the path as he walked to the park. He never took more than a **fortnight**'s holiday a year.\n\nMr. Hughes loved to teach children. He showed them how plants drink water and release it into the air in **gaseous** form, and how a seed can grow in soil as fine as **flour**. When the children asked too many questions, he would **frown** and pretend to be angry, but his eyes always smiled. A young **foreigner** who moved to the village became his best student.\n\nNot everyone agreed with him. There was some **friction** between him and the council over money for the park, and his temper could **flare** when he saw people damaging the flowers. But even his opponents admired him.\n\nAt the funeral, his granddaughter spoke about him, her face **flushed|flush** with emotion. Afterwards, villagers poured a little **fluid** fertiliser on the roses he had planted, as he would have wanted."},"162":{"w":["gasp","gay","gentleman","geology","geometry","ghost","glare","glide","gloomy","glory","glove","goodness","goose","governor","gown","grace","gracious","grammar","grammatical","granddaughter"],"title":"The Governor's House","t":"Last autumn, I took my **granddaughter** to visit the old **governor**'s house on the hill. It was a **gloomy** afternoon, with grey clouds and a cold wind, and she whispered that the place looked as if it had a **ghost**.\n\nOur guide was an elderly **gentleman** in a long black **gown**, which he said was the kind worn by servants two centuries ago. He was **gracious** and patient, answering every question my granddaughter asked. He told us that the governor had been a man of great **goodness** who used his wealth to build schools for poor children.\n\nThe house was full of beautiful things. A bright **glare** of light came through the tall windows and fell on paintings that showed the **glory** of the old colony. In one room, a lady's silk **glove** lay on a table, as if its owner had just left. My granddaughter gave a little **gasp** when she saw it.\n\nThe governor's library interested me most. His books covered subjects from **geology** to **geometry**, and one shelf held old textbooks of English **grammar**. The guide opened one and showed us exercises in **grammatical** analysis that would frighten most students today.\n\nOutside, a **goose** walked across the lawn, and we watched a hawk **glide** over the trees. In the garden, a band played a **gay** tune from the eighteenth century, and my granddaughter danced with real **grace**. On the way home, she said she no longer thought the house had a ghost. \"It just has a lot of stories,\" she said."},"163":{"w":["grandmother","grandson","grape","graph","greedy","grieve","grind","groan","grocer","guilty","gum","gunpowder","gymnasium","haircut","handkerchief","handsome","handwriting","handy","harden","hare"],"title":"Grandmother's Shop","t":"My **grandmother** ran a small food shop for forty years. As a child, I spent every summer helping her, and I learned more from her than from any teacher. She was a **grocer** of the old kind: she knew every customer by name and remembered what they liked.\n\nThe shop sold everything: bread, rice, **grapes|grape**, chewing **gum** and coffee beans that she would **grind** by hand. She kept her accounts in a thick notebook, and her **handwriting** was so neat that it looked printed. Once, she drew a **graph** showing how prices had risen over the years. \"Things used to be cheaper,\" she said, \"but people were not less **greedy**.\"\n\nMy grandmother was kind, but she was also tough. Life had taught her to **harden** her heart against complaints. When I **groaned|groan** about carrying heavy boxes, she laughed and said work was good for a growing boy. When I broke a jar of jam and felt **guilty**, she simply handed me a cloth and said, \"Clean it up, and next time be careful.\"\n\nShe liked to tell stories about history. She told me that **gunpowder** was invented in ancient China, and that our town's old **gymnasium** was built on the site of a fireworks factory. She also told me a fable about a **hare** that lost a race because it was too proud.\n\nWhen she died, I **grieved|grieve** for months. I was her only **grandson**, and now I have a son of my own. Last week, I took him for his first **haircut** and gave him a **handy** little gift: her old cotton **handkerchief**. \"Your great-grandmother would say you look **handsome**,\" I told him."},"164":{"w":["haste","hasten","hasty","hateful","hatred","hawk","hay","headmaster","heating","hedge","helicopter","helmet","helpless","hen","heroic","heroine","hillside","hint","hollow","honeymoon"],"title":"Rescue on the Hillside","t":"Mr. Bennett, the **headmaster** of our village school, had just returned from his **honeymoon** when the storm hit. It rained for three days, and on the third night, part of the **hillside** above the village collapsed. A farmhouse was cut off, and the family inside was trapped.\n\nThe farmer later described how **helpless** he felt. His animals were crying, the **hens|hen** had disappeared, and the **hay** in the barn was soaked. The **heating** had stopped working, and the family sat together in one room, wrapped in blankets. His young daughter tried to stay brave; she would later be called the **heroine** of the story.\n\nMr. Bennett heard about the family and acted with **haste**. Without waiting for help, he put on a **helmet** and climbed up the slope with two other men. Near the top, they found a **hollow** in the rocks where the farmer's daughter had hidden with her baby brother. She had seen their lights and climbed out through a gap in the **hedge** to wave.\n\nThe rescue was **heroic**. Mr. Bennett called for a **helicopter**, which arrived within an hour. A **hawk** circled above as the crew lifted the children to safety. Some people later said his decision to climb had been **hasty**, but nobody could deny that it had **hastened|hasten** the rescue.\n\nAt the school assembly the next week, Mr. Bennett spoke about kindness. \"**Hatred** and fear grow when people stop helping each other,\" he said. \"It is a **hateful** thing to look away.\" He gave no direct **hint** that he had been a hero himself. He simply asked the children to help the family rebuild."},"165":{"w":["horsepower","humorous","hydrogen","ice-cream","idiom","imaginary","immigrate","imprison","indefinite","infamous","injection","ink","innocent","insignificant","inspect","intermediate","intern","interpreter","inverse","inward"],"title":"My Internship at the Car Show","t":"Last summer, I worked as an **intern** at an international car show, helping a professional **interpreter**. My English was only at an **intermediate** level, so I was nervous on the first day. But my supervisor, a **humorous** woman from Shanghai, told me that mistakes were part of learning.\n\nThe show was full of new technology. One company presented a car powered by **hydrogen**, with an engine of three hundred **horsepower**. An engineer explained that there is an **inverse** relationship between weight and efficiency: the lighter the car, the less energy it needs. I translated his words slowly, trying not to confuse the technical terms.\n\nMy supervisor taught me to listen to the meaning, not just the words. When a speaker used an English **idiom** like \"it's raining cats and dogs,\" she said, I should not translate it word for word. She also told me about her family, who had **immigrated|immigrate** to Canada when she was a child. \"Living between languages makes you notice small things,\" she said. Every evening, she marked my notes in red **ink**, and over **ice-cream** after work she explained each correction.\n\nNot every day was easy. Once, an inspector arrived to **inspect** the safety of a display. Another day, a visitor fainted and needed an **injection** from the medical team. A third day, a reporter asked about an **infamous** businessman who had been **imprisoned|imprison** for fraud, and I had to translate very carefully.\n\nAt the end of the internship, I realised that my fears had been **imaginary**. No question was **insignificant**, and no mistake was unforgivable. In her farewell note, my supervisor praised my **innocent** enthusiasm and said the door was open for an **indefinite** period: I could come back any time. I smiled on the outside, but the deeper joy was **inward**, and it has stayed with me ever since."}});
